0,783,Alpes (mountains),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Alps The Alps are one of the great mountain ranges of Europe stretching some 1,200 km. (750 miles)."
1,815,Ap(p)enninus M.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: ITL The Apennine Mountains extend ca. 1,200 km (750 miles) along the length of peninsular Italy."
2,1004,(H)Adriaticum/Superum Mare,A body of water separating the Apennine Peninsula of Italy from the Balkan Peninsula.
3,1052,Italia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: ITL The Italian peninsula extending northward to the Alps as recognized by the Romans.
4,1113,Magna Graecia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: ITL Magna Graecia refers to the coastal areas of Southern Italy on the Tarentine Gulf that were extensively colonized by Greek colonists beginning in the eighth century BC.
5,1224,Pontus Euxinus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Black Sea The Black Sea.
6,1356,Tyrrhenum/Inferum Mare,"The Tyrrhenian Sea is part of the Mediterranean Sea off the western coast of Italy. It takes its name from the Tyrrhenian people, another name for the Etruscans."
7,1361,Ustica (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ustica ITL An island in the Tyrrhenian Sea occupied since the middle of the second millennium B.C. when Phoenician traders were present there.
8,20401,Auteinoi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: IRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 B3 Auteinoi"
9,20442,‘Dabrona’ (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lee IRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 B3 ‘Dabrona’ fl."
10,20456,Erdinoi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: IRE/UKG An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 B3 Erdinoi"
11,20466,Ganganoi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: IRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 B3 Ganganoi"
12,20469,Germanicum Mare,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: North Sea The North Sea.
13,20487,Hibernia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ireland IRE/UKG Hibernia is the Classical name for the island of Ireland.
14,20539,‘Magnatai’,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: IRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 B3 ‘Magnatai’"
15,20568,Ouenniknioi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: IRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 B3 Ouenniknioi"
16,20600,Senos (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Shannon estuary IRE The River Shannon (Ireland).
17,20624,Thule (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Shetland UKG An island mentioned frequently -- and often metaphorically -- in texts of the Roman period, said by Ptolemy, Strabo, Pliny, and others to lie to the north of Britannia. Some scholars associate it with the island known today as Shetland, but the geographic association even in antiquity (when it was more than just a rhetorical term) varied (see further Todd in New Pauly)."
18,20639,Vistula (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Wisła/San POL Vistula fl. (Vistula River) flows 1,047 km (651 miles) from the south of Poland to the Baltic Sea. Its name is first attested in the Natural History of Pliny the Elder."
19,21897,Ausoba (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: W coast Ireland An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 unlocated Ausoba"
20,21899,Boreion Akron,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: NW Ireland Boreion Akron, a cape in northwest Ireland described by Ptolemy."
21,21901,Dour (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: SW Ireland An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 unlocated Dour fl."
22,21904,Iouernis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Iouernioi? IRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 unlocated Iouernis"
23,21906,Libnios (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: W Ireland A river of western Ireland mentioned by Ptolemy, now unlocated."
24,21909,Magnata,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Nagnatai IRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 unlocated Magnata"
25,21910,Makolikon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: W Ireland An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 unlocated Makolikon"
26,21912,Notion Akron,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: SW Ireland An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 unlocated Notion Akron"
27,21914,Ouellaboroi/Velabri,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Shannon IRE An ancient tribe that was located in the south-west of Ireland.
28,21916,Ousdiai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S Ireland An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 unlocated Ousdiai"
29,21918,Raiba,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Lough Ree? IRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 unlocated Raiba"
30,21920,Regia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: central Ireland An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 2 unlocated Regia"
31,29517,Carmania,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: IRN A province of both the Achaemenid Empire and the Sassanid Empire.
32,29547,Drangiane/Zarangiane (region),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Helmand basin AFG/IRN A region located around Lake Hāmūn and the Helmand river in modern Sīstān.
33,29573,Gedrosia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Baluchistan-Makran IRN/PAK Gedrosia was a mountainous region covering parts of what is today southeastern Iran and southwestern Pakistan.
34,29691,Oarakta (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Qeshm Is.? IRN An island located in the Strait of Hormuz that is separated from the mainland by the Clarence Strait in the Persian Gulf. It is the largest island in the Persian Gulf.
35,29713,Persicus Sinus/Erythr(ae)um Mare,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Persian Gulf The Persian Gulf is located in Western Asia between Persia and the Arabian Peninsula.
36,29718,Phra(da)/Prophthasia/Frakh-kar-Peroz,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: perhaps near Farah AFG An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 3 H3 Phra(da)/Prophthasia/Frakh-kar-Peroz"
37,29746,Sarnios (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tejend TKM The Sarnios corresponds to the lower course of the modern Hari/Tejen river in Turkmenistan, from around its junction with the Kashafrud river to the Tejand Oasis beyond which it disperses into the Karakum Desert."
38,29816,Zagrus (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zagros range The Zagros Mountains.
39,30224,Chelonophagoi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: tribe Pliny the Elder discussed the Chelonophagoi, an ancient tribe that subsisted on turtle flesh and covered their houses with the turtles' shells, in the context of his excursus on the Persian Gulf."
40,30242,Kophanta/Kophas,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: port on Gedrosian or Carmanian coast An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 3 unlocated Kophanta/Kophas"
41,30249,Ogyris (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in Persian Gulf An island in the Persian Gulf. Its modern location cannot be determined today.
42,30251,Organa/Turgana (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: island An island located near the Strait of Hormuz. Some sources suggest that Organa be equated with Lārak island, while others suggest Hormuz island."
43,30256,Parsika (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on border of Carmania and Gedrosia A mountain on border of Carmania and Gedrosia.
44,30267,Saganos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river An ancient coastal river of Carmania, reported by Ptolemy. BAtlas declined to place it on a map."
45,39279,Akila/Okelis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Khor Ghurayrah (Shaykh Sa'id)? YEM A coastal station or anchorage and associated settlement that scholars identify with the modern Khawr/Khor Ghurayrah on Yemen's Ra’s Shaykh Sa‘īd headland in the Bab al-Mandab Strait. This small bay is almost closed by sand and coral reefs today. The precise location of the ancient settlement is not known.
46,39281,Alalaiou (islands),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dahalak, opposite Massawa ERT Alalaiou Inss., the modern Dahlak Archipelago in the Red Sea, which today comprises 126 islands."
47,39286,Anagrana/Negram,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nagran/Najran SAU A large archaeological site in modern Saudi Arabia's Najrān oasis: al-Ukhdūd.
48,39290,Arabicus Sinus/Erythr(ae)um/Rubrum Mare,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: large section of the Indian Ocean, including the Persian Gulf and Red Sea Arabicus Sinus/Erythr(ae)um/Rubrum Mare (The Red Sea) is an inlet of the Indian Ocean located between Africa and Asia."
49,39291,Ararene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: al-'Ara'ir, near Jizan SAU The modern al 'Ara'ir, near Jizan in Saudi Arabia."
50,39300,Athroula/Laththa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Baraqish YEM An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 B2 Athroula/Laththa"
51,39303,A(u)xoume,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aksum? ETH Present-day Aksum in Ethiopia. The center of the Aksumite Kingdom, a maritime trading power that flourished from the first to the tenth centuries AD. The city was listed as UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1980."
52,39304,Aualites,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Assab DJI An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 B3 Aualites"
53,39315,Bracheia/Prasodes Thalassa,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: E Pemba and Zanzibar Bracheia/Prasodes Thalassa was a part of the Indian Ocean described by Ptolemy lying off the coast of East Africa.
54,39326,Casani/Gasani/Kassanitai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jizan SAU An ancient people of the southwest coast of Arabia, probably localized in the area immediately south of modern Jazan, Saudi Arabia."
55,39334,Debai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: N Wadi Bayd SAU Strabo describes the Debai of southwestern Arabia as a semi-Nomadic people. He notes that they live in a land that is rich in resources (like gold) for which these people have no use. This description is part of his narrative of ""primitive"" peoples at the margins of the Roman world."
56,39350,Homeritai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: inhabitants of Himyar YEM A tribe of southern Arabia Felix, occupying territory that is now part of Yemen."
57,39352,Indicum Mare,The Indian Ocean.
58,39360,Kinaidokolpitai,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yanbu' to W Baysh SAU The Kinaidokolpitai were an ancient people inhabiting the western coastal strip of the Arabian Peninsula. The Barrington Atlas Directory notes that their territory was located from Yanbu' to the west of Baysh in Saudi Arabia.
59,39361,Kinnamomophoros Chora,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: NE Somali coast SOM Strabo dubs the area of the northern Somali coast westward from Cape Guardafui as the ""Land of Cinnamon""."
60,39376,Malichou islands,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zubayr YEM A group of 10 major volcanic islands located in the Red Sea.
61,39379,Mari(a)ba,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marib YEM Capital of the Sabaean kingdom.
62,39386,Minaei,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: inhabitants of Ma’in YEM The Minaei were an ancient people of Yemen, numbered by Strabo as one of the four great nations of the southern Arabian peninsula."
63,39403,Opone,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Hafun main or west side site (S side of peninsula) SOM Opone, mentioned in the first-century A.D. Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, was a trading center located in the Horn of Africa, likely in the area of present day Hafun, Somalia."
64,39409,Ptolemais Theron,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on the coast between 18-19 degrees N lat. SUD. Casson pages 100-101 helpfully summarizes the various modern locations posited by earlier scholars. Ptolemais Theron was an ancient marketplace on the western coast of the Red Sea. Its precise location on the Sudanese Red Sea coast is now unknown, but many scholars view modern Aqiq (Hillat `Agig) as the most likely alternative."
65,39414,Rhapta,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dar es-Salaam TAN A city and trading port on the east coast of Africa, mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea and other sources. Probably to be located in the area south of modern Dar es-Salaam, in or around the Rufiji Delta and Mafia archipelago (Tanzania). Rhapta's location has not been archaeologically verified, but monumental architecture identified in 2016 off the north coast of Mafia island is a strong candidate."
66,39428,Syagros? Pr.,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ras Fartak YEM A promontory of the southern coast of Arabia.
67,39430,Tamna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Hajar Kuhlan YEM An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 C2 Tamna"
68,40045,Antiphilou Limen,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 unlocated Antiphilou Limen"
69,40100,Charmuthas,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 unlocated Charmuthas"
70,40134,Endera,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 unlocated Endera"
71,40141,Eumenous Alsos/Eumenous Limen,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 unlocated Eumenous Alsos/Eumenous Limen"
72,40184,Kreophagoi,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 unlocated Kreophagoi"
73,40193,Licha Thera,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 unlocated Licha Thera"
74,40222,Menouthias,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monfiya? Pemba? Zanzibar? TAN An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 unlocated Menouthias"
75,40228,Moscha Limen,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 4 unlocated Moscha Limen"
76,40256,Prason,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ras Dima opposite the island Mafia (Arabic: Manfiya) or Cape Delgado TAN A headland located south of Meroë in a region referred to as Barbarica, possibly to be identified with the area opposite the island Mafia (Arabic: Manfiya) or Cape Delgado."
77,40274,Sabastrikon Stoma/Sebastikon Stoma,One of many toponyms provided by Ptolemy for ancient Ethiopia and southern Arabia that has not been located by modern scholars.
78,40303,Sebritae/Sembitae,"An ancient people, the Sebritae/Sembitae live on an island in the river Nile and are governed by a queen, according to Pliny the Elder."
79,40352,Zingis (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S Ras Hafun? SOM Zingis Pr. was a promontory of east Africa. Its precise location is uncertain, but may have been south of Ras Hafun in modern Somalia."
80,49863,Anurādhapura,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Anurādhapura SRI The ancient capital of Sri Lanka from the fourth century BCE to the 11th century CE. It was recorded under the name Anourogrammon by Ptolemy, who notes its primary political status (Basileion). It has sometimes been argued that a ""Palaesimundum"" mentioned by Pliny in retelling the story of a Sri Lankan embassy to the emperor Claudius is also to be identified with Anourogrammon. A large number of numismatic finds from many periods have been reported in the vicinity."
81,49873,Argyra Chora,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arakan coast? BRM An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 5 G2 Argyra Chora"
82,49890,Bakare,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pirakkād Malayalam: Vaikkarai An ancient port settlement on the southwest coast of India, mentioned by the Periplus, Pliny, and Ptolemy. Casson (whence BAtlas) places it at modern Pirakkād/Purakkad in the state of Kerala."
83,49895,Banabasi,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Banavasi A settlement in south India that has served as a regional administrative and sacred center since at least the beginning of the first millennium CE.
84,49931,Cabirus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kāverī An ancient river of peninsular India, known to Pliny as Cabirus and to Ptolemy as Chabēros. Scholars identify it with the modern Kāverī."
85,49939,Kaveripumpattinam/Poombuhar,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tranquebar Skt.: Kaveripattinam This ancient settlement and seaport was located on the southeastern coast of peninsular India in the vicinity of the modern village of Poompuhar/Poombuhar. Excavations (including a wood-then-brick-built wharf carbon-dated to ca. 250 BCE), as well as epigraphic and literary evidence, demonstrate a thriving seaborne trade and Buddhist religious center through to the 7th century CE. The place was known to the Romans as Chaberis (Ptolemy) and Kamara (PME)."
86,49954,Cydara (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arevi-ār SRI An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 5 inset Cydara fl."
87,49956,Dachinabades,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Deccan; in PME denotes domain of Śātavāhanas Skt.: Dakṣiṇā-patha An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 5 D2 Dachinabades"
88,50064,Korkai/Kolchoi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Korkei An ancient settlement of southeastern India associated with trade and pearl fishery in both Sangam and Roman literature (Ptolemy calls it an ""emporion""). Although the area -- where chance finds, and excavation have surfaced material ranging in date from the first millennium BCE to at least the second century CE -- now lies some 6km inland from the ocean and 2km north of the current course of the Thamirabharani river, modern scholars uniformly consider it to have been an ancient seaport equipped with a harbor of some kind. Literary sources attest to its importance during the Pandya dynasty."
89,50081,Kottapatnam,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kottapattinam? A modern village near the coast north from Chennai where an archaeologically attested seaport settlement flourished from the 3rd century BCE until the 15th century CE as attested by remnants of Chinese and Thai pottery -- as well as a Chinese coin. It is probably to be identified with Ptolemy's ""Kottis""."
90,50104,Krishna/Maisolos (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kṛṣṇa The modern Krishna river in India was known to Ptolemy as Maisolos.
91,50107,Malanga,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kanchipuram. Dayalan 2019 asserts, without argument or mention of Kanchipuram, a tentative alternative identification with Mamallapuram. Nannam 2019 uncritically resurrects an early 20th century identification with Krishnapatna/Gandagopalapattana port. Ptolemy places this ancient city in India. BAtlas identifies it firmly with modern Kanchipuram, which is located inland and southwest from modern Chennai."
92,50139,Modoutou Emporion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mullaitivu? SRI Ptolemy places the ""Modoutoi"" in the northern part of ancient Sri Lanka, and provides coordinates for a ""Modoutou emporion"". Tentatively following Berthelot, BAtlas places the emporion in the vicinity of modern Mullaitivu."
93,50150,Nagadiba,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Trincomalee? SRI Ptolemy records a city and island called ""Nagadiba"" and also provides the ethnic name ""Nagadiboi"". One strand of twentieth century western scholarship, reflected in BAtlas, tentatively placed this site on the northeastern coast of Sri Lanka in the vicinity of modern Trincomalee (see also Emmanuel 2000). Another scholarly tradition, most recently repeated in Ashire 2012, identifies the Ptolemaic Nagadiba with the island and settlement hosting what is now known as the Nagadeepa/Nainadhivu Buddhist shrine off the northern peninsula of Jaffna."
94,50168,Nagappattinam,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nagapattinam An ancient seaport, locus of Indian Ocean trade, Buddhist center, and major site for bronze production attested archaeologically and in epigraphic and literary sources from the time of Ptolemy to the present."
95,50186,Oxeia (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S cape of Koddiyar Bay SRI Ptolemy provides this name for a promontory of Sri Lanka, which BAtlDir identifies with the southern cape of Koddiyar Bay (i.e., the promontory at the tip of which the so-called Foul Point lighthouse now stands)."
96,50212,Perimula (promontory),"Twice in the Natural History (books 6 and 9), Pliny the Elder describes Perimula (presumably cribbing Megasthenes) as a ""promontory of India"". Erdosy, in BAtlas, follows McCrindle 1926, who identifies it as the ""projecting point of the island of Perimula or Perimuda, now called the island of Salsette"" (i.e., the island now occupied by the modern city of Mumbai). Ptolemy and Aelian also record a ""Perimoula"", which various commentators have tried to place -- identified also with the feature in Pliny -- in the Gulf of Thailand, on the Malay peninsula, or on Sumatra."
97,50219,Dharanikota/Pitoura,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dharanikota Dharanikota is a modern village adjoining Amaravathi on the southern bank of the Krishna river in Andhra Pradesh. Ancient habitation, attested by pottery, and a series of wharfs and water management structures in various materials including brick and stone, indicate that trade and settlement activity began at the site as early as the second century BCE. The site has been identified with Ptolemy's Pitoura, which he identifies as a ""metropolis""."
98,50221,Podouke/Arikamedu,"The site is mentioned in Ptolemy's treatise and was, in the twentieth century, excavated by Sir Mortimer Wheeler. The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arikamedu, Virampatnam. Podouke/Arikamedu is an archaeological site located in Kakkayanthope, Ariyankuppam Commune, Puducherry, India."
99,50278,Sopatma,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marakanam One of three ""emporia"" listed in the PME and placed by it after (i.e., northward from) Kamara and Poduke. BAtlas identified it firmly with modern Marakanam, but Dayalan more recently asserts it might be tentatively identified with Mamallapuram."
100,50291,Syrastrene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Saurāṣṭra, peninsular region of Gujarat Skt.: Saurāṣṭra An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 5 B2 Syrastrene"
101,50298,Sri Lanka,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sri Lanka Pāli: Tambapaṇṇi Skt.: pāra-samudra? The island of Sri Lanka lies at the center of the Indian Ocean world, connecting and connected by trade and communication with and between Africa, Arabia, southwest Asia, peninsular south Asia, Southeast Asia, Oceania, and beyond."
102,50644,Galiba (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: N Sri Lanka Galiba M. refers to mountains in the vicinity of a promontory, called Galiba Akra by Ptolemy, located on the northern coast of the ancient Taprobane (modern Sri Lanka)."
103,50737,Selour,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: settlement in Tamil Nadu An ancient ""emporion"" listed by Ptolemy among places in the chora of Pandion, around the Argarikos Kolpos (the modern Palk Bay), so therefore in modern Tamil Nadu. BAtlDir did not place it on the map; whereas Dayalan 2019 ventures a possible identification with Alagankulam (q.v.)."
104,59648,Abastanoi/Sambastai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: IND/PAK Skt.: Ambaṣṭha/Mekala An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C4 Abastanoi/Sambastai"
105,59661,Agoranis (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Garai BAN Arrian (quoting Megasthenes) lists the Agoranis among a large number of tributaries of the Ganges river. Some modern scholarship (e.g., Tomaschek in RE) has therefore identified it with the modern Gogra (Ghaghara); however, Erdosy in BAtlas silently rejects this view, preferring to identify it as a distributary of the Ganges, the modern Garai (Gorai) in Bangladesh."
106,59675,Alexandria/Rhambakia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Khandewari? PAK, but see Cohen 2013 on the relative merits of this and other tentative identifications. While on the march in 325 BC, Alexander arrived in Rhambakia, the chief village of the Oreitai. He ordered Hesphaistion to remain there and see to establishing a city. The location of this city cannot be determined today."
107,59688,Aparytai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Afridi AFG/PAK Skt.: Aparīta An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 B3 Aparytai"
108,59691,Arabitai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: PAK An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 B4 Arabitai"
109,59713,Aspasioi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: lower Kunar valley AFG An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C3 Aspasioi"
110,59731,Bandobene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Skt.: U(n)dabhanda An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C3 Bandobene"
111,59737,Barygaza,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Broach Skt.: Bhṛgukaccha Barygaza was an ancient port located on the Gulf of Cambay.
112,59750,Boukephala,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jalilpur? PAK Boukephala or Bucephala was an ancient city founded on the right bank of the Hydaspes river by Alexander the Great following the battle with Porus. Alexander named the city after his warhorse, Bucephalus. Its precise location remains debated."
113,59766,Chaarene/Arana,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kharan region, in Baluchistan An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 A4 Chaarene/Arana"
114,59773,Choaspes (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: combined streams of Swāt (Souastos) and Panjkora (Gouraios) Skt.: Gaurī The Choaspes flows from the Hindu Kush mountains to the Indus river in what is now Afghanistan.
115,59787,Daradrai/Dadikai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dards in Hindukush range, Nuristan AFG An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C2 Daradrai/Dadikai"
116,59788,Dargamanes (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ab-e-Safed AFG Ancient Greek and Roman accounts of this and other Bactrian rivers are confused. The Barrington Atlas tentatively identifies this ancient river with the modern Ab-e-Safed (Daryā-ye Safēd) in Afghanistan's Jowzjan province.
117,59808,Erannoboas/Sonus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Son Skt.: Hiraṇyavāhu/Sona A river which flowed into the Ganges river at Patna, mentioned by both Arrian and Pliny."
118,59811,Euas(...) (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kunar AFG/PAK An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C2 Euas(...) fl."
119,59826,Gaza,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nau? TAJ Gaza was a city or strongly fortified place in Sogdiana, captured by Alexander the Great."
120,59828,Glauganikai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: IND/PAK An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C3 Glauganikai"
121,59834,Hemodos/Imaus/Caucasus (mountains),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Himalayas Pāli: Hemoda Skt.: Hīmavat Although ancient, western commentators varied in their precision and ideas about location, they seem all to have meant by these names a single range or mountainous region corresponding to the modern notion of the Himalaya or at least the eastern portion of what we today consider the Hindu Kush, Pamir, and Himalaya ranges."
122,59837,Hydaspes/Bidaspes (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jhelum IND/PAK Skt.: Vitastā An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C3 Hydaspes/Bidaspes fl."
123,59838,Hydraotes/Arouadis/Porrus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ravi IND/PAK Skt.: Irāvatī VedicSkt.: Paruṣṇī A river of the Punjab that flows southwest from the western Himalaya and joins the river Acesines.
124,59907,Malana (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ras Malan PAK An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 B4 Malana Pr."
125,59915,Marakanda,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Samarkand UZB Marakanda (now Samarkand in Uzbekistan) was a major ancient settlement founded ca. 700 BC by the Sogdians. Alexander the Great conquered Marakanda during his eastern campaigns.
126,59922,Massaka,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: fort in Katgala Pass Skt.: Maśaka An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C3 Massaka"
127,59938,Mousikanoi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: N Sindh PAK Skt.: Muśika An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 B4 Mousikanoi"
128,59942,Nagara/Dionysopolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nagarahara AFG Ptolemy records the city of Nagara, also known as Dionysopolis. Cohen posits that Nagar is likely located near Jalalabad, perhaps at modern Nagarahara."
129,59948,Nysaioi?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: lower Kunar Valley AFG An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C3 Nysaioi?"
130,59956,Oreitae/Nearti,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Makran coast, between Ras Malan and R. Arabis PAK An ancient people dwelling on the coast of Gedrosia, and encountered by Alexander the Great during his march from the Indus River to Persia."
131,59969,Oxus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Amu Darya Avst.: Vaxšu Oxus fl. (modern Amu Darya river) is a major river of central Asia. It flows from the Pamir Mountains to the Aral Sea.
132,59971,Oxymatis (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ichamati, channel in Ganges delta The Ichamati River."
133,59977,Paktyike?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pakhtuns AFG/PAK Skt.: Paktha An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 6 C3 Paktyike?"
