241,147985,Agatha,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Agde Agatha (modern Agde), also known as Agatha Tyche, was a colonial foundation of ca. 525 B.C. The colony was founded by Phocaean Greeks from Massilia."
242,148056,Col. Baeterrae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: settlement and bridge at Béziers. Prior to the Roman conquest, the local toponym seems to have been Besara and the site was located in the territory of the Volcae Tectosages. The Romans annexed the site along with the rest of Transalpine Gaul. A colony for veterans of Levio VII was established in A.D. 36. Colonia Julia Baeterrae Septimanorum is located where the Via Herculia crosses the Orb river. It is now modern Béziers, France. The site has been inhabited at least since 750-650 B.C."
243,148069,Druentia (river),"A navigable river prone to devastating floods. Called the Durance (French), a tributary of the Rhone."
244,148074,Ernaginum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: St-Gabriel Ernaginum was a road station in the territory of the Salyes in Gallia Narbonensis, according to Ptolemy."
245,148127,Massalia/Massilia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marseille Massalia/Massilia (modern Marseille, France) was founded by Phocaean Greeks ca. 600 B.C. as a trading outpost. Massalia, in turn, established its own secondary colonies. During the Punic Wars Massalia, was allied to Rome and, following the sacking of Carthage in 146 B.C., the settlement enjoyed increased economic vitality."
246,148142,Nemausus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nîmes Nemausus (modern Nîmes, France) was the ancient capital of the Volcae Arecomici and later a Roman colony."
247,148168,Rhodanus (river),"One of the major navigable rivers of Europe, the Rhodanus drains the Western Alps and flows to the Mediterranean Sea. The river now known as the Rhône is the second longest river in Gaul."
248,148242,Volcae Arecomici,Lewis (1879) states that the Volcae Arecomici are part of a large ethnic group that includes the Volcae Tectosages . A Gallic people of the Languedoc. Their chief town was Nemausus.
249,151759,Albaugousta,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: city of Elykokoi? (erroneous name?) An ancient settlement, attested by literary or documentary sources, whose precise location cannot be determined today"
250,157802,Alb(i)um Intimilium/Albintimilium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ventimiglia ITL Alb(i)um Intimilium/Albintimilium (modern Ventimiglia) was the main settlement of the Ligurian tribe of the Intemelii. In the 2nd century BC, it came under the control of the Roman Republic. Albintimilium became a municipium in 89 BC in the tribus Falerna. In the same period, the city walls were built, with several towers. The city flourished in the early Imperial period, with baths, an aqueduct, and a theater. In the 6th and early 7th century, it was a Byzantine kastron, coming under the control of the Longobards in 641. During the 7th and 8th centuries, the Roman city was slowly abandoned in favour of a smaller hilltop settlement focused around the Cathedral."
251,157803,Album Ingaunum/Albingaunum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Albenga ITL The fourth century B.C. capital of the Ingauni Ligures sided with Carthage during the Punic War but was reduced by Lucius Aemilius Paullus Macedonicus in 181 B.C. A treaty was struck with Rome; the city received Roman citizenship in 45 B.C.
