286,197359,Lougeon Helos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cerkniško jezero SVN Lake Cerknica is a an intermittent lake in southwestern Slovenia.
287,197371,Marsonia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Slavonski Brod CRO An ancient settlement at modern Slavonski Brod in Croatia.
288,197401,Nauportus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vrhnika SVN An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 20 B4 Nauportus"
289,197416,Ocra (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mt. Nanos SVN Ocra M. (Mt. Nanos) is a karst limestone plateau located at the eastern border of the Slovenian Littoral.
290,197448,Pola,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pula CRO A port city on the Adriatic Sea (modern Pula in Croatia).
291,197471,Rhizon/Risinium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Risan YUG A city on the Adriatic coast of Dalmatia with a protected harbor.
292,197472,Rhizonicus Sinus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gulf of Kotor The Bay of Kotor.
293,197504,Segestica/Siscia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sisak CRO Segestica/Siscia is an Iron Age settlement recorded by Strabo. It was located at the confluence of three rivers and was used as a staging area for a campaign against the Dacians.
294,197525,Splonum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gor. Vrtače near Drvar BOS An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 20 D5 Splonum"
295,197580,Vetus Salina,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Adony HUN Vetus Salina was the site of a Roman castrum on the frontier of Pannonia Inferior.
296,206874,Acumincum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Stari Slankamen YUG Acumincum (modern Stari Slankamen in Serbia) was a settlement of the Scordisci that was later conquered by the Romans. During the Flavian period, the Cohors I Britannica equitata and II Adjutrix were based there."
297,207268,Margus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Morava YUG Now the Great Morava, the ancient Margus is part of a major river system in Serbia."
298,207269,Maris(os) (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marosch / Mureş HUN / ROM An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 21 C3 Maris(os) fl."
299,211429,Dokidava,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in Dacia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 21 unlocated Dokidava"
300,211445,Ouakontion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in Pannonia N of Drava An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 21 unlocated Ouakontion"
301,216688,Abrittus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Razgrad BUL Abrittus was the site of a Roman fortress and settlement, located near modern Razgrad, Bulgaria."
302,216717,Athrys/Iatros/Ieterus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 22 C5 Athrys/Iatros/Ieterus fl."
303,216728,Bastarnae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: MOL/UKR An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 22 F3 Bastarnae"
304,216744,Callatis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mangalia A Greek colony and port on the west coast of the Black Sea, modern Mangalia in Romania."
305,216788,Deultum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Debelt BUL An ancient settlement, modern Debelt in Bulgaria."
306,216791,Dinogetia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Garvăn Dinogetia, a Roman castrum located in the province of Moesia."
307,216817,Getae,The Greek name given to Thracian tribes dwelling south of the Danube river.
308,216830,Haemus (mountain range),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Stara Planina BUL A mountain range of northern Thrace: the modern ""Balkan Mountains""."
309,216837,Hierasos/Gerasus/Tiarantos? (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river A river of Dacia and tributary of the Danube. Identified with the modern Siret.
310,216859,Kabyle,"A.G. Poulter (1983 and BAtlas) associates the name ""Diospolis"", transmitted in the 6th-century Synecdemus of Hierocles, with the same site as Kabyle. Still, some other scholars are not convinced (e.g., von Bredow in New Pauly ). The site of this ancient settlement, which provides evidence for habitation from the Bronze Age into late antiquity, lies just to the north of the modern settlement that has inherited its name (Kabile) in the Bulgarian province of Yambol."
311,216864,Koralloi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: BUL An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 22 E6 Koralloi"
312,216882,Mesembria,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nesebur BUL Originally a Thracian site known as Menebria, Mesembria was subsequently settled by Dorian Greeks in the early sixth century BC, eventually becoming an important trading colony on the Black Sea coast (modern Nesebar, Bulgaria). Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1983."
313,216891,Naparis? (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river The Naparis was a river of Scythia and a left-bank tributary of the Danube. It can no longer be identified.
314,216893,Naulochos/Templum Iovis?,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Obzor BUL. A seaside settlement of ancient Thrace that was a colony of Mesembria. Some Romans seem to have referred to the site also as Templum Iovis.
315,216904,Odessus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Varna BUL The ancient Black Sea settlement of Odessus is now Varna, Bulgaria."
316,216947,Pyretos (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Prut The Prut river flows from the Carpathian Mountains to the Danube.
317,222078,Karpoudaimon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Thrace Now unlocated, Ptolemy indicates that Karpoudaimon was to be found between Orcelis and Bizye."
318,222103,Larisa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: village in Naulochos area An ancient settlement, described by Strabo as lying ""on the left of the Pontus, between Naulochus and Odessos, near Mount Haimos"". Its modern location is uncertain, although it may correspond to a fortified settlement on the St. Athanasius Cape near modern Byala in Bulgaria."
319,222124,Noes,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 22 unlocated Noes fl."
320,226506,Achilleios Dromos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tendrovskaya Kosa A strip of land northwest of Crimea and south of the mouth of the Dnieper river in Ukraine, running nearly due west and east."
321,226511,Agathyrsoi,"The Agathyrsoi were an ancient tribe whose territory was located on the upper Mureş river, north-east of that of the Neuri."
322,226544,Borysthenes (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dnieper The Dnieper River.
323,226546,Borysthenes/Olbia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Parutino Commonly referred to as ""Olbia Pontica"" to distinguish it from multiple other ancient sites with the same name, the remains of this multi-period settlement are located immediately south of the modern Ukrainian village of Parutyne on the west bank of the Dnieper–Bug Estuary."
324,226562,Charax,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ai-Todor Charax was a Scythian, and later, Roman, fortress founded on the Ai-Todor promontory near ancient Chersonesus in the third century BC."
325,226564,Chersonesos,"The site was inscribed as a property on the UNESCO World Heritage list in 2013 as the ""Ancient City of Tauric Chersonese and its Chora"". Chersonesos was an ancient Greek colony founded in the southwestern part of the Crimean Peninsula, ancient Taurica. Settlers from Heraclea Pontica established the site in the sixth century B.C."
326,226574,Crobyzi,An ancient tribe of Moesia.
327,226595,Eremia Geton,"Strabo describes the Eremia Geton (""the wasteland/desolation of the Getai"") as lying between the territory of the Getai and the Pontic Sea, between the river Ister (Danube) and the river Tyras (Dniester), a region where little ancient settlement has been found. This area, subject to the rising waters of the Black Sea, may also have been referred to by the name or descriptive term ""Sorsdesertus"" on the Peutinger Map."
328,226623,Hippolaou Akra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Stanislavskoye Pr. An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 23 F2 Hippolaou Akra"
329,226630,Hypanis/Southern Bug (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bug The modern Southern Bug river in Ukraine was known in Greek and Roman sources as the Hypanis, one of three distinct rivers with this ancient name."
330,226658,Kerkinitis?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Eupatoria An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 23 G3 Kerkinitis?"
331,226671,Kriou Metopon Pr.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cape Sarych Kriou Metopon Pr. (""The Ram's Forehead) is the modern Cape Sarych, the southernmost point of Ukraine."
332,226677,Leuke Ins./Achilleos Nesos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zmeinyi. For Pliny the Elder, the Racecourse of Achilles (Dromos Achilleos) is located some 125 miles from the island, near to the coast of Crimea and close to the mouth of the Dnieper in this place . An island located in the Black Sea to the east of the mouth of the Danube River, approximately 35 km from the coast. In antiquity, the island was thought to be sacred to the Greek hero Achilles. The ancient name ""White Island"" was derived from the presence of white serpents, according to Dionysius Periegetes."
333,226694,Narakon Stoma,"Narakon Stoma, one of the mouths of the Danube River."
334,226697,Nikonion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Roksolanskoye Nikonion received a sixth-century B.C. colony from either Miletos or Istros. It was assessed tribute by the Delian League in 425/4 B.C. Authors such as Pseudo-Skylax refer to the site as a polis in the urban sense. The colony was under the protection of the Skythian king Skyles. The ancient city was located on the on east bank of the Dniester river where Roksolany, Ukraine, is now located."
335,226708,Palaia Chersonesos,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 23 G4 Palaia Chersonesos"
336,226712,Panticapes? (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ingulets/Vissun An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 23 F2 Panticapes? fl."
337,226719,Peuke Nesos (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Danube delta An island located in the ancient delta of the Danube river.
338,226720,Peukes/Hieron Stoma,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: ROM The southernmost mouth of the Danube river, described by Strabo as ""hieron stoma"" or the ""holy mouth""."
339,226770,Symbolon Limen,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Balaklava An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 23 G4 Symbolon Limen"
340,226776,Taphrioi,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 23 G2 Taphrioi"
341,226778,Taurike Chersonesos,"The Crimean Peninsula, located in the northern Black Sea."
342,226800,Tyras/Ophioussa,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Belgorod-Dnestrovskiy (Akkerman) A Black Sea colony founded by Miletus.
343,226801,Tyregetai,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 23 C2 Tyregetai"
344,229552,Hermonaktos Kome,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dniester estuary An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 23 unlocated Hermonaktos Kome"
345,229561,Ktenous Limen,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Chersonesos Strabo indicates that Ktenous Limen was equidistant from Chersonesos and Symbolon Limen.
346,229583,Pyrgos Neoptolemou/Ta Neoptolemou,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zatoka? According to Strabo, the Tower of Neoptolemus is located at the mouth of the Dniester river."
347,229599,Trapezous,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ilichovka? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 23 unlocated Trapezous"
348,236377,Bracara Augusta,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Braga POR With settlement from the Neolithic onwards, Bracara Augusta (modern Braga in Portugal) was established as a civitas in 20 BCE. In the third century CE, Diocletian advanced the settlement to the capital of the administrative area known as Conventus Bracarensis, forming part of the Roman province of Gallaecia."
349,236403,Cantabri,The Cantabri were an important tribe of the Atlantic coastal region of northern Iberia.
350,236518,Limia (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Limia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 24 C3 Limia fl."
351,236544,Minius (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Minho/Miño POR/SPN Minius fl. (modern Minho River) is a river of Galicia in the Iberian peninsula. Its ancient name likely derives from minius, the Latin term for cinnabar, and thus its name is, essentially, 'red river'."
352,236567,Nerion/Artabrum/Celticum/Magnum (promuntory),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cape Touriñán Cabo Touriñán is the westernmost point of Peninsular Spain in the Iberian Peninsula.
353,236591,Palantia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palencia The chief town of the Vaccaei in the northern part of Hispania Tarraconensis.
354,236605,Pleutauroi?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near source of R. Ebro An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 24 H1 Pleutauroi?"
355,236706,Vaccaei,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S Burgos, S Palencia, Valladolid and NW Segovia provinces The Vaccaei were a Celtic tribe of the Durius river valley."
356,240935,Kambaiton,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in conventus Bracaraugustanus An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 24 unlocated Kambaiton"
357,246171,Aquae Onesiorum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bagnères-de-Luchon FRA Aquae Onesiorum (modern Bagnères-de-Luchon, France)"
358,246175,Aquitani,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: FRA A confederation of a number (?20) of Iberian tribes initially subjugated by P. Licinius Crassus in 56 BC.
359,246344,Col. Caesaraugusta,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zaragoza Colonia Caesaraugusta (present-day Zaragoza, Spain) was established as a planned Roman colony of the Augustan period (ca. 14 BCE). Military veterans of the Cantabrian wars were settled there."
360,246345,Col. Clunia (Sulpicia),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Peñalba de Castro Col. Clunia (Sulpicia) was a Roman city of Hispania Tarraconensis. The site was besieged by Cn. Pompeius Magnus during the war against Sertorius.
361,246347,Col. Narbo Martius,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Narbonne FRA Col. Narbo Martius (modern Narbonne) was established in Gaul in 118 B.C. as a Roman colony.
362,246349,Col. Tarraco,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tarragona An Iberian site in contact with Greek and Phoenician merchants that became a major Roman center from the third century BC onward.
363,246380,Elusa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Eauze FRA An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 25 F2 Elusa"
364,246382,Emporiae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Empúries A major economic center in the Iberian peninsula, Emporiae (modern Empúries) had indigenous origins but would be the site of a large Greek colonial city."
365,246418,(H)Iberus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ebro The Ebro river in Spain.
366,246433,Ill(ib)erris/Helena,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Elne FRA An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 25 H3 Ill(ib)erris/Helena"
367,246452,Kissa/Cissis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tarragona region Cn. Scipio defeated Hanno somewhere near Kissa in 218 BCE, before capturing the town by assault."
368,246458,Lacetani,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: upper Llobregat and Cardoner valleys and near Segarra Lacetani, a pre-Roman people of the Iberian peninsula."
369,246474,Lounarion Akron,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cap Begur An ancient promontory listed in Ptolemy's Geography. Thought to be the modern Cap Begur on the so-called Costa Brava: a section of the Mediterranean coast of modern Spain (Catalunya).
370,246526,Oiasso,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Irun Oiasso was a settlement of the Vascones.
371,246567,Pompelo,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pamplona Cn. Pompeius Magnus is considered to have been the founder of Pompelo (modern Pamplona) while on campaign against Sertorius in 75 BC.
372,246588,R(h)oda,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ciutadella de Roses A Greek trading settlement in northeastern Spain founded by the Rhodians.
373,246680,Tarbelli (Quattuorsignani),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: FRA The Tarbelli (Quattuorsignani) were a people of western Aquitania.
374,246694,Tolosa,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Toulouse FRA Tolosa (modern Toulouse) was a city of Gallia Narbonensis and the metropolis of the Volcae Tectosages.
375,246730,Vascones,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: N R. Ebro between Jaca and Cabo Higuer. An ancient, pre-Roman Iberian people that inhabited the regions of Navarre, Lower La Rioja, and northwestern Aragon."
376,246750,(Volcae) Tectosages,Lewis (1879) states that the Volcae Arecomici are part of a large ethnic group that includes the Volcae Arecomici . A Gallic people of the Languedoc. Their chief town was Tolosa.
377,255976,Anas (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Guadiana POR/SPN An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 26 C3 Anas fl."
378,255996,Astigi/Col. Augusta Firma,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Écija Now the modern Ecija on the Genil river, Astigi was the capital of one of the four conventus of Baetica and became an Augustan colony after 27 B.C."
379,256005,B(a)elo,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bolonia B(a)elo is located on the northern shore of the Strait of Gibraltar. The town was founded in the late second century BCE and achieved municipal status under Claudius.
380,256010,Baetis/Certis (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Guadalquivir The Guadalquivir river is the fifth longest of the Iberian peninsula.
381,256038,Caepionis Monu.,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Piedra Salmedina? near Chipiona Caepionis Monumentum (Tower of Caepio) was a tower-cum-lighthouse built by the Roman proconsul Quintus Servilius Caepio in 140 BC. The aim was to make the Salmedina reef at the mouth of the river Baitis (Guadalquivir) navigable. The modern toponym is Chipiona.
382,256045,Calpe (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rock of Gibraltar GIB The Rock of Gibraltar.
383,256177,Gadeira/Gades/Col. Augusta Gaditana,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cádiz Gadeira/Gades/Col. Augusta Gaditana (modern Cadiz, Spain) is an ancient city of Spain and one of the oldest continuously occupied cities of Europe. Its origins may be Phoenician (Gadir (Phoenician: גדר))."
384,256197,Herakleion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Isla Sancti Petri An ancient sanctuary of Hercules (Melqart) that Strabo places at one end of the long island (Cotinussa) that extended south from ancient Gades. Significant landscape and coastal change have occurred since antiquity, disrupting the integrity of these islands. Nonetheless, modern scholars have consistently identified the location of the sanctuary as at or near the 17th-18th century Castillio de Sancti Petri, which today occupies a separate homonymous islet. Archaeological finds possibly associated with the sanctuary have been made in the vicinity (both terrestrial and underwater) over the years, but precise location of foundations or other identifiable architecture have remained elusive."
385,256210,Hispalis/Col. Romula,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sevilla/Seville Hispalis/Col. Romula (modern Seville, Spain)."
386,256218,Iberos/Luxia (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tinto The modern Rio Tinto, which flows from the Sierra Morena mountains to the Gulf of Cádiz at Huelva."
387,256222,Ilipa Magna,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Alcalá del Río Ilipa Magna was located on the right bank of the Baetis river. It is now the modern Alcalá del Río. In antiquity it was notable for silver mines located nearby.
388,256231,Italica,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Santiponce. Italica was added to the ""Tentative List"" of UNESCO World Heritage sites in 2019. A city in Spain founded in 206 B.C. by Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus as a site at which to settle Roman soldiers wounded in the Battle of Ilipa."
389,256232,(I)Tucci,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tejada la Nueva, despoblado near Escacena del Campo An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 26 D4 (I)Tucci"
390,256293,Mellaria,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Valdevaqueros (Tarifa) An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 26 E5 Mellaria"
391,256322,Nabrissa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Lebrija An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 26 D5 Nabrissa"
392,256327,Norba/Col. Norbensis Caesarina,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cáceres An Roman settlement, modern Cáceres in Spain."
393,256345,Ossonoba,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Faro POR An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 26 C4 Ossonoba"
394,256363,Phosphoros/Lux Dubia (sanctuary),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Sanlúcar de Barrameda, near ancient Caepionis Monu. Strabo describes this sanctuary in his narrative of sailing up the Guadalquivir River. His text mentions a sanctuary of Phosphorus - this is likely Diana - that is known as ""Lux Dubia"" (Strabo transliterates ""Lucem dubiam"" to Greek characters, thus λοῦκεμ δουβίαν)."
395,256463,Tagus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tejo/Tajo POR/SPN The Tagus river is the longest river on the Iberian Peninsula.
396,256512,Vettones,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between R. Guadiana and R. Duero Vettones, a pre-Roman Celtic population of the Iberian peninsula."
397,265762,Abdera,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Adra An ancient Phoenician port settlement at modern Adra on the southern Spanish coast that was continually inhabited through the end of the Roman period.
398,265808,Ategua,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cortijo de Teba la Vieja (S. Cruz)? A Ibero-Roman fortified settlement with substantial archaeological remains stretching into the Middle Ages. The site of Ategua is located near an abandoned farmstead called Cortijo de Teba la Vieja in the area of modern Santa Cruz, approximately 24 kilometers to the southeast of Cordoba in Spain."
399,265814,Baecula?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bailén A settlement of Hispania Baetica in the territory of Castulo that was the site of Scipio's victories over Hasdrubal (209 B.C.), and over Mago and Masinissa (206 B.C.)"
400,265847,Carpetania,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: central meseta around R. Tajo Carpetania is the territory of the Carpetani, a powerful tribe of the Iberian interior that clashed with Hannibal during the Second Punic War."
401,265855,Castulo,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cortijos de S. Eufemia y de Yangues, 4.4 miles S Linares An Ibero-Roman settlement of Baetica that was inhabited beginning in the Neolithic period. It is noted for the silver mines of the Sierra Morena."
402,265866,Charidemos (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cabo de Gata Ptolemy places the ""headland of Charidemos"" on the southern coast of the Iberian peninsula between Abdera and Barea. Scholars have identified it as the modern Cabo de Gata."
403,265875,Colubraria/Ophiussa (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Formentera Colubraria/Ophiussa Ins. (modern Formentera) is an island of the Balearic Islands in the western Mediterranean Sea.
404,265954,Lucentum,The settlement includes archaeological evidence of a temple as well as public spaces (forum and curia) in addition to necropoleis that continued in use into the seventh century. The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in or near Alicante. Lucentum was a Phoenician and then Roman settlement in Tarraconensis that was largely abandoned after the third century.
405,265960,Mago,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mahón, on Menorca Is. Mago (now Mahón or Maó) on the island of Menorca was established as an ancient commercial center because of its high-quality harborage. Its ancient name is thought to derive from Mago, brother of Hannibal, who occupied the Insula Minor after he fled from Gades in 206 BCE."
406,265961,Mainake,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cerro del Peñón?, Torre del Mar An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 27 A5 Mainake"
407,265963,Malaca,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Málaga A Roman city of Baetica that was perhaps founded by the Carthaginians in the fifth century BC. Modern Malaga, on the Mediterranean coast of Spain."
408,265989,Oretania/Oretani,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S central Hispania, centered on Oretum A pre-Roman ancient Iberian or Celtic tribe in Extremadura, La Mancha, eastern Andalusia and Murcia. Oretania is a regional name, which associated the area with the ancient people."
409,265990,Oretum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nuestra Señora de Zúqueca, 2 miles SE Granátula Oretum was a tributary city of the Oretani that was a part of the conventus of Carthago Nova, according to Pliny the Elder."
410,265992,Orospeda (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: sierra NW from Cartagena to Sierra de Cazorla Strabo identifies Orospeda as bordering Celtiberia on the south. The Barrington Atlas tentatively locates it in and arround the Sierra de Segura, just to the east of, and across the headwaters of the Guadalquivir river from, the Sierra de Cazorla, which it more confidently identifies with the Tugiensis Saltus."
411,266001,Pityussae (islands),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ibiza and Formentera Is. Pityussae Inss., an ancient island group comprising the islands of Ebusus and Ophiussa."
412,266032,Scombraria (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Escombrera Is. Ancient Scombraria has been identified with the modern Isla de Escombreras at the entrance to the bay of Cartagena. Strabo reports that the island's name is derived from the mackerel harvested there that are in turn used to manufacture garum.
413,266070,Tucci/Col. Augusta Gemella,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Martos, W Jaén An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 27 B4 Tucci/Col. Augusta Gemella"
414,275577,Abila M.,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Acho SPN A low mountain overlooking the port of Ceuta in North Africa.
415,275639,Heras? (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palomas Is. in Straits of Gibraltar SPN An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 28 D1 Heras? Ins."
416,275640,Heras? (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Peregil Is. in Straits of Gibraltar Heras? Ins. is a small, rocky island located in the southern shore of the Strait of Gibraltar, some 200 meters from the Moroccan coast"
417,275666,Lixus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tchemmisch Lixus was an ancient settlement of Mauretania Tingitana. It was founded as a Punic port settlement as early as the eighth century B.C. Under the Roman emperor Claudius Lixus was elevated to the rank of colonia.
418,275743,Zilia (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Oued el Kebir/Oued Khobs A river on the northern Atlantic coast of what is now Morocco, presumably near the ancient settlement of Zilil/Zilis. The lower course of the modern Lhachef/Hachef is the obvious match, although its precise ancient path through the coastal wetlands to the mouth mentioned by Ptolemy is uncertain."
419,278609,Emporikos Kolpos/Sagigi Sinus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: coastal region S Lixus An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 28 unlocated Emporikos Kolpos/Sagigi Sinus"
420,278633,(Vo)pisciana,"The Antonine Itinerary attests to a station ""Vopiscianis"" (GeogRav ""Bobiscianis"") on the route from Tocolosida (modern Bled Takourart in Morocco) to Tingi (modern Tangier). Ptolemy's ""Piskiana"" would seem to reference the same feature or a nearby settlement associated with the road station. Various suggestions have been made by modern scholars for its identification with a suitable archaeological site or ruin in the area; the current communis opinio holds for Souq el Arbaâ du Gharb (see Souk el Arba)."
421,295219,Arsennaria,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sidi bou Ras A settlement in the Roman province of Mauretania Caesariensis in North Africa.
422,295240,Cartenna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ténès An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 30 B3 Cartenna"
423,295327,Quiza Cenitana,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: el Bénian Quiza Cenitana was a place located on the coast of Mauretania Caesariensis. Various ancient authors refer to it by different terms - colonia (Ptolemy), municipium (Antonine Itinerary), and oppidum (Pliny the Elder)."
424,295338,Serbes (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Oued Isser The ancient Serbes river, mentioned by Ptolemy, is probably to be associated with the lower course of the modern Yusr (Isser) in Algeria."
425,295374,Zucchabar,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Miliana Zucchabar was an ancient city of Mauretania Caesariensis with Punic origins. The modern Algerian community of Miliana lies atop and around the largely unexcavated ancient site. Epigraphic evidence indicates that the Roman emperor Augustus established a veteran colony there.
426,305031,Assarath (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Oued Nil An ancient river of north Africa, placed by Ptolemy between Igilgili (modern Jijel) and the mouth of the Amsaga river (modern Oued El Kebir). The Assarath is probably to be identified with the modern Oued Nil in Algeria."
427,305090,Hippo Regius,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Annaba, formerly Bône Hippo Regius (modern Annaba in Algeria) was a colony established by Phoenicians from Tyre as early as the twelfth century BC."
428,305095,Igilgili,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jijel Originally a Carthaginian trading post, Igilgili eventually came to be part of Mauretania Caesariensis as a Roman colony."
429,314854,Aegimoeroe (islands),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Iles de Zembra An island group located off Cape Bon, Tunisia. Pliny the Elder knew of the ""duae Aegimoeroe"", presumably referring to Ile Zembra and Ile Zembretta."
430,314921,Carthago,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Carthage The ancient city of Carthage, located in modern Tunis (Tunisia). Carthage engaged the Roman state in a series of wars conventionally referred to as the Punic Wars by scholars."
431,314923,Castra Corneli(ana)?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Galaat-el-Andeless or Galaat el Oued BAtlDir tentatively associates this site, attested in multiple ancient sources, with either modern Galaat-el-Andeless or modern Galaat el Oued in Tunisia."
432,315066,Magni Campi/Bullensis Campus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: plain SE Bulla Regia A plain, mentioned by several ancient authors, that lies to the southeast of Bulla Regia (modern Hammam-Darradji in Tunisia), across the Bagradas (modern Medjerda) river."
433,315093,Neapolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nabeul Neapolis (modern Nabeul, Tunisia) is a coastal town on the Cap Bon peninsula that was founded by Greeks from Cyrene in the fifth century B.C."
434,315151,Siagu,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ksar-ez-Zit An ancient settlement in the Roman province of Africa Proconsularis. It was located at a site known today as Ksar el-Zit, which is in the vicinity of Bir Bou Rekba to the northwest of Hammamet, Tunisia."
435,315184,Taphitis (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ras Mostafa Modern Cape Mustafa, Tunisia."
436,315192,Thabraca,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tabarca Thabraca was a maritime city of Numidia located at the mouth of the river Tusca.
437,315223,Thugga/Tokai?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dougga Thugga/Tokai? (Dougga) is an ancient site in Tunisia with a rich heritage that includes Numidian-Berber, Punic, ancient Roman, and Byzantine phases. The city's Roman phase is particularly well preserved and UNESCO listed the site as a World Heritage Site in 1997."
438,315233,Tunes,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tunis Tunes was a Libyan city in Africa Proconsularis. Rome prevailed in a land battle south of Tunes in 256 BC during the First Punic War.
439,321665,Guzantes,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: E coast of Tunisia between Hammamet and Gabes Herodotus lists the Guzantes among the Libyan tribes.
440,324666,Ammonos Balithonos Akra/Caput Vada,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ras Kapoudia This promontory, known today as Ras Kaboudia, is the easternmost point of the coast of Tunisia."
441,324684,Capsa (Iustiniana),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gafsa Capsa (Iustiniana) was an ancient city of Byzacium in north Africa.
442,324690,Cercina (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Grand Kerkenna A group of islands lying off the east coast of Tunisia in the Gulf of Gabès, the islands were a place of exile in Roman antiquity."
443,324803,Ruspina,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Henchir-Tenir An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 33 G1 Ruspina"
444,334497,Aurasius (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aurès Mts. The modern Djebel Aurès: an eastern extension of the Atlas Mountain System in northeastern Algeria and northwestern Tunisia.
445,334632,Thabudeos/Tabudium Oppidum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tehouda A tell settlement, known today as Tehouda, located in modern Algeria and mentioned by Pliny the Elder. Archaeological remains at the site provide evidence for mud-brick fortification walls and might be consistent with a sixth century reoccupation and Byzantine garrison of a ""Dabousis"" recorded by Procopius."
446,344282,Abrotonum/Sabratha,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sabrata, Zuaga The westernmost of the ""three cities"" of Tripolis, Abrotonum/Sabratha was founded ca. 500 B.C. by the Phoenicians. The city was part of the Numidian Kingdom of Massinissa before becoming a Roman city."
447,344428,Macae,"Macae, Libyan Tribe, cited: BAtlas 35 F3"
448,344429,Machlyae?,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 35 B1 Machlyae?"
449,344440,Meninx/Lotophagitis/Girba (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gerba TUN Meninx/Lotophagitis/Girba Ins. (modern Djerba) is the largest island of North Africa.
450,344491,Syrtis Maior,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Greater Syrtis The Gulf of Sidra.
451,344576,Zouchis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bahiret el-Bibane TUN Strabo provides the ancient Greek name Zouchis for the modern Bahiret el Bibane, a coastal lagoon located in Tunisia."
452,349582,Chouzis/Chouchis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: TUN Ptolemy lists this city (polis) first in a list ""between the two Syrtis's"". Some scholars have tentatively identified it with Zouchis (i.e., Taricheiai), attested in Strabo and Stephanus, and with Chouchis, attested in a fragment of Artemidorus of Ephesus. Mattingly (BAtlas) was unwilling to accept the equivalence, listing Chouzis as ""unlocated"", a decision followed here."
453,363927,Astiagi/Dysopon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bir Bu Giarada? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 37 B1 Astiagi/Dysopon"
454,373732,Apollonia/Sozousa,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marsa Susa Apollonia was an ancient city in the Cyrenaica and a port of Cyrene. The city was one of the five cities of the Libyan Pentapolis in Cyrenaica.
455,373736,Arsinoe/Taucheira,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tocra Arsinoe/Taucheira (modern Tocra) may have originally been a Greek foundation that was part of Pentapolis of Cyrenaica. It eventually became a Roman colony and, by the time of Justinian, was fortified."
456,373750,Barke,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: el-Merg/al-Marj An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 38 B1 Barke"
457,373757,Berenice,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Benghazi Berenice was a coastal city of western Cyrenaica, located at modern Benghazi in Libya. Founded in the third century BCE, it replaced the earlier Greek colony of Eusperides, which had been centered a short distance to the northeast. Berenice was named in honor of Berenice of Cyrene, the queen of Ptolemy III Euergetes."
458,373760,Boreion (promontory),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ras Taiunes Modern Ra’s Tāwīnis on Libya's Mediterranean coast.
459,373777,Cyrenaica (region),"There are a number of international archaeological projects working in this area, including the Cyrenaica Archaeological Project . Joyce Reynolds' long-awaited corpus of the inscriptions of Roman Cyrenaica is now in preparation for digital publication. An ancient region (the environs of the city of Cyrene) that lies in the eastern part of modern Libya."
460,373778,Cyrene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ain Shahat, Grennah An ancient Greek city founded in eastern Libya ca. 630 B.C., Cyrene later became part of the Roman Empire (ca. 74 B.C.). The site was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1982."
461,373786,Euesperides,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Benghazi Euesperides was the westernmost Greek colony of Cyrenaica. The remains of the site are found on the outskirts of modern Benghazi near the north Libyan coast. Under Ptolemy III Euergetes, the new city of Berenice was built a short distance southwest of Euesperides and the original site was subsequently abandoned."
462,373839,Lathon/Leton (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bu Shatin According to Strabo, the Lathon/Leton fl. flows into the lake of the Hesperides."
463,373868,Paliouros,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Wadi et-Tmimi Paliouros was a village of the Marmaridae that was located near a temple sacred to Heracles.
464,373879,Ptolemais/Barkes Limen,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tolmeta/Tulmeitia An ancient capital of Cyrenaica, Ptolemais was probably named after Ptolemy III Euergetes. The city was founded in either the seventh or sixth century BC."
465,373906,Tritonis? Palus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bu Hayrat Bu Zazirah An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 38 B1 Tritonis? Palus"
466,383543,Addua (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Adda The Adda river is a river in northern Italy and a tributary of the Padus fl. (Po river).
467,383585,Belounoi,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: E L. Garda An ancient people mentioned by Ptolemy. They are possibly to have been located to the east of modern lake Garda.
468,383587,Benacus (lake),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Garda Benacus L. (Lake Garda) is the largest lake in Italy.
469,383602,Brixellum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Brescello An ancient Roman town in the plain of the Po River.
470,383603,Brixia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Brescia An ancient settlement. Modern Brescia in Italy.
471,383607,Campi Macri,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: around Magreta The Campi Macri, described by both Greek and Latin authors, were located southwest of Mutina (modern Modena) and are noted as the site of a significant pastoral-mercantile center of the first millennium BCE that endured into the first century CE."
472,383614,Cenomani,The Cenomani were a Celtic people whose territory centered on the valley of the Maine river; Vindinum or Suindinum was their principal center.
473,383627,Comum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Como Comum (modern Como) was a key city of regio XI of ancient Italy. The Celtic site received a Roman colony (Novum Comum) under Iulius Caesar in the first century BC.
474,383628,Cremona,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cremona Cremona is a city of Cisalpine Gaul located on the Po River. The Romans established a colony of 6,000 settlers there after 225 B.C. Cremona was the first Latin colony placed north of the Po River."
475,383633,Dertona,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tortona A city founded in the second century B.C. at the junction of the Via Postumia and Via Aemilia Scauri which merged to become the Via Julia Augusta.
476,383635,Duria (Bautica) (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Doire Baltée/Dora Baltea FRA/ITL Duria (Bautica) fl. (Dora Baltea) is a river of northern Italy that is a tributary of the Padus fl. (Po river).
477,383648,Fidentia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Fidenza Fidentia (modern Fidenza) began as a Roman castrum and in 41 BC it became a municipium. The settlement is located along the Via Aemilia, between Parma and Placentia."
478,383664,Genua,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Genoa Genua was an ancient settlement of Liguria that came to be destroyed by Carthage in 209 BC. Thereafter it received Roman municipal privileges and was rebuilt.
479,383678,Insubres,"The founders of Mediolanum (Milan), conquered by Rome in the 2nd century BCE. A Gallic tribe of Northern Italy, giving their name to the region of Insubria."
480,383694,Libarna,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Serravalle Scrivia Libarna was a Roman city located in Liguria.
481,383698,Liguria (region),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Liguria Liguria is a coastal region of northwest Italy.
482,383704,Mantua,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mantua Mantua (modern Mantova) is a city of Etruscan origin that received Roman citizenship in 49 B.C.
483,383708,Mincius (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mincio The Mincio river is a tributary of the Po river.
484,383715,Mutina,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Modena Ancient Mutina (originally the Etruscan town Mutna) was first inhabited by Villanovans and later by Ligurians and the Gaulish Boii. The Boii sieged the city in 218 BC, and later Mutina was re-established as a Roman colony (183 BC). It was an important city and played a role in the civil wars during the last decades of the Roman Republic."
485,383737,Parma,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Parma The site of a Bronze Age terramare settlement, Parma was re-founded first by Etruscans and later by the Romans when a colony was established there in 183 B.C. The city was rebuilt by Augustus and was eventually sacked by Attila."
486,383741,Placentia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Piacenza Placentia (modern Piacenza) was an ancient site with Gallic and Etruscan occupation before the Roman conquest of 218 B.C. The city persisted into Late Antiquity.
487,383755,Regium Lepidum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Reggio Emilia Regium Lepidum (modern Reggio Emilia) was founded by M. Aemilius Lepidus in either 187 or 175 B.C.
488,383798,Ticinum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pavia An ancient city of Gallia Transpadana, Ticinum lay along the line of the ancient Via Aemilia. Today, Ticinum is the modern city of Pavia."
489,383799,Ticinus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ticino ITL/SWI Ticinus fl. (Ticino) is a left-bank tributary of the Padus fl. (Po river) in northern Italy.
490,383802,Trebia (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Trebbia A river of Liguria and Emilia-Romagna and one of the four main right-bank tributaries of the river Po.
491,383804,Trident(i)um,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Trento An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 39 I1 Trident(i)um"
492,383808,Vada Sabatia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vado Ligure An ancient settlement that began as a second century B.C. Roman military outpost.
493,383815,Vercellae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vercelli Originally a captial of the Libici, a tribe of Ligurians, Vercellae became an important Roman municipium."
494,383821,Victumulae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bessa A settlement of the Celtic Insubres located in the territory of Vercellae.
495,393379,Ariminum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rimini Ariminum (Rimini) is a Roman colony founded on the Adriatic coast in 268 BC.
496,393383,At(h)esis (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Adige The Adige River is the second longest river in Italy. It flows from the Alps to the Adriatic Sea.
497,393394,Butrium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: W Casa Rossa Butrium was located in Gallia Cispadana; Strabo locates it along the road from Ravenna to Altinum.
498,393397,Caesena,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cesena An Umbrian and Etruscan city on the Savio River that passed into Roman control in the third century B.C.
499,393420,Faventia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Faenza A Roman city on the Via Aemilia.
500,393424,Forum Cornelii,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Imola An ancient city founded by Lucius Cornelius Sulla as a colony ca. 82 BC.
501,393425,Forum Gallorum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Castelfranco A town on the Via Aemilia that was the site of a battle waged between M. Antonius and Hirtius and Pansa during the siege of Mutina.
502,393436,(H)Atria,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Adria (H)Atria (modern Adria) was a Venetic and, later, Etruscan settlement. The city itself is likely a sixth century BC foundation."
503,393441,Iulia Concordia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Concordia Sagittaria Iulia Concordia (Concordia Sagittaria) was established by the Romans in 42 BC at the intersection of the Via Annia and the Via Postumia.
504,393444,Labinios (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Samoggia Reported by multiple ancient sources in the context of a meeting between Octavian and Antony in 43 BCE, the Lavinius appears to have been a left-hand tributary of the ancient Rhenus (the modern Reno in Italy's Po watershed). Sources place the island venue of the meeting relative to Bononia and/or Mutina (RE after Nissen). BAtlDir tentatively equates the modern Samoggia with the Lavinius, but on BAtlas 40 the label ""Lavinius?"" is applied to a ""torrente"" that OSM names ""Lavinius"" (sic), the ""Samoggia"" in OSM being a separate ""torrente"" to the west that remains unlabeled in BAtlas 40. No Latin source appears to have preserved the name."
505,393459,Opitergium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Oderzo Opitergium (modern Oderzo) is a settlement of the Veneti along the Piave River located at a junction of the Via Postumia.
506,393480,Ravenna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ravenna Perhaps originating as an Etruscan center, Ravenna became especially important in the late Roman period. From AD 402 until 476, Ravenna served as the capital of the Western Roman Empire, after which (until AD 554) it was the Ostrogothic capital. From AD 554 to 771, Ravenna was the center of the Byzantine Exarchate of Ravenna and, finally, became the seat of the Lombard kingdom. A city of northern Adriatic Italy that served as the capital of the Western Roman Empire from A.D. 402 until 476."
507,393484,Rubico (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pisciatello A river in northeastern Italy that served, during the late Roman Republic, as the southern boundary of the province of Cisalpine Gaul, and which Caesar famously crossed with an army in 49 BCE, thereby precipitating the Roman Civil War. Its modern location is debated (see ""Connections"" and ""Details"" sections)."
508,393490,Sapis (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Savio Sapis fl. (Savio river)
509,393492,Scultenna (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Panaro The Scultenna, the modern Panaro river, is a tributary of the Po river in northern Italy."
510,393498,Spina,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Valpega An Etruscan port city at the mouth of the Po River on the Adriatic Sea, most likely founded at the end of the sixth century B.C."
511,393513,Vicetia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vicenza Vicetia (modern Vicenza) as a settlement of the Veneti in northern Italy.
512,403180,‘Argoos Limen’,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Portoferraio A port on the island of Ilva (modern Elba), so named based on the resting place of the Argonauts, according to Strabo."
513,403181,Arnus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arno The Arno River flows from its origin at Mount Falterona to the Marina di Pisa, where it empties into the Tyrrhenian Sea."
514,403208,Fossae Papirianae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Massarosa Fossae Papirianae refers to a navigable coastal canal and road station location along the Via Aemilia Scauri between Luna and Pisae.
515,403212,Fufluna/Populonium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Populonia and mine Populonia was likely the first Etruscan state to mint its own coins. During the Second Punic War, Populonia supplied iron to Cornelius Scipio in his contest against Hannibal. Populonia likely derives its name from the Etruscan god 'Fufluns' and was an important maritime and commercial center of Etruria located in the Colline Metallifere."
516,403222,Ilva/Aethalia (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Elba The largest island of the Tuscan Archipelago, Ilva (modern Elba) is located in the Tyrrhenian Sea 50 km east of Corsica. It is the third largest Italian island."
517,403234,Luca,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lucca Luca (the modern Lucca) was originally an Etruscan settlement that became a Roman colony ca. 180 B.C.
518,403235,Luna,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Luni Luna (the modern Luni) is an ancient settlement of Liguria. It was re-founded by the Romans as a colonial settlement in 177 BC.
519,403253,Pisae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pisa A city situated on the Tyrrhenian seaboard and likely of Etruscan origin. Its early nature was that of a mercantile center. The city became a Roman colony in 89 B.C.
520,403254,Pistoriae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pistoia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 41 D2 Pistoriae"
521,403260,Portus Herculis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Port’ Ercole Portus Herculis is located on the eastern side of the Monte Argentario peninsula, about 40 km south-east of Grosseto and about 7 km south-west of Orbetello. and about 12 km from Porto Santo Stefano. The site was in use as a port during the Etruscan, Roman, and Late Antique phases of occupation. It is included on the Peutinger map and in the Itinerarium Maritimum."
522,403280,Telamon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Talamonaccio An ancient city of coastal Etruria, Telamon was eventually razed by L. Cornelius Sulla."
523,403288,Vada Volaterrana,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vada Vada Volaterrana was an Etruscan port city located north of the mouth of the Cecina River.
524,403291,Vatl/Vetulonia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vetulonia The Etruscan Vatluna resisted Rome's influence in the seventh century BCE, although the city may have been the source of Rome's adoption of the curule chair and the fasces."
525,403292,Velathri/Volaterrae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Volterra A major Etruscan city of North Etruria.
526,409218,Bondelia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: inland town in N Etruria An ancient settlement, attested by literary or documentary sources, whose precise location cannot be determined today"
527,409225,Herakleous Hieron,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on coast between Luna and Arnus fl. An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 41 unlocated Herakleous Hieron"
528,413003,Aesis (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Esino Aesis fl. (modern Esino river) is a river of the Marche region of Italy and flows into the Adriatic Sea.
529,413005,Alba Fucens,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Albe A town of the Aequi along the Marsic frontier where the Romans established a Latin colony in 304/3 B.C. Alba Fucens was a significant center of the middle republican period and became a place where Rome kept state prisoners, including Syphax of Numidia, Perseus of Macedonia, and Bituitus, king of the Arverni."
530,413012,Ameria (Umbria),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Amelia. Given the evidence from Cicero's speech Pro Roscio Amerino , the town was still prosperous ca. 80 BC. Ameria, claimed by Cato the Elder (quoted by Pliny) as the oldest town in Umbria, was an important center that may have gained Roman municipal status as early as 338 B.C., following the Latin War."
531,413013,Amiternum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Vittorino This site is not to be confused with the similarly named Amiternum located in Samnium. Amiternum was an ancient settlement of the Sabines that Rome captured in 293 BC. The settlement was situated at the intersection of four Roman roads: the Via Caecilia, the Via Claudia Nova, and the two branches of the Via Salaria. The historian Sallust was born at Amiternum in 86 B.C."
532,413014,Ancon(a),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ancona A port city founded by colonists from Syracuse in 387 BC.
533,413024,Aquae Cutiliae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Paterno Roman city situated on mineral springs known today as Terme di Cotilia or Terme di Vespasiano in the Sabina region, Italy"
534,413032,Arretium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arezzo Arretium (the Etruscan Aritim) was one of the duodecim populi Etruriae and described by Livy as one of the ""Capitae Etruriae"". The Romans captured the city in 311 B.C. and it became a station on the via Cassia."
535,413036,Asculum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ascoli Asculum (modern Ascoli Piceno) was the principal city of the Piceni.
536,413037,Asisium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Assisi Asisium is famous as the birthplace of St. Francis as well as the Roman poet Propertius. A Roman settlement built atop an earlier Umbrian one on a western foothill of the Apennines, Asisium received municipal status and was assigned to the Tribus Sergia. Totila destroyed most of the city in A.D. 545."
537,413039,Aternum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pescara Aternum (modern Pescara) was a settlement at the mouth of the Aternus River.
538,413045,Auximum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Osimo Auximum (modern Osimo) is an ancient settlement of Picenum. The original foundation was likely carried out by Greeks from Ancona. The Romans made it a base for military operations from 174 B.C.
539,413048,Axia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Castel d’Asso Axia was an Etruscan center located to the west of Viterbo.
540,413054,Blera,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Blera Blera was a significant Etruscan settlement in the vicinity of Tarquinii. It lay along the line of the Roman Via Clodia. It received Roman municipal status and was enrolled in the tribus Arnensis. The city flourished in the sixth and fifth centuries BCE.
541,413065,Carsulae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Damiano An ancient settlement originating the late fourth or third centuries BC and lying along the route of the Roman Via Flaminia.
542,413076,Centum Cellae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Civitavecchia Centum Cellae (modern Civitavecchia) was an ancient coastal settlement in Tyrrhenian Etruria. The emperor Trajan rebuilt the harbor at the site in 106/7 and Pliny the Younger mentions its name, Centum Cellae. Saracens destroyed the city in A.D. 812."
543,413088,Clanis (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Chiani Clanis fl. (Chiana river) is a river of central Italy that flows into the Tiber river.
544,413096,Clusium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Chiusi Clusium, the Etruscan Clevsin (modern Chiusi), was a major city of Etruria. Lars Porsena was a sixth century B.C. king of Clusium."
545,413105,Corfinium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Corfinio Corfinium was the principal city of the Paeligni in the valley of the Aternus river. The site becomes the capital of the breakaway Italic groups that oppose Rome during the Social War.
546,413107,Cosa,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ansedonia A Latin colony in south Etruria founded ca. 273 B.C.
547,413114,Cures,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Corese Terra Cures (also Cures Sabini) was a Sabine city in the Tiber River valley along the Via Salaria. The Romans conquered Cures in 290 B.C. under M'. Curius Dentatus and granted it status as a municipium in the tribus Sergia.
548,413121,Eretum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Casacotta Eretum was a settlement of the Sabines and is involved in legendary accounts of early Rome.
549,413122,Etruria/Tuscia (region),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Toscana and N Lazio Etruria/Tuscia was an ancient region of Central Italy inhabited by the Etruscans. Its spatial footprint was roughly equivalent to the modern Italian regions of Toscana and northern Lazio.
550,413124,Faesulae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Fiesole Faesulae (modern Fiesole) was an Etruscan foundation called Viesul, Viśl, or Vipsul. The city was conquered by Romans in 283 B.C. Sulla would settle Roman veterans there. Citizens of the town supported the conspiracy of Catiline."
551,413126,Falerii Veteres,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Civita Castellana Falerii Veteres was the principal city of the Faliscans. It is located on a tuff outcropping at the point where several tributaries of the Treia join to flow into the Tiber. After rebelling against Rome in 241 B.C., Falerii suffered serious reversals of fortune."
552,413129,Fanum Fortunae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Fano Fanum Fortunae, the site of a shrine to Fortuna, is first mentioned in the ancient sources in 49 B.C. when Iulius Caesar held the town. An Augustan colony was established at the site and the Romans prevailed in the Battle of Fano in A.D. 271. The town was eventually destroyed by the Ostrogoths."
553,413135,Firmum Picenum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Fermo Firmum Picenum was founded as a Latin colony in 264 B.C. with 6,000 colonists. It received municipal status after 48 B.C."
554,413142,Foruli,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Civitatomassa Foruli was a Sabine town located between Amiternum and Interocrea.
555,413147,Forum Flaminii,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Giovanni Profiamma, near Foligno Caius Flaminius founded Forum Flaminii in 220 B.C. during the construction of the Via Flaminia."
556,413149,Forum Sempronii,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Fossombrone An ancient Roman colony, founded in the time of Gaius Sempronius Gracchus."
557,413151,Fulginiae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Foligno Fulginiae was an ancient town of Umbria, Italy, on the later line of the Via Flaminia, ca. 20 km south of Nuceria."
558,413157,Graviscae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Porto Clementino The port of the Etruscan city of Tarquinia.
559,413170,Hispellum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Spello Hispellum (modern Spello) is an ancient settlement of Umbria that became a Roman colony in the first century BC.
560,413174,Iguvium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gubbio An Umbrian hilltown, Iguvium is best known as the find-spot of the Iguvine Tables."
561,413178,Interamna Nahars,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Terni An Umbrian center founded in the seventh century B.C. that became an important Roman municipality along the Via Flaminia.
562,413180,Intercisa/Petra Pertusa,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Galleria del Furlo Intercisa/Petra Pertusa was a road station on the Via Flaminia. The toponym derives from the rock-cut tunnel or galleria that dates to the reign of Vespasian.
563,413181,Interocrium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Antrodoco A Sabine town that was later known as a mansio on the course of the Via Salaria.
564,413184,Lucus Feroniae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Scorano A sanctuary and grove of Feronia and an associated settlement located along the Via Tiberina in the territory of Capena.
565,413189,Manliana,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Torrita di Siena One of two Roman road stations with this name in Italy attested in ancient sources. The Barrington Atlas Directory localizes this one at modern Torrita di Siena.
566,413190,Marrucini,"The Marrucini were an Italic tribe centered around Teate in the Abruzzo. In conflict with Rome through the end of the Second Samnite War, the Marrucini became allied with Rome thereafter. Their language was likely in the Sabellic family."
567,413191,Marsi,The Marsi were an ancient Italic tribe. Their chief center was located at Marruvium.
568,413198,Matrinos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Piomba An ancient river of Picenum, mentioned by Strabo and Ptolemy, which is to be identified with the modern Torrente Piomba. The ancient Latin name is reflected in that of a coastal road station labeled on the Peutinger map (Macrinum), whence (correcting ""c"" to ""t"" after the Greek sources) BAtlas ""Matrinus? fl."""
569,413199,Metaurus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Metauro Metaurus fl. (Metauro river) flows from near Monte dei Frati in the Apennine Mountains to the Adriatic Sea. The Metaurus was the site of two key battles in antiquity. The first, in 207 B.C., saw the defeat of Hasdrubal Barca by the consuls Marcus Livius Salinator and Gaius Claudius Nero. The second, the Battle of Fano, saw Aurelian defeat the Alamanni in A.D. 271."
570,413200,Mevania,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bevagna Mevania (modern Bevagna) was a Roman municipium in Umbria located on the Via Flaminia. The town was enrolled in the tribus Aemilia.
571,413220,Nar (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nera The Nera river, a tributary of the Tiber."
572,413224,Nepet,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nepi Nepet had become a Roman town before 396 B.C. when Livy dubs it, along with Sutrium, as one of the ""keys"" to Etruria. It became a Roman colony in 383 B.C. and a municipium after the Social War."
573,413225,Nequinum/Narnia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Narni Nequinum/Narnia (modern Narni) was an ancient settlement in Umbria. It became a Latin colony in 299 BC.
574,413234,Ortona,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ortona An ancient site in Italy that most likely originates with the Frentani.
575,413248,Perusia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Perugia A city of ancient Umbrian and Etruscan origin, Perusia aided Rome during the Second Punic War but was sieged and reduced by Octavian in the first century B.C. during campaigns against Lucius Antonius. It was rebuilt by Augustus and became Colonia Vibia Augusta Perusia quite late, in A.D. 251-3, under Trebonianus Gallus."
576,413253,Picenum (region),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S Marche and N Abruzzo Picenum was a region of Central Italy, extending along the coast of the Adriatic Sea from the mouth of the Aesis to that of the Matrinus, and inland as far as the central Apennines."
577,413265,Plestinus L.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Padule di Colfiorito In antiquity, the lacus Plestinus was located in Umbria and was the site of a military engagement during the Second Punic War."
578,413283,Reate,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rieti Reate (modern Rieti) was a major Sabine center that came to be captured by Manius Curius Dentatus in late third century B.C.
579,413284,Regis Villa/Regae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Le Murelle An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 B4 Regis Villa/Regae"
580,413288,Rusellae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Roselle An Etruscan city captured by Rome in 294 B.C., Rusellae's >2 mile circuit of megalithic walls represent an important early instance of stone fortification walling in the Italian peninsula."
581,413290,Sabatinus lacus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lago di Bracciano A volcanic lake in Latium north of Rome (modern Lago di Bracciano).
582,413291,Sabini,"The Sabini (Sabines) were an Italic tribe of central Italy and belonged to the Osco-Umbrian language group. Their territory straddled parts of the modern regions of Lazio, Umbria, and the Abruzzo. Ancient writers connect them with Rome at the time of its foundation, as represented by the episode of the 'rape of the Sabine women' reported by various ancient authors."
583,413294,Sagrus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sangro A river of eastern Italy (modern Sangro).
584,413306,Sarsina,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sarsina Sarsina was a town of the Umbri that was captured by Cornelius Scipio in 271 B.C. and was the birthplace of the Latin author Plautus. The necropolis at Sarsina has been extensively studied.
585,413310,Sena Gallica,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Senigallia Senonian Gauls founded Sena Gallica at the mouth of the Misa River in the fourth century B.C. This place became the site of Rome's earliest colony on the Adriatic seaboard before 280 B.C. Pompeius Magnus destroyed the city in 82 B.C.
586,413312,Sentinum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Sassoferrato An ancient town in the region of Italy now known as Le Marche, Sentinum is perhaps best known for the pivotal Battle of Sentinum of the Third Samnite War that took place nearby in 295 BC."
587,413314,Septempeda,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: San Severino Marche Septempeda was an ancient settlement of Picenum. Its Roman phase began as a conciliabulum in the third century B.C.
588,413320,Spoletium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Spoleto An ancient city along the route of the Via Flaminia; an initial colony was established there in 241 BCE.
589,413321,Piammiano,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: probably Piammiano Site of an ancient settlement that flourished between the seventh century BCE and the early sixth century CE, located west across the Tiber from the modern village of Attigliano in Italy. Piammiano is probably to be identified with ""Statonia"", which is mentioned in ancient sources."
590,413328,Sutrium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sutri An ancient settlement along the line of what would become the Roman Via Cassia and described by Livy, together with Nepet, as one of the keys to Etruria. A Latin colony was founded at the site after the fall of Veii."
591,413330,Tadinae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gualdo Tadino The Umbrian site was originally known as ""Tarsina"", becoming Tadinum after the Roman conquest in 266 B.C. During the Second Punic War (217 B.C.) the site was razed by Hannibal. The site suffered violent destruction on two more occasions - once in 47 B.C. and again under Alaric in A.D. 410. Tadinae (Sant'Antonio della Rasina to the west of Gualdo Tadino) is an ancient municipium of Umbria and was a road station on the Via Flaminia."
592,413332,Tarchna/Tarquinii,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Tarquinia An ancient city of Etruria and the leading city of the doudecim populi Etruriae, Tarquinia (called Tarch(u)na or Tarchna in Etruscan) was a major urban center of ancient Italy."
593,413333,Teate,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Chieti Originally named Teate Marrucinorum, Teate (modern Chieti) was the principal settlement of the Marrucini. Strabo attributes its origins to Arcadian Greeks."
594,413340,Tinea (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Topino The Topino river in Umbria.
595,413342,Tiora Matiene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Teora?, near Barete Tora is also connected with the legend of Picus. Tiora Matiene was the site of an ancient oracle of the god Mars. The Barrington Atlas tentatively placed it at modern Teora, near Barete."
596,413354,Tuder,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Todi Tuder, modern Todi, is an ancient city of Umbria. Its foundation likely occurred during the eighth/seventh centuries BC, although legendary traditions associate the site with the activities of Herakles."
597,413373,Velzna/*Volsinii Veteres/Urbiventus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Orvieto Volsinii Veteres was one of the most powerful and important cities of Etruria, as it was the location of the important federal sanctuary located at the Fanum Voltumnae."
598,413376,Vestini,"The Vestini were an ancient tribe of Italy and lived in the southern part of the modern Abruzzo, between the Gran Sasso and the northern bank of the Aterno river. A major center was located at Pitinum."
599,413393,Vulci,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Montalto di Castro Vulci (originally Velch or Velx in Etruscan) was an Etruscan city 80 km northwest of Rome. Vulci was one of the duodecim populi Etruriae.
600,416773,Aequum Faliscum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Via Flaminia near site of Falerii A settlement along the Via Flaminia, now unlocated. Strabo places it between Otricoli and Rome; the possibility must be allowed that it is taken to be identical with Falerii."
601,416779,Batia/Vatia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Reate An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 unlocated ‘Batia’/‘Vatia’"
602,416787,Caprium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Etruscan town An ancient settlement, attested by literary or documentary sources, whose precise location cannot be determined today"
603,416817,Issa (unlocated),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in a lake near Reate An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 unlocated Issa ins."
604,416822,Korsoula,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: 80 stades from Reate An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 unlocated Korsoula"
605,416824,Larolon,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Via Flaminia between Ocriculum and Narnia A toponym attested in Strabo but not elsewhere - perhaps the name of a stream emptying into the Tiber near Ocriculum.
606,416825,Lista,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sabine town An ancient settlement, attested by literary or documentary sources, whose precise location cannot be determined today"
607,416828,Marouion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: not far from Reate An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 unlocated Marouion"
608,416830,Mephyla,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: 190 stades from Reate An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 unlocated Mephyla"
609,416839,Orouinion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: 230 stades from Reate An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 unlocated Orouinion"
610,416842,Palation,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Reate An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 unlocated Palation"
611,416845,Pollition,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: town among Marrucini Mentioned only by Diodorus, Pollition is described as ""a city of the Marroukinoi"" against which Rome conducted military operations ca. 312 BCE. The Marrucini were an Italic tribe centered around Teate in the Abruzzo, but the precise location of Poliltion cannot be determined today."
612,416861,Souna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Reate An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 unlocated Souna"
613,416864,Syessola,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Reate An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 42 unlocated Syessola"
614,416872,Tribola,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Trebula Suffenas? According to Dionysius of Halicarnassus, Tribola (or Tribula) is an early city in central Italy, now unlocated. Dionysius claims it was some sixty stades from Reate."
615,416880,Veregra/Beregra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: inland town in Picenum Veregra/Beregra was an ancient settlement of Picenum that is attested in the texts of Pliny and Ptolemy, although its precise location cannot be determined today."
616,416883,Via Quinctia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: road from Reate This instance of a road named ""Via Quinctia"" is included as an unlocated place on map 42 of the Barrington Atlas. This road is mentioned only by Dionysius of Halicarnassus in his discussion of roads in the territory of Reate. This road is distinct from the homonymous road located in northern Etruria."
617,422813,Ad Pictas,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Le Macere Ad Pictas is a road station east of Rome, known mostly for its role in Sulla's march on Rome in 88 B.C. The precise location of the statio continues to be debated."
618,422822,Aequi,The Aequi were an ancient Italic tribe of northeast Latium in the first millennium BC.
619,422824,Alba Longa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Castel Gandolfo Alba Longa, located in the Alban Hills southeast of Rome, was the chief city and founder of the Latin League. Conflict with Rome caused Alba's destruction in the seventh century B.C. The legendary history of Romulus and Remus connects them to the royal lineage of the city. The ancient city is probably to be located at and around the site of modern Castel Gandolfo."
620,422826,Albanus M.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Cavo The second-highest summit of the Alban Hills, Mons Albanus, is an extinct volcano with an elevation of 950 m (3,117 ft). In the middle of the first millennium B.C., there may have been volcanic activity, as noted by the historian Livy. The mountain was sacred for Latin peoples who held important festivals there, and a major shrine to Iuppiter Latiaris stands on the mountain top."
621,422829,Algidus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Colle della Mola An ancient settlement of Latium located in the territory of the Aequi.
622,422831,Alsium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palo Alsium served as a port for Caere, together with Fregenae. A Roman colony was established ca. 245 BCE. The Antonine Itinerary places it 18 Roman miles from Portus at the mouth of the Tiber."
623,422833,Anagnia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Anagni Anagnia was a Hernican town that sided with Rome against the Volscians and later became civitas sine suffragio. By Cicero's time it received the status of municipium.
624,422834,Anio (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aniene The Anio river (modern Aniene) is a river of northern Latium.
625,422836,Antemnae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Antenne Antemnae is an ancient Latin settlement located north of Rome near the point where the Anio and Tiber rivers intersect. Connected with Rome's early history, Antemnae was involved in episodes such as the 'Rape of the Sabine Women' and Romulus is said to have celebrated a triumph over the Antemnates in 752 BC."
626,422837,Antium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Anzio. According to Tacitus ( Ann. 15.39 ), this was the location where Nero was during the Great Fire of Rome (64 AD). Antium ranked as one of the most important and most powerful centers of Latium and was captured by Rome at the close of the Latin War in the fourth century B.C. Imperial Antium was well known for its number of coastal villas."
627,422840,Aquae Albulae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Acque Albule (lakes) An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 43 C2 Aquae Albulae"
628,422843,Ardea,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ardea Ardea was an ancient settlement of the Rutuli in Latium.
629,422844,Aricia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ariccia Aricia was an ancient Latin settlement and a member of the Latin League.
630,422846,Astura (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Astura The Astura is a river of Latium that flows from the Alban Mount to the Tyrrhenian Sea.
631,422859,Caere,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cerveteri Caere (also known as Cisra or Cerveteri) was a major Etruscan polis located ca. 50 km north-northwest of Rome in southern Etruria.
632,422898,Collatia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lunghezza. Strabo suggests the city is located 30 stades from Rome. Collatia was an ancient city of Latium located between Gabii and the Anio River. A colony of Alba Longa, Collatia was one of the most ancient cities of Latium. Subjugated by Rome under the Tarquins, Collatia would decline during the Republican period and numbers among the lost Latin cities by the accounting of Pliny the Elder."
633,422909,Cora,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cori A Latin center in the Monti Lepini that was already a Roman municipium by 211 B.C.
634,422912,Cremera (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Valchetta The Cremera is a small stream in Lazio that played a role in the fourth century BC war between Rome and the Etruscan city-state of Veii.
635,422917,Temple of Diana at Nemi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Nemi A site sacred to Diana near Lake Nemi, comprised of a Tuscan-style sanctuary and a sacred grove."
636,422923,Fidenae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Borgata Fidene Fidenae was an ancient city of Latium.
637,422930,Fregenae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Fregene An ancient town of Etruria, Fregenae was located between Alsium and the mouth of the river Tiber."
638,422944,Hernici,The Hernici (Hernicans) were an Italic tribe of first millennium B.C. Latium. Their territory was bounded by the Trerus fl. (Sacco) and the Lacus Fucinus (Fucine Lake).
639,422952,Labicum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Colonna Originally a member of the Latin League, the city of Labicum was taken by the Romans ca. 418 B.C. By the time of Augustus, the site was almost certainly deserted and its precise location remains debated today."
640,422959,Laurentes,"Laurentum was an ancient city of Latium located some 16 miles from Rome, between Ostia and Lavinium. Its precise location can no longer be determined. In Rome's legendary history Laurentum is the site of king Latinus' dwelling."
641,422987,Norba,"Norba is an ancient settlement of Latium Adiectum located close to the modern town of Norma, Italy, in the Monti Lepini , also known as the Volscian Mountains. The ancient site occupies an impressive natural promontory that rises to over 1,500 feet above sea level and affords great visibility. Already a member of the Latin Leagues from 499 B.C., the site became a Latin colonia in 492 B.C., according to the historian Livy. The end of the ancient site came in 82 B.C. during the Sullan crisis when the city was laid waste by means of fire after having taken sides against Sulla. It would seem that the ruined city was not subsequently re-occupied in antiquity. The archaeological excavation of the site began in the early twentieth century with the work of L. Savignoni and R. Mengarelli (1901) (see reports in Notizie degli Scavi 1901 and 1904). Important aerial documentation of the site was carried out by Giulio Schmiedt for the Atlante aereofotografico delle sedi umane in Italia . In the twentieth and twenty-first centuries, the site has been further excavated, with extensive work on areas adjacent to the forum and private domus in the vicinity of the minor acropolis. These excavations have been directed by Stefania Quilici-Gigli of the University of Naples. The site is an important urban center for the study of colonial sites in the Republican period, particularly the middle republic. Among the most notable archaeological remains visible at the site are those of the polygonal masonry fortification walls that enclose an area of more than 38 hectares. Norba, located in the Monti Lepini, was a member of the Latin League (ca. 499 B.C.) and became a Latin colony ca. 492 B.C. The city was destroyed in the early first century B.C."
642,422995,Ostia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ostia antica The port city of ancient Rome located at the mouth of the river Tiber.
643,423013,Praeneste,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palestrina An ancient city of central Italy, the eighth and seventh century B.C. origins of which demonstrate high-level trade contact with the Eastern Mediterranean world. The city withdrew from the Latin League (499 B.C.) and opposed Rome in the Latin war; Cincinnatus eventually subdued Praeneste. Sulla founded a new colony there in the first century B.C."
644,423025,Roma,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Roma/Rome. The capital of the Roman Republic and Empire.
645,423028,Sabina,The one-time territory of the ancient Sabine people of central Italy.
646,423068,Setia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sezze A Volscian settlement of the fifth century B.C. that became a Latin colony in 382 B.C.
647,423072,Signia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Segni A city of Latium Adjectum established as a Latin colony ca. 494 B.C.
648,423080,Tiberis (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tevere/Tiber The Tiber River is the third-longest river of the Italian peninsula, flowing 406 km (252 mi) from the Apennine Mountains to the Tyrrhenian Sea."
649,423081,Tibur,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tivoli A Sabine town, 30 km east-north-east of Rome, Tibur was the seat of the Tiburtine Sibyl."
650,423103,Trebula Suffenas,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Passo della Fortuna An ancient Sabine city.
651,423108,Tusculum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tuscolo Tusculum was a Latin city of the Alban Hills in central Italy. After 381 B.C., Tusculum became the first ""municipium cum suffragio""."
652,423115,Varia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vicovaro Varia (modern Vicovaro) was a settlement of the Sabines.
653,423116,Veii,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Veio An important city of South Etruria located north-northwest of Rome, Veii was eventually sacked by the Romans in 396 B.C. The ancient city covered some 180 ha."
654,423117,Velitrae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Velletri Velitrae (modern Velletri) was an ancient Volscian settlement. In the Roman Republican period, Velitrae was attacked by Marcus Furius Camillus (379 B.C.) and again by Cincinnatus (377 B.C.); the Romans controlled the site by 365 B.C. The gens Octavia hailed from the city and a youthful Octavian spent time there."
655,426618,Mugilla,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: settlement (Mugilla?). Mugilla was a settlement in Latium Vetus. Dionysius of Halicarnassus mentions it as one of seven Latin cities sacked by Coriolanus. Its location is unknown and its obscurity is further highlighted by the fact that Pliny the Elder does not include it in his catalog of vanished Latin peoples and places. The cognomen of a branch of the gens Papiria - Mugillanus - is thought to derive from the name of the settlement. Lucius Papirius Mugillanus held the consulship at Rome in 427 BCE.
656,432617,Abella,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Avella Abella was an ancient settlement of Campania of Samnite origin.
657,432618,Abellinum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: La Cività, near Atripalda Abellinum was a settlement of the Hirpini in Campania."
658,432622,Acherusia Palus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lago Fusaro Acherusia Palus, now known as Lago del Fusaro or Lake Fusaro, is a lake located in the Italian community of Bacoli. The acropolis of Cumae is about 1.5 km from the lakeshore. The lake itself is separated from the sea by a narrow strip of land. The lake has also been noted since antiquity for the presence of freshwater springs and the quality of its oysters and mussels."
659,432648,Aenaria/Pithekoussai (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ischia. The acropolis of Monte Vico has Bronze Age settlement remains and Euboean Greeks established a colonial settlement on the island in the eighth century BC. An inscribed cup (""Nestor's Cup"" ca. 730 BC) was discovered on the island in 1953 and represents important early evidence for the Greek alphabet. Aenaria/Pithekoussai Ins. (modern Ischia) is a volcanic island located in the northern part of the Bay of Naples."
660,432658,Al(l)ifae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Angelo d’Alife An ancient town of either Oscan or Samnite origin situated in the Volturno river valley.
661,432664,Aletrium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Alatri A Hernican hill town that formed part of a sixth-century B.C. defensive league against Volscian and Samnite aggression and allied itself to Tarquinius Superbus. Rome eventually took Aletrium in 306 B.C.; by the first century B.C., the town was a Roman municipium."
662,432695,Aquinum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aquino Aquinum was a municipium by the time of Cicero. It was thought to be the place of birth for the satirist Juvenal and the emperor Pescennius Niger.
663,432700,Arpinum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arpino An ancient center that originated as early as the seventh century B.C., which became a civitas sine suffragio in 305 B.C. Arpinum was the birthplace of Caius Marius and Marcus Tullius Cicero."
664,432708,Atina,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Atina Atina was a Volscian settlement located at the source of the river Melpis. Atina was located on the via Latina between Casinum and Sora.
665,432709,Aufidena,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Castel di Sangro A probable Samnite center, the Romans captured Aufidena in 298 BC. It only became a municipium after 49 B.C."
666,432712,Avernus (lake),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lago Averno A volcanic crater lake located in the Phlegraean Fields, Lake Avernus served a major ritual role for ancient Romans and Greeks as they viewed it as an entrance to Hades."
667,432721,Beneventum/Maleventum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Benevento Beneventum/Maleventum (modern Benevento) was an ancient city of Campania of Samnite origin.
668,432727,Buca,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Termoli? Buca was an ancient settlement of the Frentani.
669,432729,Caecubus Ager,"A tract of Latium that bordered the Gulf of Amyclae, lying in the territory of Fundi."
670,432734,Caieta,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gaeta Caieta was a town of ancient Latium located on the Tyrrhenian Sea coast between Tarracina and Formiae.
671,432739,Cales (Campania),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Calvi Cales was an ancient city of Campania of Ausonian origins. The Romans captured the town in 335 B.C. and established a colony there.
672,432742,Campania,Campania was a region of southern Italy.
673,432744,Campi Phlegraei,"Surviving Hellenistic Greek sources treat the Phlegrean Fields as a large area, roughly bounded by Capua, Nola, and Mount Vesuvius. Writers of the Roman period seem to have in mind a smaller area, lying between Cumae and Puteoli (Gargini provides citations in Brill's New Pauly ). Modern vulcanologists refer to the Campi Flegrei volcano, understanding its location, extent, and history from a scientific perspective that both differs from and contextualizes the ancient perspectives (see Costa 2021, pages 4-8 and map in Figure 3). The Campi Phlegraei (Phlegraean Fields) is a large caldera (13 km wide) in the Bay of Naples region of Italy. In total, the the area comprises 24 craters, although a good portion of the caldera now lies under water. The area was important in Greek and Roman times; the Greeks established their first mainland colony at Cumae within the Campi Phlegraei."
674,432754,Capua,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Maria Capua Vetere An important ancient city of Campania with Villanovan and Etruscan origins.
675,432763,Casilinum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Capua A settlement located in the border region between the ager Falernus and the ager Campanus, north of Capua."
676,432776,Cereatae Marianae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Abbazia di Casamari Known as the birthplace of Caius Marius Cereatae Marianae, purported to have been the birthplace of Caius Marius, is thought to have been located where the 13th-century abbey church of Casamari now stands (in the modern province of Frosinone, Lazio, Italy)."
677,432781,Kirkaion Akron/Veneris Pr.,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Circeo Kirkaion Akron/Veneris Pr. (Monte Circeo) is a mountainous promontory on the Tyrrhenian coast of Italy.
678,432786,Clanius (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Regi Lagni A river of Campania that likely flowed into the Tyrrhenian Sea near Liternum.
679,432808,Cumae/Kyme (Campanian),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cuma An ancient settlement of Campania, Cumae was the first Greek colony established on the Italian mainland in the eighth century BC."
680,432815,Dikaiarcheia/Puteoli,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pozzuoli A maritime city of Campania situated on the Phlegrean Peninsula. The settlement started as a Greek colony and became a Roman colony under the name Puteoli in 194 B.C.
681,432826,Fabrateria Vetus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ceccano Fabrateria Vetus was a Volscian settlement that surrendered to Rome in 424 B.C.
682,432839,Formiae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Formia An ancient settlement of likely Greek origin along the Via Appia. Cicero was assassinated near Formiae (now Formia) in 43 B.C.
683,432843,Forum Popillii,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Carinola An important town of the Ager Falernus, not to be confused with Forum Popili, the modern Polla."
684,432846,Fregellae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Ceprano Fregellae was a city of Latium Adiectum that became a Roman colony in 328 B.C., but was subsequently captured by the Samnites; a Roman presence was re-established in 313 B.C. Loyal to Rome during the Second Punic War, Fregellae revolted in 125 B.C., and the site was razed."
685,432849,Frentani,"An Italic people of eastern Italy occupying a territory between the Apennines and the Adriatic seaboard, the Frentani had a close cultural connection with the Samnites."
686,432852,Frusino,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Frosinone Frusino (modern Frosinone) was a town in Latium along the Via Latina that was originally a Volscian settlement.
687,432853,Fucinus (lake),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lago Fucino A lake of ancient Italy, the site of a major battle of 89 BC. Both Claudius and Hadrian attempted flood and drainage controls in antiquity. The Torlonia family drained the lake in the nineteenth century."
688,432855,Fundi,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Fondi Fundi achieved civitas sine suffragio after the Latin War in 338 B.C.
689,432859,Gallinaria Silva,An evergreen thicket of the area around Cumae.
690,432873,Herculaneum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Resina An ancient Roman town of Campania destroyed by the eruption of Mt. Vesuvius in A.D. 79. Named as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1997.
691,432884,Interamna Lirenas,A Latin colony founded ca. 312 B.C. along the Via Latina. The site was sacked by Samnites (ca. 294 B.C.) and subsequently by Hannibal (212 B.C.). It received Roman municipal status in 90 B.C.
692,432910,Liris/Clanis (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Garigliano A major river of central Italy, originating in the Colles Simbruini and flowing into the Amyclanus Sinus just south of Minturnae. The modern Garigliano."
693,432911,Liternum,"An ancient city of Campania located near the mouth of the Volturnus River; a Roman colony was established at Liternum in 194 B.C. The site is most famous as the location of the villa of Scipio Africanus, as described by Seneca."
694,432915,Lucrinus (lake),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lago Lucrino A small lake near Lake Avernus, the modern Lucrino in Italy."
695,432927,Marruvium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Benedetto Ancient Marruvium was the principal city of the Italic tribe of the Marsi.
696,432935,Melpis (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Melfa The Melfa River flows through Lazio, originating from the Monti della Meta and joining the Liri River near San Giovanni Incarico."
697,432938,Minervae (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Punta della Campanella The Minervae promunturium is identified by modern scholars with the Punta Campanella, which lies at the western extremity of the Sorrentine Peninsula."
698,432940,Minturnae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Minturno Originally an Ausonian town that fought against Rome in 314 B.C., Minturnae became a colony in 296 B.C. The site is located on the Garigliano river (ancient Liris) and served an important economic function. The Via Appia passes through the city."
699,432941,Misenum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Miseno An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 44 F4 Misenum"
700,432981,Nola,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nola The city of Nola (Nuvlana) is considered one of the oldest in Campania. Under Augustus, the settlement became a Roman colony; it was the spot where Augustus died in A.D. 14."
701,432985,Nuceria (Alfaterna),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nocera Nuceria (Alfaterna) (modern Nocera Inferiore) is an ancient settlement in Campania. In pre-Roman times, this was the chief settlement of the Sarnus river valley."
702,433011,Pandataria (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ventotene An island in the Pontine archipelago of the Tyrrhenian Sea, Pandataria is volcanic in origin and is best known, in historical terms, as the place to which Augustus banished Julia the Elder, his daughter, in 2 BC and to which Tiberius banished Agrippina the Elder in AD 29."
703,433014,Parthenope/Neapolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Napoli/Naples Founded as Parthenope by Greeks in the eighth century B.C., the site eventually gave rise to Neapolis in the sixth century B.C. and was one of the most prominent cities of Magna Graecia. Present-day Naples, Italy."
704,433032,Pompeii,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pompei Scavi An ancient city of Campania destroyed by the volcanic eruption of Mt. Vesuvius in A.D. 79, Pompeii was named as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1997."
705,433041,Pontia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ponza A settlement on Pontia Ins. The largest of the Pontine Islands, Pontia lies 33 km south of Cape Circeo on the Italian mainland."
706,433052,Privernum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Madonna di Mezzagosto, near Priverno A Volscian center that Rome reportedly destroyed in the late fourth century B.C."
707,433053,Prochyta (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Procida Procida is one of the Flegrean Islands located between Cape Miseno and the island of Ischia.
708,433059,Puteolanus Sinus/Crater,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Golfo di Napoli Also called the Bay of Naples, the Puteolanus Sinus is a gulf located on the southwestern coast of Italy. It opens west to the Mediterranean Sea and is bordered on the east by Mt. Vesuvius and the south by the Sorrentine Peninsula."
709,433061,Pyrgi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Severa Pyrgi is an ancient Etruscan port city on the Tyrrhenian coast of Italy. A Roman colony was established at the site by 191 B.C. In 1964, the bilingual 'Pyrgi Plaques' were discovered during the course of excavations."
710,433075,Salernum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Salerno A city of Campania with Osco-Etruscan origins that became a Roman colony in 197 B.C.
711,433102,Sarnus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sarno The modern Sarno River; its course was much altered in antiquity by the eruption of Vesuvius in 79 CE and in later times by both natural and man-made changes.
712,433122,Sinues(s)a/Sinope,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Torre S. Limato An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 44 E3 Sinues(s)a/Sinope"
713,433124,Sirenes/Seirenoussai (islands),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: island-group Sirenes/Seirenoussai Inss. (modern Le Sirenuse or Li Galli) is an archipelago off the Amalfi Coast of Italy. In Greek mythology, the islands were home to the Sirens who tempted sailors, including Odysseus."
714,433126,Sora,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sora An ancient city of Latium located in the valley of the Liris River that likely had Volscian origins. The Romans captured the city in 345 BCE and later established a colony there in 303 BCE after the Second Samnite War.
715,433133,Suessula,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Cancello Suessula was an important ancient city of Campania.
716,433134,Sulmo,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sulmona. The Latin poet Publius Ovidius Naso was born in Sulmo on 20 March 43 BC. A chief settlement of the Peligni that was ravaged by the Carthaginians during the Second Punic War.
717,433139,Surrentum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sorrento An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 44 F4 Surrentum"
718,433146,Teanum Sidicinum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Teano Teanum Sidicinum (modern Teano) was the capital of the Oscan-speaking Sidicini tribe. The city likely submitted to Rome after 334 B.C.
719,433147,Telesia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Salvatore Telesino An ancient Samnite city located in the valley of the Calor River.
720,433153,Tifata (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monti di Maddaloni A mountainous region of central Italy known to be densely wooded with oak trees.
721,433154,Tifernus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Biferno The Tifernus (modern Biferno) is a river of Molise that flows from near Bojano to the Adriatic Sea.
722,433185,Venafrum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Venafro Venafrum was an ancient town of Campania.
723,433189,Vesuvius (volcano),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Vesuvio A stratovolcano located in the Gulf of Naples, Vesuvius stands to an elevation of 1,281 m (4,203 ft). It is notable in historical terms for its eruption of AD 79 in which numerous settlements, including Pompeii and Herculaneum, were destroyed."
724,433199,Vietri,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vietri Modern Vietri sul mare is the likely site of ancient Marcina, an Etruscan settlement in the ager Picentinus located 120 stades from Pompeii. It later came to be inhabited by Samnites."
725,433208,Volsci,"The Volsci (Volscians) were an Italic tribe of central Italy during the first millennium B.C. They inhabited parts of southern Latium and were bounded by other tribes, including the Aurunci, Samnites, and Hernici."
726,433210,Volturnum,Volturnum was located at the mouth of the Volturno River.
727,433211,Volturnus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Volturno The Volturno River flows 175 km from the Apennines to the Tyrrhenian Sea at Castel Volturno.
728,438677,Apiolae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: SE Rome An ancient city of Latium that led the Latin resistance against Tarquinius Priscus.
729,438698,Bubetani,The Bubetani number among the 53 peoples of Latium Vetus that Pliny the Elder records as having perished without leaving a trace (interiere sine vestigiis).
730,438703,Caenina,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: N Rome Caenina was an ancient town of Latium.
731,438704,Cameria,Tarquinius Priscus captured Cameria and it was destroyed by Opiter Verginius Tricostus in 502 B.C. Tacitus links the site with the gens Coruncania. An ancient city of Latium that is noted by Pliny the Elder as one of the lost cities of central Italy.
732,438705,Capitulum Hernicum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Anagnia A Hernican town that still existed in Pliny's time, Capitulum Hernicum received colonists under both Sulla and Iulius Caesar. An inscription found in the area of Piglio mentions the town, but its precise location is uncertain."
733,438707,Carventum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Algidus M. (Map 43) Carventum was an ancient city of Latium, listed by Dionysius as one of the thirty states of the Latin League."
734,438722,Corbio (Latium),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Algidus M. Corbio was an ancient city of Latium located on the northeastern flank of the Alban Hills, although its precise location is no longer secure. This Latin city is said to have fallen to the Aequi and then been the subject of contention between the Aequi and the Romans, ultimately recaptured by the latter after Cincinnatus' victory at Mons Algidus in 458 B.C."
735,438723,Corioli,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Antium and Aricia Corioli was an ancient city of Latium connected in the literary/historical tradition with C. Marcius Coriolanus. The location of Corioli is no longer known.
736,438730,Ecetra,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: not La Cività d’Artena Ecetra was a Volscian settlement that in the archaic period is identified as being in conflict with Rome. It subsequently disappears from the sources.
737,438746,Gerounion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Gerione? Polybius describes Gerounion, a place in Apulia, as being ""about two hundred stades from Luceria""."
738,438760,Kalene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Larinum; Sicalenum? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 44 unlocated Kalene"
739,438775,Longula,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Ardea and Antium Longula was an early settlement of Latium Vetus, although its location is no longer known. A Volscian city, Longula was first taken by the Romans in 493 B.C. under the consul Postumus Cominius. It subsequently changes hands several times during the fifth and fourth centuries B.C. It likely lay between Ardea and Antium."
740,438788,Mugilla,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: E Rome Mugilla was an ancient oppidum of Latium, located at Corioli. The gens Papiria is associated with the site. Its precise location is now unknown."
741,438797,Opikoi,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: people An ancient people whose territory is unlocated.
742,438799,Ouerestis (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Praeneste A river in Latium near Praeneste that is now unlocated.
743,438802,Panna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Samnium Now unlocated, Panna was an ancient place in Samnium mentioned only by Strabo."
744,438805,Piketia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Fidenae An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 44 unlocated Piketia"
745,438810,Plistia,"A town of Samnium located near Saticula, perhaps at modern Prestia."
746,438811,Politorium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S Rome An ancient town of Latium located near Castel di Decima. Tradition holds that Ancus Marcius captured the settlement and relocated its occupants to the Aventine Hill in Rome.
747,438813,Pollusca,"The ancient city of Polusca is known only from the textual sources which report that, along with Longula, it was founded by Volscians from Antium and that it numbered among the earliest cities of Latium. Pollusca was an early city of Latium Vetus connected with Longula and Corioli. Coriolanus captured these three cities in 488 BC. The location of Pollusca is no longer known."
748,438838,Solonium/Solonius Ager,"The Ager Solonius is an unlocated ager in Latium, located between Ostia, Ardea, and Lanuvium."
749,438850,Tellena,Tellena was a prominent city of ancient Latium that factors in the early history of the city of Rome. Its location is no longer certain.
750,438853,Tolerium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near source of Trerus fl. Tolerium was an ancient city that was a part of the Latin League. Antonio Nibby supported associating its location with Valmontone.
751,438854,Touxion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Samnite city Touxion was a Samnite metropolis that is referred to in several obscure texts. It is linked to the career of Fabius Fabricianus, a kinsman of Fabius Maximus, who is said to have sacked the city and carried off to Rome as plunder the statue of Aphrodite Nikephoros (a.k.a. Venus Victrix). The location of Touxion is uncertain. Torelli speculates that it may have been conquered in the campaigns of 292-1 B.C. along with Cominium and Venusium. Smith notes the Oresteia-like parallels in this narrative."
752,438870,Venusia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: possibly Castelvenere near Telesia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 44 unlocated *Venusia"
753,438872,Verrugo,Verrugo was a Volscian settlement at the borders of Latium. Its location is now uncertain.
754,439420,Koukoulon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: ms. error for Gr. ‘*Aikouikoulon’: see Aequiculi An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 44 false name ‘Koukoulon’"
755,442432,Acalander (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cavone Acalander fl.. a river of Lucania emptying into the Gulf of Tarentum.
756,442476,Argos Hippium/Arpi/Argyripa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Arpinova An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 45 C1 Argos Hippium/Arpi/Argyripa"
757,442485,Aufidus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ofanto The Aufidus is the longest of the rivers in Apulia.
758,442500,Barium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bari A city of the Peucetii that came under Roman control in the third century BCE.
759,442509,Brundisium/Brentesion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Brindisi A Greek and Roman port, terminus of the Via Appia."
760,442516,Caelia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ceglie Messapica Caelia (Ceglie Messapica) was a Messapic site of south Italy that received Greek colonists after 700 B.C.
761,442525,Canusium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Canosa di Puglia A center of the ancient Daunians, Canusium became allied to Rome in 318 B.C. It received municipal status in 88 B.C."
762,442536,Castrum Minervae/Athenaion/Limen Aphrodites,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Castro An ancient settlement of the Sallentini in Apulia. Legendary history held that this was the spot where Aeneas first landed in Italy, thus the assignation 'Limen Aphrodites' or 'Portus Veneris'."
763,442564,Daunii,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 45 B2 Daunii"
764,442593,Garganou Kolpos,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 45 B1 Garganou Kolpos"
765,442603,Grumentum,"The earliest Roman settlement belongs to the third century BC. Hanno was defeated at Grumentum in 215 BC and in 207 BC Hannibal made the town his headquarters. Grumentum was an ancient settlement of Lucania, likely inhabited since the sixth century B.C. The site played a role in the Second Punic War and later became a bishopric in the fourth century A.D."
766,442610,Temple of Hera at Foce del Sele,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Foce del Sele The sanctuary was constructed around the end of the sixth century BC. An earthquake destroyed the sanctuary in AD 63 and the AD 79 eruption of Vesuvius further obscured the site. A sanctuary at the mouth of the Sele river in Italy with an octastyle temple dedicated to the Argive Hera.
767,442613,Herdoniae/Ardaneae/Civitas Serdonis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ordona Herdonia was an ancient city of Apulia located along the via Minucia. The city was the site of two major engagements of the Second Punic War (212 and 210 BCE, respectively), both of which were Carthaginian victories. The city's extensive remains have been investigated by the Belgian Institute since the Second World War."
768,442614,Hirpini,The Hirpini were an ancient Samnite people of central Italy.
769,442615,Hydruntum/Hydrous,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Otranto A Greek city at the easternmost point of the Italian peninsula that sided against Rome in both the Pyrrhic and Hannibalic wars. In the time of the Roman Empire the city was located in the province of Calabria and was an embarkation point for ships leaving for Apollonia across the Adriatic Sea.
770,442618,Iapyges,"The Iapyges were a pre-Roman, Indo-European people that inhabited Apulia in southeastern Italy."
771,442621,Ionios Kolpos/Tarentinus Sinus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gulf of Taranto Ionios Kolpos/Tarentinus Sinus (Gulf of Taranto) is a gulf of the Ionian Sea.
772,442639,Lucania (region),An ancient region of south Italy stretching from the Tyrrhenian Sea to the Gulf of Taranto.
773,442640,Luceria/Noukeria (Apoulon),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lucera Luceria/Noukeria (Apoulon) was originally a Daunian city. It was designated a Colonia Togata in 320 B.C. and went on to play a significant role in the Second Punic War. Constans II sacked the town in A.D. 663.
774,442642,Lupia(e),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lecce A settlement of the indigenous Messapii that was refounded under Roman influence in the third century B.C.
775,442644,Manduria,"Manduria was a key Messapic center. Archidamus III, king of Sparta, fell there in 338 B.C. while leading the army of Taras."
776,442658,Metapontum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Metaponto An important city of Magna Graecia, Metapontum was located on the Gulf of Tarentum. The Achaeans were responsible for its foundation, perhaps as early as the eighth century B.C."
777,442693,Numistro?,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Raia S. Basile New Pauly asserts that Numistro and the modern day Muro Lucano are one and the same. A town in Lucania that was the location of a military engagement between Hannibal and Marcus Claudius Marcellus in 210 B.C.
778,442704,Paestanus Sinus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gulf of Salerno A gulf of the Tyrrhenian Sea.
779,442714,Peucetii,The Peucetii were an ancient tribe of Apulia in southern Italy.
780,442733,Poseidonia/Paestum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Paestum Greeks from Sybaris originally founded Poseidonia/Paestum, a major Greco-Roman center of south Italy, in the seventh-century BCE. It was originally named Poseidonia. After the Pyrrhic war, the city became a Latin colony named Paestum ca. 273 BCE."
781,442753,Rudiae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rugge An ancient Messapic town, Rudiae was the birthplace of the poet Ennius."
782,442758,Sal(a)pia(i)/Elpia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lupara An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 45 C2 Sal(a)pia(i)/Elpia"
783,442763,Sallentinum/Iapygia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Capo S. Maria di Leuca Cape Leuca, known as Sallentinum and Iapygia in ancient sources, is a headland that forms the extreme southeastern point of Italy and separates the Gulf of Tarentum from the Adriatic Sea."
784,442765,Samnites,"An ancient, Oscan-speaking people who lived in the central southern part of the Apennine mountains in Italy."
785,442789,Satyrion/Saturum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Saturo, Porto Perone An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 45 F4 Satyrion/Saturum"
786,442795,Silarus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sele The Sele river.
787,442796,Silvium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Botromagno/Gravina di Puglia Silvium (modern Gravina in Puglia) was founded as a Greek colony. It was a town on the frontier of the Peucetii.
788,442797,Sipous/Sipontum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Siponto Sipous/Sipontum was a Greek colony that fell first under Samnite control, then was later taken by Alexander of Epirus. The site became a Roman colony in 189 BC. Legend ascribed its foundation to the hero Diomedes."
789,442810,Taras/Tarentum/Neptunia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Taranto A Greek colonial city founded in 706 B.C.
790,442815,Teanum Apulum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near S. Paolo di Civitate A town in ancient Apulia located between Larinum and and Sipontum. Rome took control of the site in 318 BC, granting it municipal status."
791,442839,Uria,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Oria An ancient city of the Messapii.
792,442841,Uzentum/Ouxenton,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ugento Uzentum/Ouxenton (Ugento) was an ancient city in the territory of the Salentini with both Messapic and Roman phases.
793,446256,Kalasarna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in interior of Lucania Now unlocated, Strabo describes Kalasarna as a place of interior Lucania. This description comes in the context of Strabo's discussion of the activities of both Philoctetes and Aegestes the Trojan."
794,446258,Katarakta,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in Apulia Now unlocated, Katarakta was a place in Apulia. The Romans reportedly captured it during the Second Samnite War in 311 BCE."
795,446259,Keraunilia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: possibly between Herdoniae and Canusium Keraunilia was a Samnite town captured by the Romans in 311 B.C.
796,446273,Netion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Via Minucia between Caelia and Canusium An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 45 unlocated Netion"
797,446275,Oenotrides (islands),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: lost islands near Velia; now submerged Strabo identifies these islands as lying off the territory of Velia and providing good anchorage.
798,446277,Ouertinai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: interior of Lucania An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 45 unlocated Ouertinai"
799,452250,Ad Columnam,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Reggio di Calabria A Roman road station on the Calabrian coast of the Messenian Strait, located to the north of Regium (modern Reggio di Calabria)."
800,452274,Blanda,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palecastro di Tortora Blanda was an ancient city of Lucania, although Pliny the Elder and Ptolemy differ as to its placement; the former places it near the coast, the latter inland. Modern scholars are in agreement that Ptolemy was incorrect. It is to be identified with the significant archaeological site at Palecastro di Tortora in modern Italy."
801,452275,Bruttii,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Calabria An ancient Italic people of extreme southern Italy, the Bruttii inhabited an area that roughly corresponds to the modern Calabria."
802,452304,Clampetia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Amantea An ancient city of Bruttium, located on the coast of the Tyrrhenian Sea, between Blanda and Temesa."
803,452308,Consentia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cosenza The capital city of the ancient Bruttii in Calabria.
804,452317,Croto(n),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Crotone An Achaean colony in Magna Graecia established in 710 BC by the oikist Myscellus from the city of Rhypes in the Peloponnese.
805,452323,Emporion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: port of Medma An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 C5 Emporion"
806,452324,Euonymus (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Panarea One of the Aeolian Islands with occupation evidence beginning ca. 1200 B.C.
807,452331,Halex? (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Melito or Galati An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 C5 Halex? fl."
808,452333,Heraclea,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Policoro Heraclea was an ancient city of Magna Graecia.
809,452337,Hipponion/Vibo Valentia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vibo Valentia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 D4 Hipponion/Vibo Valentia"
810,452346,Italia (region),That portion of the Toe of Italy lying to the south of the Terina/Scylletium isthmus as known to the Greeks in the 5th century BCE. Thus called after the Itali who inhabited it at that time.
811,452350,Kainys (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Punta Pezzo/Torre del Cavallo Modern Punta del Pezzo on the Calabrian coast of Italy, opposite Sicily and north of Rhegion. According to Strabo, the promontory was located near the Scyllaean rock, opposite the Pelorus promontory in Sicily."
812,452352,Kaulonia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monasterace Marina Kaulonia is an Achaean colony of Magna Graecia founded in the eighth or seventh century BC.
813,452358,Lametikos Kolpos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gulf of S. Eufemia The name ""Lametikos Kolpos"" is given by Aristotle in a passage of the Politics to the body of water into which the river Lametus (modern Amato in Calabria, Italy) flows, i.e., the modern-day Gulf of Saint Euphemia (Italian: Golfo di Santa Eufemia or Golfo di Sant'Eufemia). Other ancient sources provide several other names for the same modern gulf, but BAtlas treated this one as separate, placing the label for ""Lametikos Kolpos"" in the north in the immediate vicinity of the river mouth and the other primary label (Vibonensis Sinus) more centrally in the southern half."
814,452361,Laos/Lavinium?,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marcellina Founded as a colony by survivors of Sybaris after the destruction of the city in 510 BC.
815,452362,Laos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lao The Laos River of southern Italy formed what ancient geographers reckoned as the boundary between Lucania and Bruttium. The river today flows some 51 km from its source near the Serra del Prete mountain to the Tyrrhenian Sea, into which it debouches near Scalea, Italy. The Argentino River, its main tributary, flows into it near Orsomarso."
816,452366,Leucopetra (cape),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Punta di Pellaro Leucopetra Pr. (Punta di Pellaro) in Calabria is the extreme southwestern point of mainland Italy.
817,452367,Leucosia (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vibo Marina In ancient times, the area of the modern Italian port town of Vibo Marina on the Gulf of Saint Euphemia was an island named Leucosia. Artificial port construction began in antiquity and, together with natural processes, has connected Leucosia to the mainland."
818,452371,Lucani,An ancient Oscan-speaking population of south Italy.
819,452375,Medma,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rosarno Medma (Rosarno) was an ancient settlement founded from Lokroi Epizephyrioi.
820,452383,Monte Coppolo,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Coppolo, ancient Lagaria? Monte Coppolo is an ancient settlement usually referred to in archaeological literature by the modern name of the mountain on which it is located in modern Italy's Basilicata region. It is perhaps the site of ancient Lagaria, referred to by Strabo as a fortress (Φρούριον)."
821,452395,Oenotri,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Calabria and Basilicata An ancient Italic population occupying a portion of southwestern Italy from Poseidonia/Paestum to Calabria. Their name may derive from the Greek 'oinos' meaning wine.
822,452403,Petelia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Strongoli Petelia was an ancient city on the coast of Bruttium in southern Italy. Petelia remained allied to Rome during the Second Punic War.
823,452411,Pyxous/Buxentum (settlement),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Policastro Bussentino An ancient coastal settlement of south Italy, modern Policastro Bussentino."
824,452416,Rhegion/Regium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Reggio di Calabria A site with settlement origins in the Bronze and Iron Ages, Rhegion was eventually colonized by Chalcis in either 743 or 730 BCE."
825,452424,Sabutus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Savuto A river of Bruttium: the modern Fiume Savuto in Italy.
826,452425,Sagra(s) (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Kaulonia and Lokroi Epizephyrioi, perhaps Allaro An ancient river, attested between Kaulonia and Lokroi Epizephyrioi. The Sagra(s) is perhaps to be identified with the modern Allaro."
827,452436,Scylla,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Scilla An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 C5 Scylla"
828,452448,Sikeloi,"Thucydides relates that the Sikels (or Sicels) who would settle the island of Sicily and impart their name thereto got their start in the Italian peninsula and would eventually migrate to Sicily via raft. Thucydides goes on to relate that Italy, in fact, takes its name from Italus, a Siceliote king."
829,452450,Siris,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Policoro Siris was an ancient Greek colonial city of Magna Graecia situated at the mouth of the Siris (modern Sinni) river. The city was founded by Ionian Greeks between 690 and 660 BC.
830,452452,Skidros,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sapri? Skidros or Scidrus was an ancient Greek city located on the Tyrrhenian coast of Lucania between Pyxus (Buxentum) and Laüs.
831,452455,Strongyle (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Stromboli. It is believed that Odysseus arrived in the Kingdom of Aeolus on the beach of San Vincenzo on the North-East side of Strongyle island (according to Cuisenier). One of the eight Aeolian Islands, Strongyle Ins. is one of the three active volcanoes in Italy."
832,452457,Sybaris/Thurii/Copia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sibari/Copia. Froelich Rainey (1969) offers a succinct summary of the settlement phases. Following the destruction of Sybaris ca. 510 BCE, there was a brief re-establishment of the city, but that, too, was razed. Athenians were encouraged to establish Thurii ""near"" the site of Sybaris in the fifth century BCE, a site famously planned by Hippodamos of Miletus. Lucanians then sacked Thurii ca. 390 BCE. The Roman Republic established a garrison at the site in 282 BCE. The ancient settlement lived until it was abandoned in the ninth century. Sybaris was an ancient city of Magna Graecia founded as an Achaean colony as early as 720 BCE on a plain that was drained by both the Crati and Sybaris rivers. Greeks from Kroton destroyed Sybaris ca. 510 BCE. Following this destruction, two successive cities, Thurii and Copia, respectively, were established on the same site. This topic is of particular interest to ancient authors, at least 70 of whom mention it."
833,452469,Temesa/Tempsa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nocera Tirinese/Piano della Tirena An ancient city located on the west coast of Bruttium, just north of the Gulf of Hipponium."
834,452470,Terina?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Eufemia Vetere An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 D4 Terina?"
835,452481,Traeis (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Trionto An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 E2 Traeis fl."
836,452488,Velia/Elea,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Castellamare di Velia The ancient city of Elea / Velia was said to have been founded by Phocaean Greeks in the sixth century B.C. and then became an ally of Rome in the third century B.C.
837,452489,Gulf of Saint Eufemia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gulf of S. Eufemia A number of ancient names were used to identify the modern Gulf of Saint Euphemia off the west Calabrian coast of Italy: ""Vibonensis sinus"", ""Hipponiates kolpos"", and ""Terinaeus sinus"" (as well as its Greek equivalent ""Terinaios kolpos""). A passage in Aristotle's Politics uses the unrelated Greek name ""Lametikos Kolpos"" for the same gulf, or at least its northern extent near the mouth of the ancient Lametus (modern Amato) river. The BAtlas treated this name as separate from the others in labeling the map, and the distinction has been maintained in the Pleiades gazetteer by using two separate (but connected) place resources."
838,456060,Elleporos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: R. Stilaro? An ancient river, mentioned by Polybius. It is possibly to be identified with the modern Stilaro in Calabria, Italy."
839,456082,Lagaria,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: probably Monte Coppolo An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 unlocated Lagaria"
840,456087,Lokanos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Lokroi Epizephyrioi and Scylletium, perhaps Stilaro An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 unlocated Lokanos fl."
841,456089,Lyka,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: spring at Temesa An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 unlocated Lyka"
842,456113,Petelina (mountains),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Petelia? An unlocated mountainous area to which Plutarch has Spartacus reposition before his forces' defeat of the Roman force commanded by Quintus and Scrophas. Its precise location is not known, but presumably lies in the vicinity of Petelia (modern Strongoli)."
843,456116,"Polites, T.","The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Heroon near Temesa A heroon located near Temesa that, according to Strabo, was dedicated to Polites, one of the companions of Odysseus."
844,456128,Terinaeus Sinus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: at Terina? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 46 unlocated Terinaeus Sinus"
845,462062,Abakainon,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Casale di Tripi A city of the Siculi on the northern coast of Sicily.
846,462067,Acium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Capo Molini An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 G3 Acium"
847,462068,Acrae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Palazzolo Acreide Akrai, an ancient city of Sicily, originated as a colony founded by Syracuse in 664 B.C., making it the first of the city's three colonial foundations."
848,462069,Acrillae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Chiaramonte Gulfi A Greek colony founded in Sicily ca. 598 B.C., Acrillae was an important economic center. In 213 B.C., the Roman consul M. Claudius Marcellus fought an engagement against the Syracusans there."
849,462074,Adranon,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Adrano Adranon was a settlement founded ca. 400 B.C. on the western slopes of mount Aetna.
850,462076,Aeoliae (islands),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Isole Eolie A volcanic archipelago in the Tyrrhenian Sea composed of eight islands.
851,462079,Agathyrnum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Capo d’Orlando (contra Manni 1981, 138-39) An ancient city of Siceliote origin on the north coast of Sicily."
852,462082,Agyrium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Agira An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 F3 Agyrium"
853,462084,Aigousa (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Favignana Aigousa Ins. - meaning 'goat island' - is an island west of Sicily that was the scene of a major naval engagement of the First Punic War.
854,462086,Akragas/Agrigentum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Agrigento Akragas/Agrigentum was an ancient city of Sicily and is now known as Agrigento. The Archaeological Area of Agrigento was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1997.
855,462092,Amenanus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Giudicello An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 G3 Amenanus fl."
856,462097,Apollonia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Fratello (contra Manni 1981, 145) An ancient settlement in the modern S. Fratello commune in northern Sicily."
857,462103,Asinius (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Alcantara An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 G3 Asinius fl."
858,462106,Assorus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Assoro Assorus was a settlement of the Siculi in interior Sicily that never received a Greek colony.
859,462113,Bidis?,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Poggio Bidini Bidis? was a fortress located near Syracuse.
860,462118,Brikinniai?,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte S. Basilio A fortified ancient settlement near modern Lentini. It has been associated with the area of Monte San Basilio.
861,462123,Calacte,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Caronia An ancient Sicilian city located on the northern coast of the island, founded by Ducetius in 446 B.C."
862,462126,Camarina,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Camarina Camarina was founded by Syracuse in 599 B.C., but subsequently destroyed in 552 B.C. The city was re-founded by Gela in 461 B.C. Carthage razed the site in 405 B.C., but Timoleon restored it in 339 B.C. From the late third century B.C. the town fell under Roman administration."
863,462130,Capitium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Capizzi Capitium was an ancient settlement in the Monti Nebrodi of Sicily.
864,462153,Centuripae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Centuripe A city of the indigenous Sikels, Centuripae allied itself with Athens during the expedition against Syracuse."
865,462154,Cephaloedium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cefalù An city founded on an imposing promontory on the northern coast of Sicily.
866,462157,Charybdis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cariddi (whirlpool) Charybdis was a sea monster that was rationalized in the form of a whirlpool located in the Strait of Messina.
867,462168,Pantelleria (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pantelleria An island in the Strait of Sicily with human occupation dating back at least 35,000 years, Pantelleria has evidence for Bronze Age, Punic, Roman, Medieval, and modern phases. The Romans first acquired the island in 255 B.C. They called it Cossyra."
868,462187,Drepanum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Trapani Drepanum, the modern Trapani, Sicily, is an ancient port city. The archaeological remains of the ancient city are meager. During the First Punic War, Hamilcar fortified the site. The Roman Republic lost a naval engagement near Drepanum in 249 B.C. but then prevailed in the battle of the Egadi islands in 241 B.C. The city was likely of censorial status thereafter."
869,462193,Elymi,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: W Sicily The Elymians were an indigenous people inhabiting western Sicily during the Bronze Age and the first millennium BC.
870,462195,Engyon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Troina (contra Canale 1955; Manni 1981, 167-68) An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 F3 Engyon"
871,462197,Entella,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rocca d’Entella Entella was an ancient city in the interior of Sicily, situated on the left bank of the river Hypsas."
872,462198,Epipolai,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: plateau NW Siracusa A substantial plateau that is roughly triangular in shape and extends to the north and west of Syracuse.
873,462201,Eryx,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Erice An ancient, indigenous settlement of western Sicily, likely inhabited by the Elymians."
874,462204,Euryelos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: W extremity of Epipolai The fortress was first built by Dionysius I between 402 and 397 B.C. Various future modifications took place under Agathocles and Hiero II. The fortress remained in use following the Roman conquest of 212 B.C., continuing up to the Byzantine period. A fortress built by Dionysius the Elder 9 km west of Syracuse, in the modern hamlet of Belvedere (Sicily)."
875,462213,Gaulos (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gozo MLT The island called Gozo in modern times, part of the country of Malta."
876,462214,Gela,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gela A Greek colony of Sicily founded by Rhodians and Cretans ca. 688 B.C. The oikists were the Rhodian Antiphemos and the Cretan Entimos.
877,462225,Halaesa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Castel di Tusa Halaesa is located on the north coast of Sicily between between Kalakta and Kephaloidion. The city was founded in 403 B.C. by Archonides of Herbita. The city sided with Rome during the First Punic War and was made ""libera et immunis"". By the Augustan period it had become a municipium."
878,462227,Halicyae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Salemi A town of the Elymians located between Lilybaeum and Entella.
879,462228,Haluntium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Marco d’Alunzio A small, extramural Hellenistic temple that is traditionally identified as being sacred to Hercules lies outside the modern village. Of isodomic construction, it measures ca. 20 by 8 meters. An ancient settlement located between Tyndaris and Calacte. Some authors ascribe the foundation of the site to the Acarnanians."
880,462234,Helorus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Eloro An ancient settlement of Sicily that was likely a colonial foundation of Syracuse.
881,462235,Helorus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Eloro Helorus fl. (Tellaro), a river of southeast Sicily."
882,462236,(H)Enna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Enna The origins of (H)Enna are unclear. Some sources claim it was founded by Syracuse in 664 B.C., but the archaeological record suggests it is likely a Hellenized Sikel settlement."
883,462239,Herbessos?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Montagna di Marzo Herbessos was a town of the Siculi (the modern Montagna di Marzo near Piazza Armerina, Sicily)."
884,462244,Himera,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Himera A colony on the north shore of Sicily founded in 649/8 BC by the Myletidai.
885,462247,Hippana,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte dei Cavalli Ancient settlement in the western part of Sicily, probably founded in the seventh century B.C."
886,462251,Hybla Gereatis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Paternò Hybla Gereatis was an ancient settlement in Sicily located on the slopes of Mount Etna. Its remains are located near Paternò, Sicily."
887,462252,Hybla Heraia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ragusa Ibla Hybla Heraia (modern Ragusa) was an indigenous center of ancient Sicily that came into contact with Greek and, later, Roman and Byzantine cultures."
888,462254,Hykkara?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Carini A settlement of the Sicani on the northern coast of Sicily, conquered by the Athenians in 415 BC."
889,462256,Hypsas (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Anna. This river should not be confused with the similarly named Hypsa(s) river . The Hypsas was an ancient river of Sicily that is mentioned by Polybius. It is one of two Sicilian rivers with the same ancient name. This river empties into the Mediterranean Sea near Agrigento. Its early modern and modern toponym is complicated, not only by changing names but also by variable names for different segments of the river channel."
890,462258,Ietas,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Iato Located in western Sicily, Ietas has a long and complex history. The settlement is today located within the municipality of San Cipirello, atop Mount Iato in the hinterland of Palermo. Elymians occupied this site beginning in the eighth century BCE, with the urbanized area eventually covering some 40 hectares. Greek influence becomes apparent in the sixth century. Western Sicily came under Carthaginian control during the fourth century BCE. Pyrrhus attacked Ietas in 275 BCE. During the First Punic War, Ietas surrendered to the Romans and, according to Plutarch, became a tributary state. The site was destroyed by Frederick II in 1246 and abandoned."
891,462259,Imachara?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vaccarra di Nicosia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 E3 Imachara?"
892,462266,Monte Saraceno di Ravanusa (Kakyron?),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Monte Saraceno di Ravanusa The ancient city of Kakyron, said to have been founded by the Sicani in the eighth century B.C., is probably to be identified with the remains of a fortified city, north of Licata, located on the western side of Sicily."
893,462267,Kale Akte,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marina di Caronia? Kale Akte was the site of a Sikel center that received an Ionian colony. The precise location of the ancient settlement is unknown, although it is placed in the area of Marina di Caronia."
894,462269,Kasmenai,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Monte Casale Kasmenai was founded as a colony of Syracuse in 643 B.C.
895,462270,Katane/Catina,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Catania The colonial city of Katane (modern Catania) was founded by Chalcidian Greeks sometime after the foundation of Leontini in 730 B.C.
896,462281,Lilybaeum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marsala Lilybaeum (modern Marsala) is located at the extreme westernmost point of Sicily. Lilybaeum was a Punic settlement that was conquered by the Romans in 241 B.C.
897,462283,Lipara (settlement),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lipari A Greek colony and long-time settlement on the island of the same name, located to the north of Sicily in the Tyrrhenian Sea. Modern Lipari."
898,462295,Maktorion?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Bubbonia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 E4 Maktorion?"
899,462305,Mazara,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mazara del Vallo An ancient settlement located on the western coast of Sicily.
900,462307,Megara Hyblaia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Megara Hyblaea Megara Hyblaia was an ancient Greek colony in eastern Sicily founded in the eighth century B.C.
901,462311,Melita/Malta (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Malta The island of Malta, located in the Mediterranean Sea between Sicily and Libya."
902,462312,Menai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mineo An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 F4 Menai"
903,462372,Morgantina,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Serra Orlando (contra Manni 1981, 204-205) In the twentieth century, the site has been the locus of excavations carried out by a series of academic missions, including those of Princeton University, the University of Illinois, Wesleyan University, and the University of Virginia. An indigenous Sicilian site founded by the Morgeti, according to Strabo, Morgantina was eventually defeated by Syracuse and thus, in turn, passed into Roman hegemony in the third century B.C."
904,462373,Motya,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mozia A small island in the Stagnone Lagoon of western Sicily, Motya has evidence for quite ancient settlement, including a significant Punic phase. The settlement on the island was destroyed by Syracuse in 379 BC. At one point, the island was connected to the mainland via a causeway."
905,462378,Mutyce,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Modica Ancient settlement on the southern side of the Hyblean Mountains, between Syracuse and Camarina."
906,462379,Mylae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Milazzo An ancient Greek settlement on the north-east coast of Sicily, located at the base of a peninsula that juts into the Tyrrhenian Sea. A colony of Zancle, it was probably founded in the eighth or seventh century BC. In 260 BC, during the First Punic War, the Romans won their first major naval battle against the Carthaginians in the waters off Mylae."
907,462381,Myttistraton,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Castellazzo di Marianopoli An ancient Greek city in the central part of Sicily, near modern Marianopoli, known for its fortifications."
908,462386,Naxos (settlement),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Naxos-Giardini Naxos was the earliest Greek settlement in Sicily, according to Thucydides who places its foundation in 734 B.C."
909,462388,Neaition/Netum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Noto Vecchia Neaition/Netum was an ancient town in Sicily that likely became subject to Syracuse after 263 B.C. Pliny the Elder indicates that its citizens eventually acquired Latin rights.
910,462399,Omphake,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Butera Omphake was an ancient Sikanian center that came into conflict with the Greek colony of Gela. It is probably to be identified with modern Butera on the island of Sicily.
911,462402,Ortygia (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ortygia (Siracusa) A coastal island of Sicily that became the center of ancient Syracuse.
912,462405,Pachynum (promontory),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Capo Passero A promontory of extreme southeastern Sicily.
913,462408,Palikoi/Palicorum Stagna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lago di Naftia A pair of boiling lakes in Sicily, whose effervescence was attributed to the divine agency of the Palikoi."
914,462410,Pan(h)ormus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palermo First founded in 734 BC by the Phoenicians as Ziz, Pan(h)ormus (modern Palermo) was an important ancient city of the Greek, Roman, and Arabic civilizations."
915,462424,Phintias,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Licata (contra Uggeri 1970 , 110; Manni 1981 , 60) An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 D4 Phintias"
916,462425,Phoenicusa (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Filicudi One of the Aeolian Islands, Phoenicusa shows evidence for Bronze Age occupation, as well as activity during the Greek and Roman periods."
917,462428,Phorbantia (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Levanzo Phorbantia Ins. (modern Levanzo) is the smallest of the Aegates Inss.
918,462487,Segesta/Egesta,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Segesta A center of the Elymian people in northwestern Sicily.
919,462489,Selinus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Selinunte A Greek colonial city located on the southern coast of Sicily, Selinus was said to have been founded as a colony of the city of Megara Hyblaia."
920,462492,Sicilia (island),"Sicily is the largest island in the Mediterranean Sea and has been the site of human habitation since ca. 8,000 BC. In historical terms, Sicily has seen important settlement activity connected to numerous culture groups - Punic, Carthaginian, Greek, Roman, Vandalic, Ostrogothic, Byzantine, Arabic, and Norman."
921,462498,Soluntum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Solunto An ancient settlement on the north-west coast of Sicily, some 16 km east of modern Palermo."
922,462502,Symaethus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Simeto An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 F3 Symaethus fl."
923,462503,Syracusae/Syrakousai,"Syracusae was founded on the southeast coast of Sicily as a colony of Corinth in 734/3 B.C. Initially concentrated on the coastal island Ortygia , the settlement moved to the mainland and developed into an important and powerful urban center. Syracusae would, in turn, establish secondary colonies: Akrai (664 B.C.), Kasmenai (624 B.C.), and Kamarina (559 B.C.). The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Siracusa/Syracuse An important ancient city on the island of Sicily."
924,462506,Tauromenium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Taormina Tauromenium was an ancient city in Sicily founded in 358 B.C.
925,462513,Thermae Himeraeae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Termini Imerese An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 D3 Thermae Himeraeae"
926,462514,Thermae Selinuntinae/Aquae Larodes,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sciacca The site of thermal spings, founded as a sub-colony of Selinus in the seventh century B.C. Thought to be in the area of modern Sciacca."
927,462516,Thorax,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Marro An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 G2 Thorax"
928,462527,Tyndaris,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tindari An ancient city on the north coast of Sicily. Modern Tindari.
929,462538,Zancle/Messana,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Messina Zancle was founded as Greek colony in the eighth century B.C. Home of the Mamertines who called for Roman assistance against Carthaginian forces in 264 B.C., sparking the First Punic War."
930,465907,Herakleia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: settlement in W Sicily A now unlocated settlement of western Sicily, Herakleia lies somewhere in the area of Mt. Eryx. It was likely founded in the fifth century B.C."
931,465912,Herkte,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: fort in W Sicily, apparently near Palermo and/or Ietas (unless confused with Heirkte) Now unlocated, Herkte is described by Polybius as a fortified place in coastal Sicily between Eryx and Palermo."
932,465922,Inessa/Aitna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: town, 12 m.p. W Catania; possibly AC settlement abandoned; in Roman times Aitna was alternative name for Hybla Gereatis An ancient settlement, attested by literary or documentary sources, whose precise location cannot be determined today."
933,465923,Inyx/Inikon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: town in SW Sicily? An ancient settlement, attested by literary or documentary sources, whose precise location cannot be determined today"
934,465928,Kallipolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: town in E Sicily, near Naxos Kallipolis was founded in eastern Sicily as a colony from the neighboring city of Naxos. Its precise location is unknown."
935,465931,Kimissa,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: hill town in central S Sicily An ancient town of Sicily that is now unlocated. It is known from coins that date to the time of Timoleon.
936,465935,Krastos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: settlement near Castronovo? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 47 unlocated Krastos"
937,465960,Neapolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: town in S Sicily A place named ""Neapolis"" is included on map 47 of the Barrington Atlas, with the compilers including it among the list of unlocated places. BTCGI informs the reader that this place may correspond to a toponym mentioned once by Plutarch. In that case, Plutarch refers to a district of the chora of Akragas (modern Agrigento, Sicily)."
938,465970,Palike,"Town in east central Sicily, close to Palikon Limne (called Palicorum Stagna in The Barrington Atlas ). An ancient settlement in east central Sicily, atop the Rocchicella, which included a sanctuary to the Divine Palikoi (a pair of boiling lakes) within a grotto found on the hillside."
