941,471889,Balari,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Berchidda A fierce mountain tribe of interior Sardinia, said to have descended from Iberian and Libyan mercenaries who had deserted from the Carthaginian army."
942,471894,Bitia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Punta di Chia Bitia was an ancient settlement located in extreme southern Sardinia. It likely had a nuragic phase that was followed by Punic and Roman phases of occupation.
943,471899,Caralis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cagliari A city with its origins in a Phoenician settlement of the seventh century BC, Caralis comes under Roman control in 238 BC during the Punic Wars. Florus refers to it as urbs urbinum, or capital of Sardinia"
944,471907,Cornus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Caterina di Pittinuri Cornus was an ancient settlement on the west coast of Sardinia. The site is located in the modern municipal territory of S. Caterina di Pittinuri.
945,471908,Corsi,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gallura The Corsi were an ancient tribe occupying a region of ancient Sardinia that is now known as Gallura.
946,471914,Diabate (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Isola Piana An island near Sardinia recorded by Ptolemy and Stephanus. It is possibly to be identified with the modern Isola Piana.
947,471942,Herakleous? Limen,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Porto Malfatano The Herakleous Limen, mentioned by Ptolemy, was an ancient bay or anchorage on the island of Sardinia, probably to be identified with the modern Porto Malfatano, which is located on the southern coast of the island between the Capo Malfatano and the Capo Spartivento."
948,471944,Tavolara (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tavolara A small island located just off the northeast coast of Sardinia. It may correspond to a ""Hermaia"" mentioned in Ptolemy's Geography."
949,471951,Ilienses,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mulgaria The Ilienses were an important people of the interior of Sardinia who were never subjugated to Roman authority.
