951,471977,Neapolis (Sardinia),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Maria de Nabui Neapolis was an ancient city of Sardinia, located on the west coast of the island at the southern extremity of the Gulf of Oristano, near the present-day località of Santa Maria di Nabui."
952,471979,Nora,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pula Pausanias attributes the foundation of Nora to a group of Iberians under Norax. A pre-Roman and Roman site in southern Sardinia.
953,471987,Olbia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Olbia A Phoenician, Carthaginian, and Roman center in northeast Sardinia."
954,472014,Sardinia (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sardinia Sardinia is the second-largest island in the Mediterranean Sea.
955,472024,Sulci(s),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Antioco A site with Phoenician origins (founded ca. 730-700 B.C.) located in southwestern Sardinia. Site of a Roman naval victory against Carthage by Gaius Sulpicius Paterculus in 258 B.C. during the First Punic War.
956,472032,Tharros,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Giovanni di Sinis Smith 1857 (2.1135) emphasizes that the site is mentioned by Ptolemy as Tarrae or Tarras and also in the Antonine Itineraries, which place it 12 miles from Othoca. An ancient Phoenician settlement located in western Sardinia on a promontory in the Gulf of Oristano, just south of modern San Giovanni di Sinis. While it originates as a Punic or Carthaginian settlement ca. 700 BCE, it continued as a major town in Roman times."
957,472033,Thyrsos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tirso The Tirso river, one of the most important rivers of Sardinia, flows from the Punta Pianedda to the Gulf of Oristano."
958,472034,Tibula,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S. Teresa di Gallura An ancient port settlement in the north of Sardinia, taken by the author of the Antonine Itineraries as the starting point for the description of all the routes on the island. It is possibly to be identified with modern Lungoni/Santa Teresa Gallura (thus, tentatively, BAtlas)."
959,472035,Turris Libisonis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Porto Torres. W. Smith, quoting Pliny 3.12.17, suggests that the site was one of the more substantial towns of Roman Sardinia. A settlement of northwestern Sardinia, located at the mouth of the Mannu River. The site served as a port in Phoenician, Carthaginian, and Roman phases of occupation. The modern Porto Torres stands on the site, about 10 miles distant from Sassari."
960,472048,Alalie/Aleria,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aleria A city of Corsica with significant pre-historic, Greek, Etruscan, and Roman phases."
