1092,511461,Zeleia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sarıköy An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 52 B4 Zeleia"
1093,514912,Anastasian Wall,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Murus Anastasii/Makron Teichos Also known as the ""Long Wall of Thrace,"" this fortification line (nearly 60km in length) spanned the Thracian peninsula west of Constantinople. It was built in the 5th century CE and refurbished and modified by the emperor Anastasius in the early 6th century."
1094,515667,Phinopolis,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 52 unlocated Phinopolis"
1095,520977,Bosphorus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bogaziçi A strait that forms part of the boundary between Europe and Asia.
1096,520988,Chalcedon,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kadıköy Chalcedon was an ancient maritime city of Bithynia.
1097,520998,Constantinopolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Istanbul Capital city of the Eastern Roman Empire, the Latin, and the Ottoman Empires founded by Constantine I in AD 330."
1098,530769,Acheron (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akheron A river of Epirus.
1099,530770,Acherusia (lake),"This ancient lake is to be identified with the modern Kastri dry lake in Preveza, Epirus (Greece)."
1100,530771,Actium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aktion, formerly Punta Site of an ancient temple of Apollo near which Octavian erected a monument to commemorate his naval victory over M. Antonius in 31 BCE."
1101,530787,Aiginion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nea Koutsouphliani? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 D2 Aiginion"
1102,530789,Aithikia,"An area of northwest Thessaly at one time in antiquity occupied by a people named Aithikes, whence the name."
1103,530793,Alyz(e)ia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kandila A city located on the west coast of Acarnania. The southwestern portion of the walled city underlies modern Kandila.
1104,530794,Ambracia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arta Ambracia (modern Arta) was an ancient city of central Greece, founded as a colony of Corinth in the seventh century BCE. It was located near the Ambracian Gulf along the course of the river Arachthos."
1105,530797,Amphilochia,"An ancient region and its associated people, located in west Greece, to the east of the Gulf of Ambracia."
1106,530798,Anactorium,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ag. Petros, near Nea Kamarina A city founded by Corinth in the seventh century B.C. on the gulf of Ambracia."
1107,530799,Anapos? (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gero Poros An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 D4 Anapos? fl."
1108,530807,Arachthos/Araththos/Aratthos/Aracthus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arachthos A river in eastern Epirus, Greece that flows from the Pindus Mountains to the Ionian Sea."
1109,530808,Argeia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: region of Argos Amphilochikon An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 D4 Argeia"
1110,530812,Artemita? Pr.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Khounovina An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 D5 Artemita? Pr."
1111,530822,Rogous,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rogous A fortified acropolis known as Kastro Rogon and located near modern Nea Karasounta in Epirus, Greece has been identified as the likely site of ancient Bouchetion, a colony of Elis."
1112,530824,Bouthroton,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Butrinti ALB Modern Butrint (Butrinti) in Albania, ancient Bouthroton was an ancient Greek and later Roman city located in Epirus. The city became an important bishopric by the sixth century. The site was enrolled as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1992 because of its archaeological remains, which date from prehistoric times down through the late Middle Ages."
1113,530826,Cephallania (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kephallenia The largest of the Ionian islands.
1114,530829,Charadros,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastri at Palaia Philippias An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 C3 Charadros"
1115,530833,Cheimerion? (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akra Trophale An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 B3 Cheimerion? Pr."
1116,530834,Corcyra (settlement),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kerkyra/Corfu The eponymous settlement of the island of Corcyra (modern Korfu).
1117,530835,Corcyra (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kerkyra/Corfu An island located in the Ionian Sea. Modern-day Corfu, Greece."
1118,530842,Dioryktos?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Peratia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 C4 Dioryktos?"
1119,530843,Dodona (Epirus),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dodone An ancient site of northwestern Epirus, Dodona was home to one of the most ancient oracular traditions of the Hellenic world."
1120,530845,Dolicha (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Koutsilaris The ancient Ionian island of Dolicha is probably to be associated with the modern, now-landlocked peak known as Koutsilaris in the modern Greek municipality of Mesolongi."
1121,530852,Echinades (islands),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Echinades A group of islands in the Ionian Sea off the coast of Acarnania.
1122,530856,Elaiatis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: region of Ephyra An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 C3 Elaiatis"
1123,530859,Elat(r)eia?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Paliorophon An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 C3 Elat(r)eia?"
1124,530870,Ephyra/Kichyros,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mesopotamon An ancient settlement located on the hill immediately north of modern Mesopotamo in the modern Greek regional unit of Preveza (Epirus).
1125,530871,Epirus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Epeiros ALB/GRE An ancient region that included northwest parts of modern Greece and the southern tip of modern Albania.
1126,530879,Glykys/Elaias?/Cheimerion? Limen,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Ormos Ag. Ioannou An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 B3 Glykys?/Elaias?/Cheimerion? Limen"
1127,530906,Ithaca (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ithake A rocky, Ionian island located off the west coast of Greece that boasted important natural harbors. By historical Greek times, the island was assumed to be identical with the Ithaca of Homer (i.e., the home of Odysseus) and a hero cult dedicated to him was active there."
1128,530942,Kestrine/Kammania,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: region between R. Thyamis and R. Pavla A district of Epirus.
1129,530962,Krane,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Argostolion An ancient settlement near modern Argostoli on the island of Kefalonia.
1130,530966,Krithote Pr.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akra Tourkovigla An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 D4 Krithote Pr."
1131,530974,Leucas,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kalligoni An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 C4 Leucas"
1132,531019,Onchesmos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sarandë, formerly Santi Quaranta ALB Onchesmos was a port in Epirus."
1133,531025,Orraon/Horreum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ammotopos Orraon was a city of the Molossians, founded probably in the early fourth century BCE and planned on a grid system. It may have been destroyed by the Romans after Pydna in 168 BCE, and its inhabitants were removed to Nikopolis in 31 BCE. The site is remarkable for the preservation of its stone-built domestic architecture."
1134,531026,Oxeiai/Thoai (islands),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Oxia, Koutsilaris An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 D5 Oxeiai/Thoai Inss."
1135,531028,Paianion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mastron An ancient settlement mentioned only by Polybius who reports its destruction and despoiling by Philip of Macedon. Its acropolis is generally agreed to be identifiable with the fortified ""Palaiokastro"" that lies north of the modern Greek village of Mastron, just to the west of the church of Ag. Georgios. Other architectural finds were reported in the 20th century between the fortified site and the river Achelous to the west."
1136,531034,Palairos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kekhropoula A fortified ancient Greek city located in southern Epirus.
1137,531036,Pale,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lixouri An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 B5 Pale"
1138,531039,Pandosia?,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Trikastro Pandosia? was a colony founded by Elis on the river Acheron.
1139,531051,Pelodes Limen,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gji i Butrintit ALB A lake near Buthrotum that served as a port.
1140,531070,Poseidion (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akra Skala (on Hexamili) ALB A promontory or cape mentioned by Strabo in his description of coastal northwest Greece. Scholars identify it with modern Akra Skala, Albania."
1141,531078,Ptychia (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vido An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 A2 Ptychia Ins."
1142,531104,Stratos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sourovigli Stratos was the chief town of Acarnania. Modern Sourovigli in Greece.
1143,531107,Sybota (islands),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sivota (Nisi Sivota and Nisi Ag. Nikolaos) An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 B3 Sybota Inss."
1144,531112,Talares,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 C2 Talares"
1145,531113,Taphiae? (islands),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Meganisi, Kalamos, Kastos A group of small islands in the Ionian Sea located between Leucas and the Acarnanian coast. The main island of this group can probably be identified with the modern island of Meganisi, which in the ancient sources is called Táphos."
1146,531117,Thesprotia,One of the three main ancient tribes of Epirus.
1147,531121,Thyamis (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Thyamis, formerly Kalamas An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 B2 Thyamis fl."
1148,531124,T(o)maros (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tomaros, formerly Olytsika An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 C2 T(o)maros M."
1149,531154,Zakythos/Zacynthus (settlement),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zakynthos An ancient settlement on the Aegean island of the same name.
1150,531155,Zacynthus (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zakynthos An island in the Ionian Sea.
1151,531158,Zeus Ainesios (temple),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: atop Mt. Ainos A temple of Zeus Ainesios located atop mount Ainos on the island of Cephallania.
1152,536082,Hestiaia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Acarnania An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 54 unlocated Hestiaia"
1153,536133,Sollion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Leucas and Palairos on Plagia Pr. Mentioned in fifth-century BCE sources, this as-yet-located harbor city probably lay on the Plagia peninsula in Acarnania, likely between Leucas and Palairos."
1154,536807,Charadros,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river Polybius thus names a river in Epirus where the Aphas (modern Louros) must be meant.
1155,540582,Abai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palaiokhori Exarkhou Abai was an ancient town in Phocis, said to have been founded by the Argive Abas. It has been identified with a hilltop, fortified archaeological site known as Palaiokhori, near modern Exarchos. The site of an oracular sanctuary of Apollo and Artemis associated with Abai in ancient literature has been identified at nearby Kallipodi."
1156,540591,Aetolia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aitolia An ancient region and associated ethnic group (tribe) of central Greece.
1157,540613,Dimini,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dimini The modern Greek village of Dimini in Thessaly lends its name to important prehistoric and Bronze Age settlements whose remains lie in its vicinity. In the 20th century, some scholars attempted to assign the placename ""Aison"" (reported by Stephanus of Byzantium) here as well, but others now reject the name entirely as false. The vicinity was abandoned at the end of the thirteenth century BCE and not reoccupied until modern times."
1158,540617,Akraiphiai,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Karditsa An ancient town of Boeotia located on the eastern edge of the former Lake Copais.
1159,540618,Akyphas/Pindos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pyrgos A town that was part of the tetrapolis of Doris in ancient Greece.
1160,540626,Ambrossos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Distomon An ancient Greek settlement in Phocis. Modern Distomo.
1161,540627,Amphanai?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Soros An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D2 Amphanai?"
1162,540628,Amphiareion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mavrodilesi The Amphiareion of Oropos was an ancient sanctuary dedicated in the fifth century BCE to the hero Amphiaraos and located in the hills southeast of the port of Oropos.
1163,540629,Amphikleia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dadi/Nekrotapheio/Paliopyrgos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D3 Amphikleia"
1164,540630,Amphissa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Salona An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 C3 Amphissa"
1165,540634,Anauros (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: E Iolkos A torrent flowing from Mount Pelion to the east of Iolkos.
1166,540639,Anthedon,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Loukisia A port city of northern Boeotia located in the foothills of Mount Messapion on the Gulf of Euboea.
1167,540642,Antikyra,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastro tou Stenou in Phocis Antikyra was a port city located east of Kirrha. Pausanias identifies it with the Homeric Kyparissos.
1168,540645,Aphetai?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Platania/Kato Yeoryios An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 E2 Aphetai?"
1169,540655,"Apollo Thourios, T.","The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S Isoma An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D4 Apollo Thourios, T."
1170,540659,Argoura,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gremnos Magoula Argoura was a city of Thessalian Pelasgiotis that may have been the Homeric Argissa. The city was located on the Peneios river, some 40 stades from Atrax."
1171,540663,Arne,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Magoula Balomenou An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D4 Arne"
1172,540672,Asopos (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river A river of Boeotia and northern Attica that marked the border between the cities of Thebes and Plataea.
1173,540673,Aspledon/Eudeielos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Avrokastro/Polyyria An ancient town in Boeotia that was mentioned by Homer in the Iliad.
1174,540678,"Athena Itonia?, T.","The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mamoura An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D4 Athena Itonia?, T."
1175,540679,Athena Kranaia (temple),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tsouka The temple of Athena Kranaia, mentioned by Pausanias, was located near ancient Elateia in Phokis."
1176,540681,Athenai Diades,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yialtra Kastelli/Loutra An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D3 Athenai Diades"
1177,540682,Atrax,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aliphaka/Koutsokhero An ancient Greek polis in Pelasgiotis in Thessaly. The site is located in modern Greece, immediately south of the Peneios river and 2.5 km west of a small village named Κάστρο (Kastro), which is within the municipality of Larissa."
1178,540687,Azoros,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vouvala Azoros was a city belonging to the tripolis of the Perrhaebi.
1179,540689,Boeotia,A region of central Greece inhabited since prehistoric times.
1180,540690,Boibe (lake),"An ancient lake of Thessaly in Greece whose modern remnants were drained in the 20th century. A lake restoration project, inaugurated in 2018, has transformed the area into the modern Lake Karla."
1181,540692,Boudoros (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Voudoros The ancient river called Boudoros is associated with the Voudoros Potamos, in Euboia, Central Greece."
1182,540703,Chalcis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Khalkis, formerly Negroponte Chalkis was a settlement on Euboea, inhabited from the sub-Neolithic period, which played an important role in Greek colonisation of the Mediterranean in the 8th century BC. It was part of the Delian League in the 5th century BC and the Second Athenian Sea League in the 4th century BC. In Hellenistic times it was one of the three 'fetters of Greece' used by Macedon to control Greece. It came under Roman control in 197 BC."
1183,540709,Charadra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mariolates An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 C3 Charadra"
1184,540711,Chorsiai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastron Khostion An ancient Greek settlement in Boeotia, located north of the modern bay of Hagios Sarandi on the Corinthian Gulf."
1185,540717,Coronea,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palaia Koroneia/Pyrgos An ancient settlement of Boeotia, located about 4 km north of the modern Greek city that now bears its name."
1186,540719,Cynoscephalae,A pass in the hills of Thessaly where a battle was fought in 197 BCE between Rome and Philip V of Macedon.
1187,540722,Daphnous,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ag. Konstantinos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D3 Daphnous"
1188,540723,Daulis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Davleia/Kastro. Pausanias offers interesting commentary about a sanctuary of Athena located at Daulis that housed a wooden cult image or xoanon that was said to have been brought from Athens by Procne. Daulis was an ancient city of eastern Phocis.
1189,540726,Delphi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Delphi, formerly Pytho The ancient panhellenic sanctuary of Delphi in Greece, seat of the oracle of Apollo."
1190,540743,Drymaia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Glunitsa An ancient settlement. Its archaeological remains lie just to the southwest (pace NP) of modern Drymaia.
1191,540750,Eilesion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Khlembotsari A city of southeast Boeotia mentioned by Homer.
1192,540752,*Ekarra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Makryrakhi/Kaitsa An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 C2 *Ekarra"
1193,540755,Elateia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Drakhmani/Piperis Elateia was an ancient Greek city of Phocis, and numbered as the most important place in that region after Delphi. The remains of the ancient site are located to the northeast of the modern town."
1194,540756,Eleutherai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gyphtokastro A city of northern Attika at the boundary of Boeotia, Eleutherai is one of the best preserved ancient fortresses in Attica."
1195,540770,Erochos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kato Souvala An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D3 Erochos"
1196,540775,Euboea (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Euboia Euboea is the second largest Greek island by area and is separated from the Greek mainland by the Euripus Strait.
1197,540782,Eupalion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastro Soule An ancient Greek settlement in the region of Locris. Modern consensus identifies an ancient ""kastro"" above modern Efpalio (called Soule prior to 1907) as Eupalion's acropolis."
1198,540787,Eutresis (Boeotia),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arkopodi An ancient settlement in Boeotia. The site, which is located near modern Leuctra, has been known in modern times as Arkophodi."
1199,540790,Glaphyrai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kopraena/Kapurna An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D2 Glaphyrai"
1200,540792,Gomphoi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mouzaki An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 B2 Gomphoi"
1201,540801,Haliartos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastri Maziou Haliartos was an ancient city of Boeotia, founded as a contemporary of Orchomenos. The city was destroyed by C. Lucretius during the Third Macedonian War. There is a modern Greek town adjacent to the site, which now carries the ancient name."
1202,540805,Harma,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lykovouni An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 E4 Harma"
1203,540811,(H)Ellopia (Euboea),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastaniotissa? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 E3 (H)Ellopia"
1204,540820,Hyampolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Exarkhos, formerly Bogdanou Hyampolis was a city of eastern Phocis, located some 2.5 km from Abai."
1205,540823,Hyettos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dendri An ancient settlement of Boeotia. The site is located about 2 km east of modern Loutsi in Greece.
1206,540829,(Hypo)Chalkis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kato Vasiliki An ancient settlement on the Aetolian coast. The archaeological site of Chalkis is located immediately east of modern Kato Vasiliki in Western Greece.
1207,540837,Iolkos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Volos Kastro Iolkos was an ancient city located in Magnesia, Thessaly, Greece."
1208,540844,Kabeirion,"A sanctuary near Thebes where the mystery cult of the god Kabeiros and his son, Pais, was celebrated."
1209,540846,Kallas (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Xerias An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 E3 Kallas fl."
1210,540852,Kaphereus (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kavo Doro A prominent cape at the southern end of the island of Euboea. Modern Kavo Doro, Greece."
1211,540880,Korax (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ghiona An ancient mountain of Aetolia, known today as Giona. Some ancient sources, echoed or interpreted by modern commentators, seem also to include or alternately identify the nearby mountain known today as Vardousia, although most recent scholarship rejects this notion. A peak of Vardousia (Βαρδούσια) seems to have acquired variants of the name ""Korax"" in modern times."
1212,540883,Korykion Antron (Parnassos),A sacred cave on mount Parnassos named for Apollo's daughter Corycia. It is not to be confused with another cave of the same name at Corycus in Cilicia.
1213,540886,Krannon/Ephyra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastro/Douraki An ancient Thessalian settlement, with evidence of occupation in the area stretching back to the Neolithic."
1214,540888,Kreusis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Livadostro Ancient Kreusis was the market town for Thespiae and was located on the slope of Mt. Korombili, near the modern town of Livadhostro."
1215,540890,Krokion Pedion,"The Crocus field was a plain in Achaia Phthiotis, where Philip II of Macedon won a major victory over the Phokians and Athenians in 353/2 B.C."
1216,540898,Kyrtones,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastron Kolakas/Karaouli An ancient settlement of eastern Opuntian Lokris mentioned only by Pausanias. BAtlas followed Fossey 1990 and earlier scholars in tentatively associating it with a Hellenistic tower near the modern village of Kolakas / Karaouli, which has subsequently been renamed Kyrtoni by virtue of the association. Katsonopoulou 1990 (an unpublished dissertation) separately identified Kyrtones with the unexcavated settlement site of Monachou/Palio Chorio."
1217,540899,Kytinion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Paliokhori/Ag. Georgios An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D3 Kytinion"
1218,540904,Larisa Kremaste/Pelasgia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pelasgia An ancient settlement of Achaea Phthiotis, located in the area of the modern town of Pelasgia in Greece."
1219,540905,Larissa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Larisa The ancient capital of the Thessalian Pelasgiotis, modern Larisa in Greece."
1220,540906,Larymna (Boeotia),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastri/Larmes Located on the Gulf of Euboea, Larymna was a town of eastern Locris."
1221,540907,Lebadeia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Levadeia Lebadeia was an ancient settlement located close to the western frontier of Boeotia.
1222,540908,Ledon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Modi An ancient Greek settlement in Phocis, mentioned by Pausanias and abandoned by his time. Possibly to be located at modern Modi."
1223,540911,Lelant(i)on Pedion,A plain on the island of Euboea located between Chalcis and Eretria.
1224,540913,Leuktra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Parapoungia Leuktra was a settlement located in ancient Boeotia. It was located along the road leading from Thespiae to Plataea, somewhere in the vicinity of the modern town. In 371 B.C. the Thebans defeated the Spartans in a celebrated battle near the site."
1225,540923,Magnesia (region),"A coastal region of Thessaly, including the long, narrow peninsula stretching into the Aegean on the southeast. Magnesia gives its name to the modern Greek regional unit."
1226,540925,Makkarai?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arabises An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 C2 Makkarai?"
1227,540927,Makyneia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kato Mammakou An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 B4 Makyneia"
1228,540939,Medeon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ag. Theodoroi Medeon was a polis of ancient Greece with occupation stretching from the Archaic period to Late Antiquity. It is located southeast of Aspra Spitia, Viotia, Central Greece."
1229,540941,Meliteia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Avaritsa A town in Achaia Phthiotis, which usually provided one of the regions' two representatives to the Delphic Amphiktyony. It became part of the Aetolian League around 260 BC and part of the Thessalian League in 189 BC. It remained inhabited through the Roman Imperial period and into Byzantine times. The modern town of Avaritsa is nearby."
1230,540946,Methone (Magnesian),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nevestiki An ancient settlement located on the western side of the Magnesian peninsula on a hill named Nevestiki near modern Lekhonia (Volos municipality, Greece)."
1231,540950,Mopsion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Stenon Rhodias An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 C1 Mopsion"
1232,540953,Mykalessos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rhitsona An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 F4 Mykalessos"
1233,540967,Oeroe (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river The modern Livadostras river, emptying into the Corinthian Gulf near ancient Kreusis."
1234,540968,Oeta (mountain),"A mountain range of southern Phthiotis and northern Phocis in Greece, Oeta is celebrated in mythology as the site of Herakles' death."
1235,540969,Oiantheia/Euantheia?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tolofon, formerly Vitrinitsa A town of the Ozolian Locrians, situated at the western entrance of the Crissaean Gulf."
1236,540979,Olizon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ag. Andreas/Palaiokastro An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 E2 Olizon"
1237,540980,Olmones,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: N Pavlon. Vici.org and DARE , evidently following the now out-of-date Smith 1854 , associate ancient Olmones with a primarily Helladic site immediately south of modern Stroviki . An ancient village (kome) of Boeotia, mentioned by Pausanias and Stephanus of Byzantium. It is generally identified with a paliokastro located 1km north-northeast of modern Pavlon (1.75km south-southwest of the site of Hyettos); however, the ToposText editors note a recent proposal that it be identified with ""unexcavated remains at Monachou-Paliochori 4.3 km E of Hyettos."""
1238,540981,Olooson,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Elassona/Panayia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 C1 Olooson"
1239,540984,Onchestos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kazarma Onchestos was located northwest of Thebes and was the site of an ancient cult of Poseidon. Prior to the Macedonian period the Boeotian confederacy met here.
1240,540985,Onochonos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river A river of Thassalia, associated by modern scholars with the Kalentzis."
1241,540987,Orchomenos/Orchomenus (Boeotia),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Skripou. Not to be confused with Arcadian Orchomenos . The so-called ""Boeotian Orchomenos"", located in the vicinity of the Byzantine church of Panaghia Skripou. The longer, modern title is used to distinguish this settlement from another Greek site with the same ancient name located in Arcadia in the Peloponnese."
1242,540989,Orobiai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rovies/Ag. Ilias An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 E3 Orobiai"
1243,540992,Orthe/Phalanna,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Damasi Orthe/Phalanna was a city in Perrhaebia in Thessaly.
1244,540993,Ossa (mountain),"A mountain in central Greece (between Olympus and Pelion), important in Greek mythology. Several ancient cities and a shrine to the Muses were located on its western slopes."
1245,540998,Pagasai,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D2 Pagasai"
1246,541006,Pamphia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sitaralona An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 B3 Pamphia"
1247,541008,Panopeos/Phanotis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ag. Vlasios A city of Phocis on the Boeotian frontier. Substantive remains of the ancient walled city are visible on the height immediately south of modern Hagios Vlasios (Municipality of Livadia, Boeotia, Greece)."
1248,541011,Parapotamioi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Belesi/Levendi According to Strabo, Parapotamioi ""is a settlement on the Cephissus River near Phanoteus and Chaeroneia and Elateia""."
1249,541016,Pedieis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palaia Phiva An ancient polis in Phocis, possibly to be identified with the fortified ancient site on the Kephissos river known in the 19th and 20th centuries as Palaiophiva."
1250,541021,Pelion (mountain),A mountain mentioned in ancient sources.
1251,541035,Phalassia (promontory),"A promontory, listed by Ptolemy among other coastal features of Euboea, Phalassia is probably to be associated with a promontory on the Malian Gulf in the modern locality of Βασιλίνας (Vasilinas) where a late 19th century lighthouse now stands."
1252,541041,Pharkadon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Klokoto An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 C1 Pharkadon"
1253,541042,Pharsalus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pharsala Pharsalus was one of the most important cities of ancient Thessaly.
1254,541044,Pherai,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Velestino Pherai was an ancient city of Thessaly in the district of Pelasgiotis.
1255,541048,Phocis,"A region of central ancient Greece. In ~ 356 BCE, Phocian generals seized Delphi, starting the Third Sacred War."
1256,541053,Phylake,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Persouphli/Dervesi A city in Achaea Phthiotis, the mythical home of Protesilaos. It had been absorbed into Thebai Phthiotides by the 3rd century BC."
1257,541056,Phyllos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Magoula Paliambela An ancient city of Thessaly, possibly to be associated with a mound located 2 km south-southwest of modern Fyllo, Karditsa that has yielded Hellenistic tombstones and earlier material."
1258,541062,Pindus (mountain range),A large mountain range in north and central Greece.
1259,541063,Plataea,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kokkla Plataea is an ancient city of Boeotia, located south of Thebes. In 479 BC Plataea was the site of the Battle of Plataea, in which an allied Greek army defeated the Persians. Plataea was destroyed in the Peloponnesian War by Thebes and Sparta in 427 BC and rebuilt in 386 BC."
1260,541069,Poteidania,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kambos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 C4 Poteidania"
1261,541077,Pteleon,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gritsa An Archaic to Late Antique settlement and Mycenaean tombs on the hill Gritsa south of modern Pteleos in Magnesia Thessaly.
1262,541078,Sanctuary of Ptoan Apollo,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastraki Described by Pausanias, this sanctuary was located near Akraiphiai in Boeotia."
1263,541081,Pyrasos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nea Ankhialos Ancient Pyrasos in Thessaly overlooked the Bay of Volo. Its site is occupied by the modern Néa Anchiálos (Magnesia, Thessaly, Greece)."
1264,541085,Rhizous,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tarsanas An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D1 Rhizous"
1265,541092,Skiathos/Sciathus (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: island Skiathos is an island in the northwestern part of the Aegean Sea and the westernmost island of the northern Sporades.
1266,541102,Siphai/Tipha,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aliki An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 E4 Siphai/Tipha"
1267,541103,Skarpheia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Molos? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 D3 Skarpheia"
1268,541106,Skolos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Neokhorakion/Moustaphades An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 E4 Skolos"
1269,541107,Skotoussa (Thessaly),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ag. Triada/Souphli An ancient settlement, abandoned by Pausanias' day."
1270,541108,Skyros (settlement),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastro Homonymous ancient settlement on Skyros island, co-located with the modern settlement. Habitation dates from the early Helladic onward."
1271,541112,Spercheios,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river A river of central Greece that rises in the Tymfristos mountains. Today, it empties into the Malian Gulf some 14km east of its ancient mouth (BAtlas 55 C3 Spercheios fl.)."
1272,541113,Stiris/Steiris,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palaiokhori/Ag. Nikolaos An ancient settlement of eastern Phocis. The site of ancient Steiri has only ever been partly explored and defined. The city was located in an area known locally today as Paliokhori. The acropolis has been identified on a hill locally known today as Bouli, where a church or monastery of Agios Nikolaos (now ruined?) reportedly succeeded it. The site was not coincident with the modern town that has inherited the ancient name. Rather, ancient Stiris was located to the south and east of the famous monastery of Hosios Loukas, which lies between modern Steiri and modern Kyriaki (archaeological evidence for fortifications and other structures have, however, been noted both at modern Steiri and at Hosios Loukas as elsewhere in the area)."
1273,541117,Styra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: settlement and quarries, Nea Styra Styra was an ancient port city located in the southwestern part of Euboea that possessed also an inland acropolis (on the modern peak of Hagios Nikolaos), extensive quarries, and a series of large stone structures known today as drakospita (dragon's houses)."
1274,541131,Tempe,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: gorge A gorge in Thessaly, Greece, located south of Mt. Olympus and north of Mt. Ossa. The Peneios river flows through the gorge."
1275,541136,Thaumakoi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Domokos Kastri An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 C2 Thaumakoi"
1276,541138,Thebai/Thebae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Thivai The ancient city of Thebes in Boeotia (modern Greece).
1277,541140,Thermopylae,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: pass and fort Thermopylae is the name of a narrow pass leading from Thessaly into Locris. It was the site of a pivotal battle during the Graeco-Persian wars in 480 BC.
1278,541141,Thespiai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Erimokastro Thespiai was an ancient city of Boeotia, located at the foot of Mt. Helicon."
1279,541146,Thisbe,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kakosi Thisbe was an ancient town of Boeotia located on the southern flank of Mt. Helicon.
1280,541153,Tithronion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palaiokastro An ancient city of Phocis in Greek, which according to Pausanias was burned by Xerxes. Modern consensus places its acropolis at an unexcavated paliokastro north of modern Ano Kalivia, just inside the southern boundary of the Kapenitsa-Vertzianou-Amfikleia protected area."
1281,541161,Trichonion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gavalou An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 B3 Trichonion"
1282,541163,Trikka,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Trikkala/Ag. Nikolaos An ancient settlement in Thessaly. Modern Trikala in Greece.
1283,543617,Akontion (Euboean settlement),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Euboean town According to Stephanus, an ancient settlement named Akontion was located on the island of Euboea. Its precise location cannot be determined today."
1284,543638,Argiopion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: sanctuary of Demeter near Plataea An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 unlocated Argiopion"
1285,543639,Argos Pelasgikon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: city or region of Homeric Thessaly An ancient settlement, attested by literary or documentary sources, whose precise location cannot be determined today"
1286,543694,Echedamia/Pyrrha,"Pausanias mentions the city of Echedamia, positioning it in Phocis and listing it among cities ""burned to the ground"" during the Sacred War. Its precise location is not known today."
1287,543704,Eteonos/Skaphaliai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Skolos An ancient settlement in Boiotia, mentioned by Homer, where Oedipus was reputed to have been buried in a sanctuary of Demeter. Its precise location cannot be determined."
1288,543727,Hypereia (spring),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: spring in Thessaly Strabo locates a Hypereia krēnē, mentioned in the Iliad's ""Catalog of Ships"", at Pherai in Thessaly (modern Velestino) and geographically criticizes its assignment by Homer to the contingent led by Eurypylos. Without comment, Pliny places ""fons Hyperia"" immediately before ""oppida Pherae"" in a list of places in Thessaly (E. Kirsten in RE accepts this as a reference to the spring at Pherai, but BAtlDir lists it as an ""unlocated spring in Thessaly""). Archaeological work confirms a perennial spring at Velestino was a locus of settlement activity from the Neolithic period onwards, and a structure monumentalizing the spring was built and modified during the Classical and Hellenistic periods."
1289,543758,Kourion (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Pleuron An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 unlocated Kourion M."
1290,543766,Larissa,"For this entry in the ""Unlocated Toponyms"" list in the Map-by-Map Directory for the Barrington Atlas Map 55, Fossey and Morin cite Stählin 1924, but the relevant section thereof is entirely about the well-known Thessalian Larissa (modern Larisa in Greece) . An unlocated ancient settlement of Thessaly, listed in the Map-by-Map Directory for Barrington Atlas Map 55 as ""located somewhere on Mount Ossa."""
1291,543768,Libethra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Magnesian spring An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 unlocated Libethra"
1292,543770,Lykoreia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Parnassus M., perhaps near Korykion Antron An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 unlocated Lykoreia"
1293,543787,Messapion (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Anthedon in Boeotia A small mountain range that is located in the northeastern part of Boeotia and mainland Euboea. This mountain range borders the North Euboean Gulf and the Euripus Strait.
1294,543807,Nisa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Homeric site in Boeotia Now unlocated, Nisa in Boeotia is included in the Homeric 'catalogue of ships'."
1295,543819,Ormenion/Orminion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: deme of Demetrias An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 unlocated Ormenion/Orminion"
1296,543840,Pharygion M.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Phocis An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 55 unlocated Pharygion M."
1297,543882,Thaumakie,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: city of Homeric Thessaly An ancient settlement, attested by literary or documentary sources, whose precise location cannot be determined today"
1298,543884,Thermodon (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: seasonal brook, near Tanagra? A river of Boeotia located between Tanagra and Glisas. The precise location is uncertain."
1299,543891,Tisaion (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S Magnesian Pr. An unlocated mountain or promontory mentioned in ancient sources, situated somewhere in the southern part of the peninsula of Magnesia."
1300,550399,Achaion Limen,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Haci Ahmet Ağa An ancient settlement in Aeolis, located between Myrina and Gryneion. According to Strabo, there was an altar of the Twelve Gods there."
1301,550401,Achilleion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Beşika Burnu An ancient Greek city in the southwest of the Troas. A site that in antiquity was considered to be the tomb of Achilles is nearby at Beşiktepe.
1302,550403,Adramyttium,"The Barrington Atlas provides the following additional information: Karataş (Ören) Current Archaeology in Turkey glosses the site with the modern appellation ""Balıkesir"" An ancient city of Aeolis, located on the modern Karataş peninsula at Ören in Turkey's Balıkesir province."
1303,550406,Aeolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: coast and islands between Troas and Ionia Sometimes defined as covering the entrance of the Hellespont to the mouth of the Hermus River, Aeolis was a region centered on the North West coast of Asia Minor and some islands, notably Lesbos. Herodotus lists the eleven ancient cities of Aeolis as Cyme, Lerisae, Neon Teichos, Temnos, Cilla, Notion, Aegiroessa, Pitane, Aegaeae, Myrina, and Gryneia. He also claims that Smyrna was once an Aeolian city, but was taken by the Ionians at an unspecified date."
1304,550415,Aianteion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: mouth of Azmak Çay Aianteion refers to a place on a promontory near İn Tepe where classical sources believed Telamonian Ajax was buried.
1305,550416,Aidoneus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ilıca Dere An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D2 Aidoneus fl."
1306,550432,Andiros (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kursak Çay An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D2 Andiros fl."
1307,550435,Antissa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Skalokhori/Obriokastro, on Lesbos GRE An ancient settlement on a promontory on the island of Lesbos sometimes referred to as ""Ovriokastro"". Antissa was destroyed by Roman forces in 166 BCE. The visible archaeological remains derive mostly from a later Genoese fortress."
1308,550451,Argyria?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Karaidin Maden (Gümüş Maden) An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 E2 Argyria?"
1309,550460,Aspaneus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Avcılar An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D2 Aspaneus"
1310,550463,Assos/Apollonia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Beyramkale An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C3 Assos/Apollonia"
1311,550467,Astyra?/Andeira?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Büyük Çal Tepe An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 E2 Astyra?/Andeira?"
1312,550470,Atarneus sub Pitanem,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gavurgör An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D4 Atarneus sub Pitanem"
1313,550486,Birytis?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mersinoba An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C2 Birytis?"
1314,550491,Caicus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bakır Çay The modern Bakırçay river, a river of southwest Asia Minor that flows from the Temnos mountains to the Elaitic Gulf."
1315,550497,Chios (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Khios GRE Chios is the fifth largest of the Greek islands.
1316,550500,Chrysa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Göztepe An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C2 Chrysa"
1317,550502,Chyton/Chytrion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: SW Urla An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D5 Chyton/Chytrion"
1318,550506,Cyme,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nemrut Limanı An ancient city of Aeolis.
1319,550512,Delphinion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Delphinion, on Khios GRE An ancient settlement and fortress on the island on Chios, mentioned in Thucydides."
1320,550525,Elaitikos Sinus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gulf of Çandarlı The Elaitic Gulf, now Çandarlı Körfezi."
1321,550528,Embaton,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Agrilya An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C5 Embaton"
1322,550533,Eresos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Skala, on Lesbos GRE An ancient settlement on the island of Lesbos (modern Skala Eresou), reputed birthplace of the poet Sappho. Strabo describes it as being built on a hill (presumably the modern Vigla) and extending down to the sea."
1323,550535,Erythrai,Erythrai was one of the twelve Ionian cities of Asia Minor. It is located on a small peninsula that extends into the Bay of Erythrae.
1324,550544,Gargara,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Arıklı An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D2 Gargara"
1325,550546,Gentinos?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ballı Dağ An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C2 Gentinos?"
1326,550547,Gergis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Karınkalı A city of the Troad in the territory of Lampsacus that Strabo describes as being ""rich in vines""."
1327,550548,Gergitha?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yirce An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 F3 Gergitha?"
1328,550555,Gryneion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Termaşalık Burnu An ancient coastal city of Aeolis where an oracle of Apollo was located. It was located near the modern Turkish village of Yenişakran (İzmir Province) and the small cape known as Termaşalık Burnu.
1329,550556,Gygaia/Koloe/Talaimenis (lake),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marmara Gölü An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 G4 Gygaia/Koloe/Talaimenis L."
1330,550562,Halisarna,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Eğrigöltepe Halisarna was a small center located in the border regions of Mysia and the Troad.
1331,550564,Halonnesos (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ag. Eustratios or Strati GRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 A2 Halonnesos Ins."
1332,550565,Hamaxitos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Beşiktepe, Gülpınar Hamaxitos was a Greek city in the southwestern Troad which was considered to mark the boundary between the Troad and Aeolis. Its territory included the temple of Apollo Smintheus."
1333,550567,Hekatonnesoi (islands),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yuntadası An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D3 Hekatonnesoi Inss."
1334,550569,Hephaistia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastro Bouni, on Lemnos GRE An ancient settlement on the northern coast of the Greek island of Lemnos."
1335,550571,Herakleia/Elateia?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Ayvalık An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D3 Herakleia/Elateia?"
1336,550575,Hermus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gediz Çay The Gediz River, the second largest of Anatolia's rivers, flows into the Aegean Sea."
1337,550585,Hypaipa,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Datbey Hypaipa or Hypaepa is an ancient city of Lydia located at the foot of Mount Aipos.
1338,550586,Hypokremnos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gülbahce An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D5 Hypokremnos"
1339,550587,Hyrcanius Campus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Manisa Ovası The modern Manisa Ovası (Manisa plain) in Turkey stretches to the north, west, and east of the modern city of Manisa (ancient Magnesia ad Sipylum). The plain surrounds the confluences of the Hyllos (modern Demirci Çay) and Pidasos (a tributary of Kum Çay) rivers with the Hermus (modern Gediz)."
1340,550594,Ilieon Kome,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Karatepe The ""VIllage of the Ilians"" located on the slopes of Karatepe in Asiatic Turkey."
1341,550595,Ilium/Troia,"The site of ancient Troy has been excavated by scholars of many nationalities, including by a joint team from the University of Tübingen and the University of Cincinnati. Their website, Projekt Troia , provided a wide variety of information about the site and the excavation in both English and German. The current excavator is Rüstem Aslan. The mound site of Hisarlık is generally believed to be the site of the Troy that features in Homer's Iliad . Settlement at the site began as an Early Bronze Age citadel in the third millennium B.C. An ancient city of northwest Anatolia with occupation ranging from the Bronze Age to the Roman period. Troy features as the central setting of Homer's Iliad."
1342,550597,Ionia (region),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Phocaea-Lebedos The coast of Asia Minor between Aeolis and Caria, known as Jaena/Jaman in Akkadian sources."
1343,550607,Kale Peuke,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: N Gürgen Dağ Kale Peuke (""beautiful pines"") was an ancient settlement of the Troad, located north of Gürgen Dağ. Strabo mentions the site in his text."
1344,550609,Kallikolone M.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Karatepe An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C2 Kallikolone M."
1345,550618,Kane/Autokane,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Acan Limanı (Bademli) An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D3 Kane/Autokane"
1346,550619,Kardamyle?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marmaron, on Khios GRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C4 Kardamyle?"
1347,550631,Kaukasa?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bolissos, on Khios GRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 B5 Kaukasa?"
1348,550633,Kebren,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Fuğla Tepe, Çal Dağ Kebren was an ancient settlement of the Troad."
1349,550635,Kenchreai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kayalı Dağ A ""Kenchreai in the Troad,"" mentioned first by Stephanus, is probably to be identified with a settlement of Roman date, located atop the Kayalı Dağ in modern Turkey."
1350,550644,Killaion (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Lesbos GRE A mountain on Lesbos.
1351,550648,Kisthene?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gömeç An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 D3 Kisthene?"
1352,550678,Lamponia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kozlu Dağ An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C2 Lamponia"
1353,550683,Buruncuk,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Buruncuk, in Aeolis An ancient city was located near modern Buruncuk, 28 km north of Smyrna (modern Izmir) in Turkey. Some scholars identify it as the ""Pelasgian"" Laris(s)a in Aeolis mentioned by Homer, Xenophon, Herodotus, and Strabo, but that identification is disputed."
1354,550684,Larisa,"For Barrington Atlas map 56, Foss and Mitchell place this Larisa to the north of the main stream of the Cayster river, on the rising terrain just east of the Çatal train station, citing only RE, which makes no mention of this location, but cites Buresch 1898. For New Pauly , Kaletsch also cites Buresch 1898 and follows him in placing Larisa 5km north-northwest of the Tire train station. This location is not only south of the Çatal station, but also south of the Cayster itself, adjacent to and including a pair of hills now bounded on the north by the Izmir-Ödemiş Road (D310) and split north-to-south by the continuation of the Karateke Road. Buresch, who visited the area in 1891, reported blocks of marble in nearby fields, a partial Hellenistic inscription, and remains of a ring wall on the hills. An ancient settlement known to Strabo as a village (formerly a city) in the territory of Ephesus, but closer to Mt. Tmolus than to Ephesus itself. Modern scholars differ as to the precise location of Larisa, but agree in placing it between modern Tire and modern Bayındır in Turkey."
1355,550685,Larisa/Ptolemais?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Limantepe, in Troad An ancient city of the Troad, located at modern Limantepe in Turkey."
1356,550691,Lekton/Lectum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bababurnu The Lekton promontory (modern Cape Baba or Baba Burnu) is the the westernmost point of Asia Minor.
1357,550692,Leleges,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: W Antandros Indigenous peoples of the Aegean littoral, mentioned in Classical and Hellenistic sources. Their historicity has been questioned."
1358,550696,Lesbos (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lesbos GRE Lesbos is an island of the northeastern Aegean Sea and was named for the son of Lapithos, the descendant of Deucalion and Helen."
1359,550722,Markaion (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kayalı Dağ Known today as Kayalı Dağ, this mountain is located in the modern Turkish province of Çanakkale."
1360,550726,Melaina Pr.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Karaburun An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C4 Melaina Pr."
1361,550738,Methymna,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Methymna, on Lesbos GRE An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 C3 Methymna"
