1386,550871,Scamander (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Menderes Çay A river of the Troad (modern Karamenderes River).
1387,550877,Sigeion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yenişehir Sigeion was located in the northwest part of the Troad at the mouth of the Scamander river.
1388,550879,Sigrion (promontory),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sigri GRE Modern Cape Sigri on the west coast of the Aegean island of Lesbos.
1389,550883,Simoeis (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dümruk Su. Many modern commentaries make the Simoeis a tributary of the Scamander, but BAtlas and RE explicitly do not. An intermittent stream attested in the Troad whose plain features as the scene of battles in Homer's account of the Trojan war. After Pliny, RE and BAtlas make the Simoeis a late, right tributary of the Xanthos. New Pauly asserts it was a tributary of the Scamander. The topography of the Scamander/Xanthos delta has changed significantly throughout antiquity and up to today by virtue of both natural processes and modern agricultural and hydraulic engineering. Modern commentators agree in identifying it with the modern Dümruk Su (i.e., Dümrek Çayı), despite the varying views of its ultimate confluence."
1390,550884,Sipylos/Manisa Dağı,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Manisa Dağı A mountain peak in modern Turkey, also known as Mount Sipylus, Mount Spil (or, in Turkish: Spil Dağı)."
1391,550890,Skepsis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kurşunlu Tepe An ancient settlement in the upper watershed of the Skamander (modern Karamenderes) river, located at and around the modern village of Kurşunlu in Turkey's Bayramiç (Çanakkale province). Significant archaeological remains, including a probable temple, on the dome-shaped hill immediately west of the modern village were reported in the 19th century, but these are no longer evident, the site having been quarried for construction in the village and in Bayramiç. Rescue excavations between the hill and the river revealed Roman and Byzantine-era structures, including a bath complex, in the late 20th century, prior to the filling of the reservoir created by the Bayramiç dam."
1392,550893,Smyrna/Eurydikeia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Izmir An ancient settlement of Asia Minor, modern Izmir in Turkey."
1393,550912,Tenedos (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bozcaada An island located in the Aegean Sea 5 km off the coast of Troas. Modern Bozcaada.
1394,550916,Teuthrania,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kalerga An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 E3 Teuthrania"
1395,550919,Thebe,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: 1 mile NNE Edremit An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 E2 Thebe"
1396,550920,Thebes Pedion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: plain of Edremit A fertile plain of Mysia, located south of Mount Placius."
1397,550927,Thymbra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: area of Akça Köy and Hanay Tepe Thymbra was a place in the Troad. It is most famous as the site of the sanctuary known as the Thymbraeum, a site sacred to Apollo Thymbraeus. In the Trojan War cycle, this is the place where Achilles reportedly ambushed and killed the Trojan prince Troilus. The nature of the site and its location are debated in the ancient sources. It was said to be located along the Thymbrios River, a tributary of the Scamander. It is referred to as a polis only by Stephanus of Byzantium. Other authors, including Strabo, use the term tópos or pedíon to describe Thymbra. It may be that Thymbra should be associated with an archaeological site south of Akçaköy, at least according to Cook."
1398,550929,Thymbras Pedion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: plain at confluence of Kemer Su and Menderes Çay A plain located south of Troy.
1399,550930,Thymbrios (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kemer Su A small river of the Troad and tributary of the Scamander.
