1408,554320,Pylaion (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Lesbos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 56 unlocated Pylaion M."
1409,560221,Aegaeum Mare,"This sea appears in the Barrington Atlas as map 57 grid C2, Aegaeum Mare. The Aegean Sea is the largest body of water between Greece and Asia Minor and an embayment of the Mediterranean Sea."
1410,560230,Antikythera (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Antikythera Aigila (Antikythera) is an island in the Aegean Sea located between Crete and the Peloponnese. BAtlas was confident in also associating the attested name Ogylos with Antikythera, but New Pauly is less confident, listing Cythera as an alternative."
1411,560353,Cyclades Islands,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: island-group An island group in the Aegean Sea composed of over 200 islands.
1412,570028,Achaea/Achaia (region),An ancient Greek region on the northern coast of the Peloponnese.
1413,570030,Acheron (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ladikon An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 B2 Acheron fl."
1414,570040,Aigai,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akrata An ancient settlement of Achaia in the Peloponnese. Modern Akrata.
1415,570043,Aigeira,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Vitrinitsa An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 C1 Aigeira"
1416,570049,Aigion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aigion A city-state of ancient Achaea that numbered as one of the 12 Achaean cities. The city was located 30 stades from Rhypae, and 40 stades from Helice."
1417,570050,Aigiplanktos/Geran(e)ia (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mt. Gerenion This mountain, mentioned in ancient sources, is to be identified with the modern Gerenion, northeast of the city of Loutraki."
1418,570051,Aigosthena,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Porto Germano Aigosthena was a fortified city of in the Megaris that served as one of Megara's ports. In the Hellenistic period, it became an autonomous city."
1419,570057,Ak(r)itas Pr.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cape Akritas An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 B4 Ak(r)itas Pr."
1420,570058,Akriai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kokkinia Akriai or Acriae was a settlement of ancient Laconia, located to the south of the modern Greek village of Kokkinia (municipality of Evrotas). According to Strabo, the Eurotas river flows into the sea between Akriai and Gythium."
1421,570063,Alesiai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Ag. Ioannis An ancient settlement of Lakonia, mentioned by Pausanias and placed by him to the southwest of Sparta. It is probably to be localized in the vicinity of modern Ag. Ioannis."
1422,570065,Alipheira,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Alipheira Pausanias notes that Alipheira was mostly abandoned when Arcadians were merged into Megalopolis.
1423,570070,Aminios (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ligatares An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 C3 Aminios fl."
1424,570078,Anchisia (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kouvelaki Modern Kouvelaki, a hill in Arcadia separating the plain of Orchomenos and the plain of Mantineia."
1425,570088,Apelauron,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Phlious-Stymphalos pass An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 C2 Apelauron"
1426,570089,Aperopia (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dokos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 E3 Aperopia Ins."
1427,570090,Apesas (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Phoukas Mount Aphesas, now Mount Phoukas, separated the territory of Kleonai from the Corinthia. It is connected with the myth of the Nemean lion and was the site of an ash altar sacred to Zeus in use from the Geometric period to the fourth century B.C."
1428,570094,Aphrodision (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aphrodision Aphrodision.
1429,570098,Arachnaion (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mt. Arachnaion A mountain of the Argolid with a higher western peak (Profitis Ilias, 1199 masl) and a lower eastern peak (Mavrovouni or Arna, 1139 masl). Material from the Mycenaean, Archaic, and Classical periods has been found on the western peak and identified with altars of Zeus and Hera described by Pausanias. Aeschylus mentions its use as a signal station."
1430,570101,Araxos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cape Araxos Cape Araxos separates the Gulf of Patras from the Ionian Sea.
1431,570102,Arcadia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arkadia The ancient region of Arcadia comprised the highlands at the center of the Peloponnese.
1432,570105,Argon Pedion,"Argon Pedion, the ""untilled"" or ""unworked"" plain, is a seasonal lakebed located in the Arcadian highlands in the Peloponnese to the west of Nestane, Arkadia."
1433,570106,Argos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Argos Argos was a city of ancient Greece that reached its cultural highpoint under Pheidon in the seventh century BC.
1434,570112,Aroania (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mt. Aroania Aroania M. is the third highest mountain of the Peloponnese (2,355 m asl)."
1435,570113,Aroanios (Arcadia: river above Psophis),"BAtlDir has clearly mixed Pausanias citations and possibly other information between the various Arcadian rivers with this name. The modern name/location glosses therein cannot be correlated with modern digital gazetteers. This record has been adjusted for Pausanias citations after Hirschfeld in RE and by correlating BAtlas placement with Pausanias' descriptions. Three rivers of Arcadia bearing this name are mentioned in ancient sources. According to Pausanias, this Aroanios rises in the ancient Erymanthos mountains and flows southward to join other tributaries of the Erymanthos river at ancient Psophis. BAtlas labels the modern Nousaitiko, which matches Pausanias' description."
1436,570121,Artemision (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Artemision A mountain located between Argolis and Arcadia, with a temple of Artemis at its summit."
1437,570122,Asea,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Asea Asea was a town of Arcadia, located in the district Maenalia. Lying near the frontier of Laconia, Asea was to be found along the road from Megalopolis to Pallantium and Tegea. Asea participated in the foundation of Megalopolis and many of its inhabitants relocated there."
1438,570123,Asinaeus/Coronaeus Sinus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Messiniakos Kolpos Asinaeus/Coronaeus Sinus (the Messenian Gulf).
1439,570124,Asine,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tolon/Asine Pausanias reports that while defending the fortified town the people of Asine killed Lysistratus of Argos. The city eventually fell and the Argives leveled it. They left the sanctuary of Apollo Pythaeus standing and there buried Lysistratus. Asine was an ancient Greek city of Argolis.
1440,570126,Asine,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Skoutari? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 D4 Asine"
1441,570129,Asopos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Plytra An ancient settlement located in a protected bay on the west coast of the Parnon peninsula (Peloponnese), now occupied by modern Plitra. The modern town that now carries the name ""Asopos"" lies some 5 kilometers north of ancient Asopos (modern Plitra)."
1442,570131,Asopos (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Asopos A river of the Peloponnesus that originated in the mountains to the south of Phleious and flowed to the Corinthian gulf.
1443,570133,Asterion (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river A wet-weather watercourse in the Argolid, mentioned by Pausanias. Camp in BAtlas marks it on map 58 on a path followed by an intermittent watercourse mapped in OSM and labeled there as ""Taksiarchis""."
1444,570134,Astron,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Paralio Astros An ancient settlement mentioned by Ptolemy and thought to have been located in the vicinity of the modern coastal town of Paralio Astros. The prominent remains of the castle immediately above the town date to the 17th or 18th century CE and are unrelated to both the ancient settlement and the Aeginetan fortification mentioned by Thucydides that some scholars associate with ancient Astron (RE).
1445,570137,Athenaion (settlement),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Athenaion/Alika, ESE Megalopolis An ancient settlement of Arcadia mentioned by Pausanias. Its precise location cannot be determined today, but it probably lay in the vicinity of the modern town that has acquired its name (Athinaio, formerly Alika)."
1446,570143,Azania,An ancient region in the Peloponnese.
1447,570146,Balyra (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mavrozoumeno/Basiliko A river of Messenia mentioned in ancient sources. The Balyra corresponds to the modern Mavrozoumena in Greece, in particular to a section stretching from modern Neokhori to a confluence with the Pamisos."
1448,570147,Basilis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: E Kyparissia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 C3 Basilis"
1449,570149,Bathos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Mauria and Kyparissia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 C3 Bathos"
1450,570150,Belbina (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ag. Georgios A small island in the Saronic Gulf, some 10 miles from the promontory of Sounion."
1451,570151,Belmina,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Chelmos Mentioned in ancient sources, this settlement of ancient Arcadia was perhaps a polis during the Classical period. It lay near the border with Laconia and Shipley argues it was seized and controlled by Sparta from the mid-sixth to fourth centuries BCE, again in the third century, and from the mid-to-late second century BCE well into the Roman imperial period. Its precise location is uncertain, but there are multiple candidate sites on the slopes of modern Mt. Chelmos (atop which the Athenaion fort stood) in the vicinity of modern Skortsinos and modern Giakoumaiika (enumerated by Shipley 2000 on page 373 note 45)."
1452,570156,Boia(i),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Neapolis An ancient settlement on the west coast of the Parnon peninsula (Peloponnese), now the site of modern Neapolis."
1453,570157,Bolinaios (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Drepanaiiko or Platanaiiko A river located in northern Achaea that drains into the Gulf of Corinth.
1454,570158,Boreion (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mt. Gravari A mountain in Arcadia above Asea, perhaps Mt. Gravari."
1455,570166,Kastro ano Diakopto,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kastro?, near Diakofto Abundant spolia, artifacts, and building foundations provide evidence for an ancient settlement in the area of modern Kastro ano Diakopto and the church of Ag. Athanasios on the hill above it to the west. It is probably to be identified with ancient Boura, an attested settlement of Achaia."
1456,570168,Brenthe,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Karytaina Brenthe was a city in Arcadia that was reportedly in ruins in Pausanias' time. It was located close to the right bank of the river Alpheius.
1457,570170,Chaa?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kalydona An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 B3 Chaa?"
1458,570171,Chaon (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Megavouni Mentioned by Pausanias, this mountain is securely identified with the modern Megavouni (Greece)."
1459,570172,Charadros (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Belbitsianiko/Bozaitiko An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 B1 Charadros fl."
1460,570173,Charadros (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Xeria An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 D2 Charadros fl."
1461,570182,Corinthus/Korinthos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Archaia Korinthos The ancient Greek and Roman city of Corinth, located in the Peloponnese, Greece. Also known today as Archaia Korinthos and not to be confused with the nearby modern town."
1462,570186,Cythera (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kythera An Aegean island located off the southeastern peninsula of the Peloponnese. It appears frequently in Greek and Roman literature beginning with Homer.
1463,570197,Dine,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: whirlpool S Kiveri The whirlpool Dine, located in the Argolic Gulf, is discussed by Pausanias in a discussion of the flow of water in the Argolid."
1464,570202,Dorion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Malthi A fortified hilltop settlement overlooking the Soulima Valley in northern Messenia. Dorion appears in the Catalogue of Ships in Homer's Iliad.
1465,570203,Drepanon (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Drepano The modern Cape Drepano, located at the western narrowing of the Corinthian Gulf, just east of Rhion/Antirrhion."
1466,570215,Elektra (river of Messenia),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Chouchlotos An ancient river mentioned only by Pausanias who describes it, along with the Koios, as lying between Andania and Kyparissia in Messenia. RE declines to identify it with a specific modern river. BAtlas 58 labels a river stretch corresponding to the middle course of the modern Mavrozoumena river as mapped and named in OpenStreetMap, but in BAtlDir the corresponding modern name / location is given as ""Chouchlotos"", which does not appear in GeoNames, OpenStreetMap, Wikidata, or elsewhere on the web."
1467,570220,Elis (city),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Elis Elis was an ancient Greek city of the northwest Peloponnese that served as the capital of the district of the same name.
1468,570225,Enipeus (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lestenitsa Located in ancient Elis, the Enipeus was a right tributary of the Alpheius, which it joined downstream (west) of ancient Olympia. It has been identified with the modern Lestenitsas."
1469,570227,Ephyra? (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Antimelos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 58 G4 Ephyra? Ins."
1470,570228,Epidauros,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Palaia Epidauros Epidauros is located on a small peninsula on the eastern coast of Peloponnesus.
