1820,579907,Dekeleia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: deme and fort, near Tatoi [VIII - inland - 4(5?)] Dekeleia was an inland deme of the tribe Hippothontis, in Attica, located 120 stades from Athens. In myth, Dekeleia ceded its autonomy under Theseus to form the city-state of Athens."
1821,579912,Diomeia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Diomeian gate, SE of wall [II(XII) - coast* - 1] Diomeia was a deme of Attica, and it was located both within and outside the walls of Themistocles at Athens. The deme was named after the hero Diomos, a son of Kollytos."
1822,579916,Eitea (Antiochis),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Grammatiko [X - city* - 2/1] Eitea was an Attic deme of the tribe Antiochis, probably located at the site of modern-day Grammatiko."
