1858,580006,Lakiadai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sacred Way, E Kephisos [VI - city - 2(3)] Lakiadai was a deme of ancient Attica located on the Sacred Way between Sciron and the Cephissus."
1859,580009,Lamptrai Paraloi/Lamptrai Hypenerthen,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kitsi [I - coast - 9(10)] Lamptrai Paraloi (""coastal Lamptrai"") or Lamptrai Hypenerthen (""Lower Lamptrai"") was a coastal deme in the tribe of Erechtheis in Attica. The epithets distinguished it from neighbouring Lamptrai Kathyperthen (""Upper Lamptrai""), which was also in Erechtheis. For some purposes the two demes seem to have operated as a single entity."
1860,580010,Laureion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Laureion region and silver mines. A mining region of Attika famous in antiquity for its silver mines. These mines were one of the chief sources of revenue for the Athenian state.
1861,580011,Leipsydrion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gaitana? Leipsydrion is the site of an ancient fortification dated to the fourth century BCE. The fort was located on the Korakofoleza hill (580 m.) on Mt. Parnitha, Attiki."
1862,580018,Makaria,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: spring near Trikorynthos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 59 D2 Makaria"
1863,580021,Marathon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Plasi NW (prehistoric acropolis?) [IX - coast - 10] Marathon was a coastal deme of the tribe Aiantis, in northeastern Attica, the site of the Battle of Marathon in 490 BC, and capital of the Marathon tetrapolis."
1864,580029,Mounichia Limen,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tourkolimani An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 59 B3 Mounichia Limen"
1865,580030,Myrrinous,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Merenda [III - coast - 6(8)] Myrrhinous was a coastal deme of the tribe Pandionis in Atticia, located at modern Merenda"
1866,580035,Nike (Monument at Marathon),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mesosporitissa Chapel A monument to the Battle of Marathon. Eugene Vanderpool describes the location of the monument as lying in the northeastern part of the plain of Marathon. A Christian chapel known as the Panagia Mesosporitissa marks the vicinity of this monument. In the area of the chapel are a well that reuses ancient marble and the ruins of a medieval tower that was built from re-used ancient blocks, including a massive Ionic column capital and two large column drums."
1867,580038,Oe?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: NE Aspropyrgos by Kallistiri [VI - coast - 6] An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 59 B2 Oe?"
1868,580040,Oinoe (Attica),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: deme and fort, Ninoi [IX - coast - 4] An ancient fortified settlement and deme of Attica. The archaeological site is located to the east of modern Oinoe/Inoi (formerly Mazi)."
1869,580044,Oropos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Skala Oropou Oropos was an ancient settlement and port located on the border between Boeotia and Attica; the bulk of its Hellenistic - Roman remains are thought to underlie the modern village of Skala Oropou. Oropos was source of ongoing disputes in antiquity. Colonists from Eretria founded Oropos.
1870,580051,Pallene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Stavros Byzantine chapel [X - inland - 6/7(9)] Pallene was an ancient mesogeia deme of the phyle Antiochis in Attica, located at Stavros in what is now the Gerakas suburb of Athens."
1871,580053,Panakton (Attika),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kavasala A fort located at the frontier between Attica and Boeotia, on the slopes of Mt. Cithaeron."
1872,580056,Cave of Pan and the Nymphs,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: N Vari A small cave, located northeast of Vari in Attica, Greece, that was used as a shrine dedicated to Apollo, Pan, and the Nymphs from the sixth to the second centuries BC."
1873,580059,Parnes (mountain),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Parnes A densely forested mountain range located to the north of Athens.
