1911,582868,Eetioneia Pr.,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: NW Megas Limen A fortified promontory located on the western side of the main port of Piraeus.
1912,582869,Kolonos Hippios,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ag. Eleousa, Kolonos Pausanias states that the Kolonos Hippios is a hill that marks the first point that Oedipus reached in Attica. It was a sacred area with rites devoted to both Poseidon and Athena, as well as a hero shrine sacred to Peirithous and Theseus, Oedipus and Adrastus. Antigonus destroyed the shrine of Poseidon."
1913,582870,Ardettos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ardettos A site to the southeast of Athens, where jurors swore their oath, named for a hero Ardettes."
1914,585128,Makra Teiche,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Makra Teiche, Athens-Peiraieus railway The Makra Teiche or ""Long Walls"" of Athens were built in the middle of the fifth century B.C. and connected Athens to its ports at Piraeus and Phalerum. They were destroyed by the Spartans after the Athenian surrender in 403 B.C., and rebuilt during the beginning of the Corinthian War, which started in 395 B.C. The walls were ultimately destroyed by Sulla during his siege against Athens in 86 B.C."
1915,585129,Phalerikon Teichos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Phalerikon Teichos, Leophoros Syngrou Mentioned by Thucydides, the ""Phaleric Wall"" (a third major component of Athens' ""Long Walls"") extended toward Phaleron, but became obsolete already late in the Peloponnesian War. It may have been destroyed or allowed to deteriorate and was subsequently spoliated and reused for building matieral: no trace of this fortification has been identified in modern times."
1916,585892,Auridai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: [VIII - coast? - 1] Auridai was a deme of ancient Attica. It originally belonged to the phyle of Hippothontis, but later to Antigonis."
1917,585897,Bothynos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Hiera Hodos, E Athens A locality along the sacred way to Eleusis from Athens. Its precise location has not been determined."
