2025,599886,Phorbia? Pr.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akrotiri, on Mykonos GRE A promontory of northeastern Mykonos. The Barrington Atlas compilers indicate ""Akrotiri"" as the modern toponym, a convention that is then followed by Topostext. Other modern gazetteers suggest that the cape may also be known as Ákra Évros (Άκρα Εύρος)."
2026,599888,Physkos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Marmaris An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 G4 Physkos"
2027,599892,Pisye,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pissiköy An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 G3 Pisye"
2028,599905,Priene/‘Lince’?,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Güllübahçe Priene/‘Lince’? was an ancient Greek city of Ionia on an escarpment of Mycale Mons.
2029,599919,Rheneia (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Rheneia GRE A Cycladic island just west of Delos in the Aegean Sea. It consists of two parts linked by a narrow isthmus.
2030,599926,Samos (island),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Samos GRE The island of Samos.
2031,599932,Selinousia (lake),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gebekilise Gölü A wetland in the neighborhood of Ephesos, mentioned by Strabo, from which the temple of Artemis derived income. Modern scholars identify it with the modern Lake Gebekirse."
2032,599943,Sounion (Paros),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Paros GRE The northeastern tip of the island of Paros.
2033,599947,Stratonikeia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Eskihisar An ancient city in Caria founded by Antiochus I Soter, according to Strabo. The city had several phases, including a period of Rhodian influence, before coming under the umbrella of Roman administration. The city was elaborately embellished with monumental programs of public art and architecture."
2034,599948,Syangela,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kaplan Dağ An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 F3 Syangela"
2035,599951,Syme (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Syme GRE An island located off the coast of Caria, some 24 km north of Rhodes."
2036,599953,Syrna,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bayır An ancient settlement in Caria. Epigraphic finds place it somewhere in the vicinity of the modern village of Bayır in Turkey's Muğla province.
2037,599957,Teichioussa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Kazıklı An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 E3 Teichioussa"
2038,599969,Theangela,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Etrim An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 F3 Theangela"
2039,599973,Thera (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Santorini GRE An island of the southern Aegean Sea, Thera is the southernmost of the Cyclades. The island's present form is the result of a Middle Bronze Age volcanic eruption that destroyed a Minoan settlement on the island."
2040,599988,Triopion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kumyer An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 E4 Triopion"
2041,599991,Trogilion (promontory),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dip Burnu The modern Dip Burun in Turkey: a coastal promontory opposite the Aegean island of Samos extending from the Mykale mountains.
2042,606274,Asopos (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Paros Strabo includes the now-unlocated Asopos river of Paros among other similarly named rivers discussed in his geography. This river of Paros is the fourth of four rivers enumerated at 8.6.24.
2043,606293,Epibomous,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Rhodos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 unlocated Epibomous?"
2044,606356,Phylincheia regio/ Phylincheia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arkesine An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 unlocated Phylincheia (regio)"
2045,606358,Prinassos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in Rhodian Peraea An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 unlocated Prinassos"
2046,606377,Zephyrion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Myndos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 61 unlocated Zephyrion"
2047,609289,Abrettene,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Rhyndakos fl. and Makestos fl. Abrettene was a region in northern Mysia.
2048,609292,Aizanoi,Current Archaeology in Turkey places the site with the modern Turkish province of Kütahya . Aizanoi was an ancient Roman city located in western Anatolia.
2049,609302,Amorion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Hisar Köy A Hellenistic and Roman settlement that eventually prospered under the Byzantine Empire, Amorion began minting its own coins in the second century BCE. Its decline comes following a sacking in the ninth century at the hands of Caliph Al-Mu'tasim."
2050,609305,Ancyra (Phrygia),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Boğaz Köy, formerly Kilise Köy An ancient settlement of Phrygia, near the origin of the Mekestos river. Heavily spoliated, especially since the mid-19th century, and now cultivated, a Byzantine fortress on the hill immediately east of modern Boğazköy in Turkey's Kütahya province is uniformly identified with this ancient Ankyra. Many modern commentators have adopted the name ""Ancyra Sidera"" on the basis of an entry for a bishop in Latin and Syriac versions of the attendees the Council of Chalcedon (missing from the Greek witnesses), but Price 2010 asserts it may be a corruption of a lost original entry for a bishop of ""Ancyra and Synaos""."
2051,609327,Atyochorion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akkent, formerly Zeyve? An ancient settlement in Phrygia on the Maeander river that was known to have sanctuaries dedicated to Apollo Lairmenos and to Zeus Trossou. It was probably located at or near modern Akkent in Çal, Turkey."
2052,609360,Dindymon/Agdistis/Agdus (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Arayit (Günüzü) Dağı The modern Arayit (Günüzü) Dağı in Turkey, below the western slopes of which lay ancient Pessinous (modern Ballıhisar)."
2053,609393,Gordion/Vindia?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yassıhüyük. Gordion was included in the UNESCO Tentative List of World Heritage in 2012; in September 2023 the site was listed a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Alfred and Gustav Körte carried out the first excavations at the site in 1900 under the auspices of the German Archaeological Institute, with Alfred Körte having identified the site in 1893. Excavation resumed in 1950 under Rodney S. Young (University of Pennsylvania). Excavations under Young continued through 1973. With Young's death in 1974, excavations lapsed until 1988 when they continued under Mary Voigt. Voigt's campaigns concluded in 2005. The capital city of ancient Phrygia, the ruins of Gordion are located near the modern village of Yassıhüyük about 10km east of the Turkish city of Polatlı."
2054,609415,Ipsos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Çayırbağ, formerly Sipsin An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 62 E4 Ipsos"
2055,609426,Kastolou Pedion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Burçak Ovası An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 62 A4 Kastolou Pedion"
2056,609427,Katakekaumene,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S portion of middle Hermos valley An area of western Asia Minor characterized by its ashy soil and volcanic rocks.
2057,609473,Midaion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Karahüyük A city in Phrygia named after its legendary founder, king Midas."
2058,609481,Mysoi Abbaeitai,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: at both sides of Demirci or Simav Dağı One of two Attalid-era regional polities located in the northeast part of modern Turkey's Manisa province (the other being the Maionians in Katakekaumene).
2059,609500,Pessinous/Iustinianoupolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ballıhisar An ancient city of Asia Minor, today Ballıhisar in Turkey."
2060,609501,Philomelion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akşehir An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 62 F5 Philomelion"
2061,609538,Synnada,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Şuhut Synnada, a Hellenistic and Roman crossroads town in Phrygia, present-day Şuhut, Turkey."
2062,609564,Tyr(i)aion?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: region of Ilgın An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 62 G5 Tyr(i)aion?"
2063,614696,Keramon Agora,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Peltai and Kaystrou Pedion Keramon Agora is a ""populated place"" mentioned only by Xenophon. It is located in the plain of the Cayster but is now unable to be located."
2064,614706,Kobedyle,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in Burçak Ovası, NE Philadelpheia A settlement mentioned in ancient sources that cannot be precisely located today. It was probably located in the area of modern Turkey called Burçak Ovası (ancient Kastolou Pedion), northeast of Philadelpheia (modern Alaşehir)."
2065,614844,Syreanos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: epithet of Zeus in “Highlands” near Eiokome An ancient sanctuary of Zeus attested in inscriptions found on ""the upland plain of Makas Alan between the [Phrygian] cities of Kotiaeion (Kiitahya) and Nakoleia (Seyitgazi)"" in modern Turkey (Drew-Bear). Its precise location cannot be determined today."
2066,614870,Trikomia 3,"Ptolemy places this Trikomia in Φρυγία Μεγἀλης (Greater Phrygia), listing it between Μιδάϊον (Midaion) and Ἄγκυρα Φρυγίας (Phrygian Ankyra, i.e. modern Ankara) . The Peutinger Map places it on a route network between ""Mideo"" and ""Pesinvnte"" (i.e., Midaion and Pessinous ). "" Dorileo "" is one route segment leftward of ""Mideo"" on the Peutinger Map; therefore, the BAtlas Directory localizes Trikomia 3 ""between Dorylaion and Pessinous"". NB: A Wikipedia entry entitled ""Tricomia (Phrygia)"" conflates this Trikomia with another ( Pleiades 609560 ) that may have been located at modern İlkburun in Turkey. Such a view is implicitly rejected by RE and, following it, BAtlas and Pleiades . One of several ""Trikomiai"" attested in the ancient Greek and Roman world. This presumed settlement cannot be located today, but probably lay in what is today the country of Turkey, somewhere between Dorylaion (Eskişehir) and Pessinous (Ballıhisar)."
2067,619103,Ancyra (Galatia),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ankara An ancient settlement and later fortified settlement that served as the seat of the koinon of Galatia and capital of the subsequent Roman province. Modern Ankara in Turkey.
2068,619137,Cham(m)anene,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 63 D2 Cham(m)anene"
2069,619161,Galatia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: central Asia Minor An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 63 B1 Galatia"
2070,619164,Garsaura/Col. Claudia Archelais/Koloneia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aksaray Originally known as Garsaura, this place was a key settlement of the strategia of Garsaouritis. King Archelaus refounded it and named it after himself. Under the Roman emperor Claudius the site received colonial status. The settlement is located at an oasis to the southeast of Tuz Gölü and some 225 km south of Ancyra."
2071,619167,Gorbeous,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: SE Oğulbey A settlement south of Ankara that served as the seat of Castor the Elder, the tetrarch of the Tectosages."
2072,619194,Koropassos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akhan An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 63 D4 Koropassos"
2073,619244,Kerkenes/Pteria?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kerkenes Dağ A large, fortified Phrygian settlement dating to the 7th/6th centuries BCE on modern Kerkenes Dağ in Turkey's Yozgat province. It is probably to be identified with a ""Pteria"" mentioned by Herodotus as the site of a battle between Cyrus and Croesus."
2074,619254,Saraouene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: around Kırşehir An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 63 E2 Saraouene"
2075,619267,Taouion/Tabia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Büyük Nefes A key junction between roads north (towards Hattusa and the Lykos River valley), south (towards Mazaka), east (towards the upper Halys River), and west (towards Ankyra), it was inhabited from the fourth millennium BCE. Greek and Roman writers mention it as the main settlement of the Galatian tribe of the Trocmi."
2076,619268,Tatta (lake),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tuz Göl Tatta L. (Lake Tuz or Tuz Gölü) is one of the largest of the world's hypersaline lakes and the second largest lake in Turkey.
2077,619275,Trokmoi,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: around Taouion An ancient Celtic people who likely migrated into Anatolia in the early third century BCE.
2078,622134,Drynemeton,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Galatian religious centre An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 63 unlocated Drynemeton"
2079,622156,Mithridation,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Trokmian stronghold Described by Strabo as a ""walled fortress"" ""which Pompey gave to Bogodiatarus""."
2080,628932,Argaeus (mountain),"Argaeus Mons is commonly depicted on coins from Cappadocia . Casearea , just north of the mountain, hosted a mint. The reverse side of the coin (1983.159.38) depicted in the image above, used by permission of the American Numismatic Society, bears a representation of Argaeus Mons. Erciyes Dağı in Turkish. A massive volcanic mountain, the highest peak of the Antitaurus range. Commonly depicted on coins from Cappadocia."
2081,628934,Ariaratheia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Pınarbaşı, formerly Azizie An ancient settlement in Asia Minor, located at modern Pınarbaşı (former Aziziye/Azizie) in Turkey."
2082,628942,Bagadania,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: land S Argaeus M. An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 64 B4 Bagadania"
2083,628959,Comana/Hierapolis/Chryse,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Şar Comana/Hierapolis/Chryse was an ancient city in Cappadocia.
2084,628972,Euagina/Sebagena?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: vicinity of Gemerek An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 64 C3 Euagina/Sebagena?"
2085,629004,Kataonia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: around Comana Kataonia was a regional subdivision of Cappadocia.
2086,629019,Kouloupene (region),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: around Sebasteia and Sebastopolis An ancient region of eastern Asia Minor, lying on the border of Armenia Minor and encompassing the cities of Sebasteia and Sebastopolis."
2087,629035,Mazaka/Eusebeia/Caesarea,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kayseri Mazaka/Eusebeia/Caesarea was the chief town of Cappadocia, modern Kayseri."
2088,629054,Phiara,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yıldızeli/Yeni Han? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 64 D1 Phiara"
2089,638736,Akalissos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Asar Deresi An ancient settlement of Lycia, located near the modern village of Karacaören in Turkey's Kumluca municipality (Antalya province)."
2090,638741,Amblada,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Hisartepe, near Kızılca An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 G2 Amblada"
2091,638749,Antiphellos/Habesos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kaş Antiphellos/Habesos was an ancient settlement in Lycia.
2092,638751,Apamea/Kelainai/Kibotos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dinar Apamea/Kelainai/Kibotos (modern Dinar) was an ancient settlement of Phrygia located at a road junction linking Ionia to the east. In 333 BCE, Alexander the Great installed his general Antigonos as satrap of Phrygia."
2093,638753,Aphrodisias,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Geyre, Turkey (nearby) Aphrodisias was a Greek city located in Caria. It is notable especially for the state of its archaeological preservation. The site was inscribed on UNESCO's World Heritage List in July 2017."
2094,638766,Artemision? Pr.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akça Burnu An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 A4 Artemision? Pr."
2095,638778,Attalea,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Antalya Now the modern Antalya, Attalea was established in 150 B.C. on the coast of Pamphylia by Attalus II."
2096,638785,Aunesis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: below Hamaxia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 G4 Aunesis"
2097,638796,Caunus,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dalyan An ancient city of Caria in southwest Asia Minor.
2098,638801,Chimaira,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Avlan valley Strabo places the myth of the Lycian monster Chimaira in the Kragos mountains, noting a nearby chasm or ravine (φάραγξ) named after the monster that he says extends up from the shore. BAtlas 65 places the label ""Chimaera"" along the low pass between the Kragos and Antikragos, i.e., from the modern Sancaklık Koyu (where the ancient settlement of Kalabantia was located) to roughly the area of the modern village of Boğaziçi in Turkey's Seydikemer district (Muğla Province). A western neighborhood of that village, Avlan, preserves the modern name of the ravine identified in RE Kragos 1, ""Tal von Alwan"" (sic) and in BAtlDir: ""Avlan valley""."
2099,638804,Cibyra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Horzum Located near the modern village of Gölhisar. An ancient Greek city, largely rebuilt in the early Roman empire after a major earthquake. Kibyra is located near the modern town of Göhlisar (formerly Horzum) in Turkey's Burdur province."
2100,638811,Colossae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Honaz An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 B2 Colossae"
2101,638814,Cremna/Col. Iulia Augusta Felix,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Girme, Çamlık A significant Hellenistic settlement of Pisidia that received an Augustan colony, taking the name Colonia Iulia Augusta Felix Cremnensium."
2102,638853,Hamaxia?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sinekkalesi? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 G4 Hamaxia?"
2103,638891,Kalynda,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kozpınar A settlement attested in ancient literature. Despite some suggestions to the contrary, scholarly consensus continues to identify Kalynda with an archaeological site at the modern village of Şerefler (formerly Kozpınar) in Turkey's Muğla province."
2104,638897,Karalis (lake),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Beyşehir Göl Karalis (modern Lake Beyşehir or Beyşehir Gölü) is a large freshwater lake in Asia Minor.
2105,638900,Karmylessos?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kaya An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 B4 Karmylessos?"
2106,638922,Kissidai? Pr.,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: İblis Burnu An ancient place, mentioned in the so-called Stadiasmus Maris Magni, that was probably a promontory (cape) and can likely be identified with the modern İblis Burnu (Cape İblis/Cape Angistro on the Turkish coast of the Gulf of Fethiye)."
2107,638926,Klimax,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: N Phaselis An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 E4 Klimax"
2108,638939,Korykos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on bay below Chimaera An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 D5 Korykos"
2109,638947,Krya,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Taşyaka An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 A4 Krya"
2110,638958,Letoon,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: SW Xanthos Sanctuary of Leto and center of the Lycian federation.
2111,638961,Limyra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: NE Finike. There are remains of an impressive Roman bridge nearby. An ancient settlement located at modern Yuvalılar in the district of Finike, Antalya province, Turkey."
2112,638990,Melanippeia (island),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Devecitaşı Ada. According to a fragment of Favorinus quoted by Stephanus of Byzantium, Melanippeia was one of two islets that constituted the ""Chelidoniai petrai"". These ""rocks"" are thought to be identifiable with the largest two of several islets that lie off modern Cape Gelidonya on the south coast of Anatolia (the modern Beş Adalar = Five Islands). Melanippeia is perhaps to be identified with the largest of the group, modern Devecitaşı Ada."
2113,639046,Pednelissos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kozan Pednelissos was an ancient settlement in Pisidia near to the Eurymedon river.
2114,639051,Phaselis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tekirova An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 E4 Phaselis"
2115,639087,Sagalassos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: above Ağlasun Sagalassos is an ancient city in Pisidia that was later part of the Roman province of Galatia. The city flourished until natural disasters and disease led to its decline in the sixth century AD.
2116,639100,Selge,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zerk between Selge and Cagrai Selge was an important city of Pisidia; Strabo claims it was founded as a Spartan colony.
2117,639105,Side,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Selimiye An ancient settlement on the southern coast of Asia Minor (modern Selimiye in Turkey).
2118,639139,Termessos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Güllük Dağ An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 D4 Termessos"
2119,639147,Tlos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Düver An ancient settlement of Lykia, located two kilometers due east of the modern village of Düğer in Turkey's Mugla province."
2120,639149,Trapezopolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Boli An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 A2 Trapezopolis"
2121,639154,Trogitis (lake),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Suğla Göl The modern Lake Suğla is a lake located in Turkey's Konya Province.
2122,639166,Xanthos,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kınık An ancient city of Lycia.
2123,639167,Xanthos/Sirbis (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Esen Çay An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 B5 Xanthos/Sirbis fl."
2124,642536,Kragos (settlement),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Mt. Kragos Strabo mentions a settlement named Kragos located on the eponymous mountain in Lycia. The settlement's precise location cannot be determined today.
2125,642565,Themisonion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Karayük plain An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 65 unlocated Themisonion"
2126,648554,Aleion Pedion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: plain E Adana Aleion Pedion (the Aleian Plain) in Cilicia.
2127,648593,Calycadnus (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river The most important river of Cilicia Tracheia.
2128,648614,Cybistra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ereğli An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 66 E1 Cybistra"
2129,648620,Derbe/Claudioderbe,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kerti Hüyük An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 66 C2 Derbe/Claudioderbe"
2130,648624,Dionysophanes,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: E Kızılburun An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 66 C4 Dionysophanes"
2131,648635,Germanicopolis/Clibanus?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ermenek An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 66 B3 Germanicopolis/Clibanus?"
2132,648647,Iconium/Claudiconium,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Konya Iconium/Claudiconium (modern Konya) was an important city in Lycaonia that later became part of the Roman province of Galatia.
2133,648651,Isaura (Nova)/Leontopolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zengibar Kalesi An ancient settlement, modern Zengibar Kalesi in Turkey."
2134,648670,Kilikiai Pylai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Gülek Bogazi The Cilician Gates, a pass through the Taurus Mountains connecting Cilicia to the Anatolian Plateau, by way of the gorge of the Gökoluk River."
2135,648692,Lamos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Limonlu (Lamas) Çay. An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 66 E3 Lamos fl."
2136,648699,Lystra/Col. Iulia Felix Gemina,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Hatunsaray An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 66 A1 Lystra/Col. Iulia Felix Gemina"
2137,648701,Magarsa/Antiochia ad Pyramum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dörtdirek, near Karataş Magarsa was a settlement in eastern Cilicia, located on the Karataş Peninsula near the mouth of the river Pyramus. In the Hellenistic period, it was re-founded as Antiochia ad Pyramum."
2138,648704,Ma(m)psoukrenai,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kırıtlar? A place located in the eastern part of Cilicia on the river Cydnus.
2139,648717,Nagidos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bozyazı An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 66 B4 Nagidos"
2140,648740,Platanistes,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: coastline between Charadros and Anemurium Strabo characterizes this stretch of rugged/rocky coastline (παράπλους τραχὺς) as lying between Charadros (modern Yakacık in Turkey) and Anamourion (Eski Anamur).
2141,648755,Regma/Rogmoi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: outlet of R. Cydnus, near Aliağa Regma/Rogmoi (also Rhegma) is an ancient lake or lagoon formed by the Cydnus river in Cilicia, approximately 5 km from Tarsus."
2142,648789,Tarsus/Antiochia ad Cydnum,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tarsos An important city and river port on the Cydnus river located in Cilicia Pedias.
2143,648801,Tyana/Col. Aurelia Antoniniana,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kemerhisar An ancient city of Cappadocia that was the capital of the Neo-Hittite kingdom in the first millennium B.C.
2144,652350,Kundu/Kyinda,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Macedonian fortress above Anchiale. Two locations have been tentatively suggested for Kundu (classical Kyinda): (1) modern Anavarza , ca. twenty km southeast of Kozan (= Sissû), and (2) modern Tirmil Tepe, ca. two km northeast of Mersin . The former seems more plausible given that Kundu and Sissû mentioned together in Esarhaddon's inscriptions, although this cannot be proven with certainty. Note that Anavarza is generally associated with classical Anazarbos/Caesarea/Ioustin(ian)oupolis . Kundu is not in the vicinity of Sidon , as suggested by W. Mayer in 1995. Kundu is mentioned in Akkadian royal inscriptions of the seventh-century-BC Assyrian king Esarhaddon. In Classical times, Strabo describes Kyinda as a fortress where Macedonian kings deposited treasure. Eumenes reportedly captured the treasure when he revolted against Antigonus. The site is now unlocated."
2145,652357,Melania,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Arsinoe and Celenderis, probably identical with Mandane or Myous An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 66 unlocated Melania"
2146,658374,Amanikai Pylai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Aslan Boğazı A mountain pass that is located on the border between Osmaniye and Gaziantep provinces in south-central Turkey. The pass played an important role in the Battle of Issus, 333 B.C."
2147,658381,Antiochia/Theoupolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Antakya A city founded ca. 300 BC by Seleucus I Nicator, a successor of Alexander the Great. Antioch was a great trading center and numbered as one of the four cities of the Syrian tetrapolis. Justinian I renamed the city 'Theoupolis' in the sixth century AD."
2148,658389,Ariseria/Ad Serta,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 67 F3 Ariseria/Ad Serta"
2149,658390,Arkeuthos/Iaphtha (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river The Arkeuthos/Iaphtha was a small river in the plain of Antioch and a tributary of the Orontes.
2150,658409,Beroia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Alep SYR Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1986 under criteria iii and iv of UNESCO's World Heritage List Operational Guidelines . Specific archaeological and cultural components highlighted in the World Heritage List entry include: Remains of Hittite, Hellenistic, Roman, Byzantine and Ayyubid structures and elements are incorporated in the massive surviving Citadel . The diverse mixture of buildings including the Great Mosque founded under the Umayyads and rebuilt in the 12th century; the 12th century Madrasa Halawiye , which incorporates remains of Aleppo's Christian cathedral, together with other mosques and madrasas, suqs and khans represents an exceptional reflection of the social, cultural and economic aspects of what was once one of the richest cities of all humanity. Aleppo is an outstanding example of an Ayyubid 12th century city with its military fortifications constructed as its focal point following the success of Salah El-Din against the Crusaders. The encircling ditch and defensive wall above a massive, sloping, stone-faced glacis, and the great gateway with its machicolations comprise a major ensemble of military architecture at the height of Arab dominance. Works of the 13th-14th centuries including the great towers and the stone entry bridge reinforce the architectural quality of this ensemble. Surrounding the citadel within the city are numerous mosques from the same period including the Madrasah al Firdows , constructed by Daifa Khatoun in 1235. The ancient city of Aleppo, located in modern Syria, where extensive elements of Hittite, Hellenistic, Roman, Byzantine, Ayyubid, and Umayyad architecture are incorporated into the urban fabric of the modern city. Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1986."
2151,658412,Birtha,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Birecik An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 67 F2 Birtha"
2152,658437,Chordiraza?/Baisampse?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Qalaat Nadjm, on E bank of Euphrates; Sarrin SYR An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 67 G3 Chordiraza?/Baisampse?"
2153,658445,Cyrrestice,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cyrrus region A region of ancient Syria that was situated between the plain of Antioch and the Euphrates and was bounded to the west by Amanus and Commagene.
2154,658450,Daphne,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Harbiye Described by Strabo as a village (katoikia) about 40 stadia (~6 km) outside Antioch, Daphne today is known as Harbiye, in Turkey's Hatay province. In ancient times, it was home to a sacred grove and sanctuary of Apollo and Artemis, but today there are no visible monumental structures of ancient date."
2155,658457,Edessa/Antiochia ad Callirhoem,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Şanlıurfa, formerly Urfa Edessa/Antiochia ad Callirhoem was an ancient settlement of upper Mesopotamia, refounded by Seleucus I Nikator."
2156,658465,Carchemish,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jerablous/Cerablus (Carchemish) SYR/TKY An ancient city that was part of the Mitanni, Hittite, and Neo-Assyrian Empires (Kargamiš in Hittite; commonly Carchemish). The site was occupied as early as the Neolithic and Chalcolithic periods; evidence for Early Bronze Age cist tombs begins ca. 2400 BC. It was a key commercial center during the time of Ebla and Mari. The Mitanni controlled the site from the sixteenth to the fourteenth centuries and was conquered by Šuppiluliuma ca. 1350 B.C. A famous battle, attested in the Bible, was fought at the site in 605 BC between the Babylonian army under Nebuchadnezzar II and that of the Egyptian Pharaoh Necho II."
2157,658475,Gindaros,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jenderes SYR An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 67 D4 Gindaros"
2158,658480,Hierapolis/Bambyke,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Membidj SYR Hierapolis/Bambyke was an ancient settlement of Commagene. The city was also a center for the worship of the Syrian goddess Atargatis.
2159,658481,Hierapolis/Kastabala,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bodrum Kalesi A city of Cilicia with Hittite origins, Hierapolis/Kastabala later became part of the Cilician satrapy of the Achaemenid Empire and, later, part of the Roman empire."
2160,658483,Hydatos Potamoi/Seleukeia Pieria,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kapısuyu/Kaboussié The capital city of Seleucus I Nicator, established ca. 300 BCE."
2161,658490,Issus/Nikopolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yeşil Hüyük, formerly Kınet Höyüğü An ancient port city of Cilicia at the apex of the gulf that bore its name (Issicus Sinus)."
2162,658503,Karmalas? (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: probably Zamantı Su An ancient river, probably to be identified with the modern Zamantı in Turkey's Adana province."
2163,658504,Karsos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 67 C3 Karsos fl."
2164,658515,Labotas (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river The Labotas was a river of the plain of Antioch and a tributary of the Orontes.
2165,658538,Mopsou(h)estia/Seleukeia pros to Pyramo,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yakapınar, formerly Misis Mopsou(h)estia/Seleukeia pros to Pyramo was an ancient settlement in Cilicia Pedias (now modern Yakapınar, Turkey)."
2166,658541,Myriand(r)os,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ada Tepe Myriand(r)os was an ancient Phoenician port near modern İskenderun, Turkey."
2167,658560,Orontes (river),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nahr el-Asi SYR / TKY The Orontes River is one of the chief rivers of the Levant.
2168,658564,Pagrae,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bağras An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 67 C4 Pagrae"
2169,658576,Pyramos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river The modern Ceyhan River in southern Turkey. Its modern mouth is well to the east of its ancient location, which was to the west of Magarsa and much inland from the modern coast."
2170,658585,Rhosos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Uluçınar, formerly Arsuz An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 67 B4 Rhosos"
2171,658587,Samosata,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Samsat An ancient city on the Euphrates River's West bank, established perhaps as early as the Hittite period. Its ruins, known in modern times as Samsat (Turkey), were submerged following the construction of the Atatürk Dam in the early 1990s. The modern village of Samsat was relocated and is not coincident with the ancient site."
2172,658589,Seleukeia epi tou Zeugmatos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Killik? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 67 G2 Seleukeia epi tou Zeugmatos"
2173,658608,Syriai Pylai,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Belen Geçidi Syriai Pylai (Belen Pass) is a narrow pass through the Nur Mountains. This may be the location at which Alexander the Great pursued Achaemenid King Darius III after the Battle of Issus.
2174,658636,Zeugma,"Excavations at the site of Zeugma are chronicled on the official website of the Zeugma Archaeological Project . This site is the same as the one addressed by the following web sites: David Kennedy, "" Zeugma on the Euphrates "" Classics and Ancient HIstory: The University of Western Australia. An ancient settlement whose site is located in the Gaziantep province of modern Turkey, at the modern village of Belkis (a.k.a. Kavunlu). The majority of the ancient remains were covered by water in the year 2000, due to the construction of the Birecik Dam."
2175,662184,Marcoupolis Thera/Gausitha/Charax Sidou,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: distinct from Batnae An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 67 unlocated Marcoupolis Thera/Gausitha/Charax Sidou"
