2184,668290,Laodicea,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lattaquié Laodicea or Laodicea ad Mare (modern Lattakia, Syria) is an ancient city with significant Punic, Greek, and Roman phases."
2185,668291,Laodicea ad Libanum,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell Nebi Mend Hellenistic Laodicea ad Libanum is located at Tell Nebi Mend, the site of ancient Kadesh. Laodicea ad Libanum was a Hellenistic city located on the Orontes River in Coele-Syria."
2186,668292,Larissa/Sizara,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Shaizar An ancient settlement located on the Orontes river to the southeast of Apamea. Scholars identify it with modern Shaizar in Syria.
2187,668318,Nicephorium/Callinicum/Leontopolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Raqqa Nicephorium (modern ar-Raqqa in Syria) was an ancient settlement on the river Euphrates in Syria. It may be identical with Callinicum, which was renamed Leontopolis in the mid-fifth century CE."
2188,668325,Orthosia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Khan ard Artuşi LEB An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 68 A4 Orthosia"
2189,668331,Palmyra,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tudmur Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1980 under criteria i, ii, and iv of UNESCO's World Heritage List Operational Guidelines . Specific archaeological components include: Art and architecture which in the 1st and 2nd centuries AD combined cultural influences from Graeco-Roman , local , and Persian traditions A 1100 meter-long grand colonnaded street Temple of Ba'al, Camp of Diocletian, an ancient Agora, and theatre Various other temples and urban quarters Unique examples of funerary sculpture which combine Graeco-Roman art with indigenous elements and Persian influence Remains of a Roman aqueduct and immense necropoleis Palmyra is an ancient oasis and trading city with monumental remains located in modern Syria. Under Zenobia, It was the capital of the breakaway Palmyrene Empire, which led to Aurelian razing the city. In 1980, Palmyra was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site."
2190,668334,Parapotamia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: district E R. Euphrates An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 68 G2 Parapotamia"
2191,668335,Pella/Apamea,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Qalaat el-Moudiq The archaeological site has been devastated by systematic looting. Pella/Apamea in Syria was an ancient city established by the Seleucids ca. 300 B.C.
