2204,678106,Damascus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Dimashq/Damascus Ghuta SYR Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1979 under criteria i, ii, iii, iv, and vi of UNESCO's World Heritage List Operational Guidelines . Specific archaeological components include: Some 125 monuments from the Hellenistic , Roman , Byzantine , and Islamic periods The Great Mosque of the Umayyads , built in 8th century AD on the site of an Assyrian sanctuary The Old City of Damascus , which is considered to be among the oldest continually inhabited cities in the world The city itself is based on a Roman plan and maintains the aspect and orientation of the Greek city Remains of the Roman Temple of Jupiter The ancient city of Damascus, located in modern Syria, with some 125 monuments dating back to the Roman period. Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1979."
2205,678145,Galilaea,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Galilee ISR Galilaea was the northernmost region of Palaestina. Galilaea became a Seleucid territory after the conquests of Alexander the Great.
2206,678158,Gerasa/Antiochia ad Chrysorhoam,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jerash JOR Gerasa (modern Jerash in Jordan) became a substantial center under Antiochos IV Epiphanes, at which time it became known as Antioch on the Chrysorhoas. It became part of the territory of Iudaea and in 63 B.C. Pompeius Magnus made it part of the Decapolis. It received the status of a Roman colony in the third century."
2207,678201,Iotapata,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Khirbet Shifat ISR A Hellenistic village that later became a key site during the Great Jewish Revolt.
2208,678215,Kadasa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tel Qedesh ISR A multi-period settlement, with Bronze Age to Roman occupation, most notably the site of a large, Persian and Hellenistic administrative building."
2209,678253,Kiboreia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Deir el-Ashayr LEB An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 69 D2 Kiboreia"
2210,678255,Klimax Tyrion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ras en-Naqura LEB An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 69 B3 Klimax Tyrion"
2211,678267,Libanus M.,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jebel Lubnan LEB A mountain range of northern Syria that is between the Mediterranean coastline and the Antilibanos mountains of the interior.
2212,678269,Lykos (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nahr el-Kelb LEB The ancient Lykos river (modern Nahr al-Kalb) flows from the Libanus mountains to the Mediterranean, passing ca. 10 km north of Beirut."
2213,678322,Palaibyblos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S Adonis fl. LEB An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 69 C2 Palaibyblos"
2214,678324,Paneas/Caesarea Philippi,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Banias SYR An ancient settlement at the southwest foot of Mount Hermon, located in the Israeli-occupied Golan Heights, just across the Lebanese border."
2215,678326,Pella/Berenice/Philippeia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tabaqat Fahl JOR Pella/Berenice/Philippeia (Tabaqat Fahl (طبقة فحل)) is a ruined ancient city located in northwestern Jordan. Pella was continuously occupied from the Neolithic period until its destruction by means of an earthquake in AD 749.
2216,678334,Phoenice (region),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: LEB Ancient Phoenicia.
2217,678370,Samaria/Sebaste,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sebastiya WBK. Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage site in July 2026. Samaria/Sebaste was an ancient city of Iudaea. In 30 B.C., it passed under the control of Herod the Great and was renamed Sebaste in honor of the emperor Augustus."
2218,678387,Sepphoris/Diocaesarea,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Zippori/Saffuriye ISR An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 69 B4 Sepphoris/Diocaesarea"
2219,678401,Stratonos Pyrgos/Caesarea,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Qesaria/Qaisariye ISR An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 69 A4 Stratonos Pyrgos/Caesarea"
2220,678403,Sychem,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Balata WBK An ancient settlement in the Levant, attested Biblically and in ancient Egyptian documents and the Amarna letters. Scholars associate this settlement with the archaeological site of Tel Balata, which is located in the modern West Bank and has seen attention from archaeologists since the early 20th century."
2221,678430,Tiberiadis Mare/Gennesar Lacus,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sea of Galilee The Sea of Galilee, also known as the Sea of Tiberias, is the largest freshwater lake in Israel."
2222,678431,Tiberias,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tiberias ISR Tiberias was founded by Herod Antipas ca. 20 BC.
2223,678435,Trachones,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lejja and Safa area SYR Trachones, hills located near Damascus mentioned in the text of Strabo."
2224,678437,Tyrus/Col. Septimia Severa,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: es-Sur LEB The ancient city of Tyre (modern es-Sur on the coast of Lebanon). A UNESCO World Heritage Site, in part because of its ""important archaeological remains, mainly from Roman times."""
2225,687838,Ashdod/Azotos (Mesogeios),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tel Ashdod An ancient settlement of the Philistines.
2226,687839,Ashqelon/Ascalon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ashkelon Ashqelon/Ascalon was an ancient city in coastal Palestine, located midway between Azotus and Gaza. It became independent in 104 B.C. and remained the only free city in Palestine. Strabo refers to it, while Pliny calls it a free city (oppidum libera)."
2227,687843,Azotos Paralios,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ashdod Yam/Minet el Qal'a Azotos Paralios, also referred to as Ashdod-Yam, is a multi-period archaeological site located on the Mediterranean coast of Israel. It dates back to the time of the Assyrian Empire. The toponym ""Azotos Paralios"" belongs to its Hellenistic phase."
2228,687854,Beth Govrin/Eleutheropolis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bet Jibrin Govrin/Eleutheropolis was a Roman city founded on the site of Betaris.
2229,687902,Gaza,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: el Ghazze/Gaza GAZ An important ancient settlement on the coast of Palestine.
2230,687915,Hebron,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: el Khalil WBK An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 70 G2 Hebron"
2231,687916,Herodeion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: el Fureidis WBK Herodeion or Herodium refers to an archaeological site located in what is now the West Bank, some 12 km south of Jerusalem and 5 km southeast of Bethlehem. In antiquity, Herod the Great established the fortress after defeating his rival Antigonus II Mattathias in 40 BCE."
2232,687917,Jericho,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jericho WBK. Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in September 2023. An ancient site in the Jordan valley with occupation beginning ca. 11,000 years BP. The tell of Hierichous (Jericho) has more than 20 settlement horizons, ranging from Natufian Hunter-Gatherers to modern."
2233,687925,Iamneia,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Yibna Iamneia was a Philistine town on the northwestern border of Iudaea.
2234,687928,Ierusalem/Hierosolyma/Col. Aelia Capitolina,The Barrington Atlas notes: Jerusalem. The city of Jerusalem.
2235,687931,Ioppe,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Jaffa Ioppe (Jaffa) is an ancient port city in Iudaea.
2236,687934,Iudaea (region),"Iudaea was an historical region of the Levant located in the mountainous southern part of the Land of Palestine, roughly corresponding to the southern West Bank. The region's name derives from the biblical tribe of Judah and the associated Kingdom of Judah (ca. 934 until 586 BC)."
2237,687953,Lydda/Diospolis/Georgioupolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Lud An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 70 F2 Lydda/Diospolis/Georgioupolis"
2238,687968,Masada,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Es Sebbe Masada is a natural fortress in the Iudaean desert that overlooks the Dead Sea. It was the site of the standoff between Jewish rebels and the Roman army in A.D. 73. Herod the Great built a palace atop the citadel during his reign. Roman camps and siege works are still visible at the site, which is listed on the UNESCO World Heritage List."
2239,687996,Pegai/Antipatris/Arethousa?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ras el-Ain/Rosh Ha-Ayin A series of settlements and fortifications localized in and around the mound known today as Tel Afek/Râs el-'Ain in Israel. The mound itself is dominated today by the remains of an Ottoman fort, built on Crusader foundations, but an excavated Roman-era odeon can also be seen."
2240,688015,Raphia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell es Sheikh Suleiman An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 70 E3 Raphia"
2241,688016,Rhinocolura,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: el Arish EGY An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 70 D3 Rhinocolura"
2242,688025,Serbonitis (lake),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sabkhat el Bardawil EGY A large, saline lake in Egypt on the north coast of the Sinai Peninsula; the ""Serbonian Bog"" described by Herodotus."
2243,688030,Sobata,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Shivta/Isbeita Sobata or Shivta is an ancient city in the Negev Desert located some 43 km southwest of Beersheba. The site was enrolled as a UNESCO World Heritage site, together with Haluza, Mamshit, and Avdat, in June 2005."
2244,688033,Socho,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Horvat Sokho/Khirbet Shuweika An Iron Age site in the Valley of Elah identified as the Biblical town of Socho.
2245,688036,Sykomazon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Khirbet Suq Mazen? GAZ An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 70 E3 Sykomazon"
2246,697634,Arnon (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Wadi Mujib An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 71 B3 Arnon fl."
2247,697665,Gadara,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell Jadur Gadara (Tell Jadur) is located northeast of Jericho.
2248,697709,Mortuum Mare/Asphaltitis Limne,"Dead Sea The modern ""Dead Sea"", which is bordered by Israel, Jordan, and the West Bank."
2249,697728,Amman/Philadelpheia,"An ancient settlement in northwest Jordan (modern Amman) received a Macedonian settlement and the name of Philadelphia from Ptolemy II Philadelphos (285-246 BC). The modern Amman, Jordan."
2250,699913,El-‘Iraq,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: E Dead Sea At modern El-‘Iraq in Jordan's Karak region, archaeologists have identified Byzantine pottery, architecture (including a church), and inscriptions. They argue, based on the archaeology and on the relative placement of other contemporary settlements on the Madaba map, that this site should be identified as ancient Tharais."
2251,707484,Arsinoe?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Geroskipou-Litharka/Vounimenos An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 72 A3 Arsinoe?"
2252,707503,Drepanon (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cape Drepanon An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 72 A3 Drepanon Pr."
2253,707507,Elaia (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Cape Elaia CYX Ptolemy places this cape on the east coast of the island of Cyprus, enumerating it between the ancient city of Salamis and the Kleides promontory at the terminus of the island's distinctive northeast peninsula."
2254,707527,Karpasiai (islands),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: island-group An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 72 E1 Karpasiai Inss."
2255,707536,Kleides (islands),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: island-group An archipelago extending from the northeast tip of the Karpas Peninsula on the island of Cyprus.
2256,707548,Kourias (promontory),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Akrotiri Pr. Mentioned in ancient sources, the Kourias promontory on Cyprus' southern coast probably corresponds to the northern, higher elevation portion of the modern Akrotiri peninsula before a thin stretch of ocean separating it from an island in antiquity filled in, creating the modern Limassol Salt Lake."
