2269,716656,Zygris (Marsa Baqqush),"The BAtlDir entries for the two ""Zygris"" labels on Map 73 have exchanged gridsquare numbers. The ""Zygris"" labeled in 72 E2 with a hollow ""station"" (diamond) symbol corrresponds to the directory entry given the grid square D2 and noting ""Marsa Baqqush"" as the Modern Name / Location. An ancient anchorage on the Mediterranean coast of modern Egypt mentioned by Ptolemy (Zygis limen) and in the Stadiasmus Maris Magni. This Zygris (not to be confused with another also mentioned by Ptolemy) has been identified since the early 20th century with a small, reef-walled bay whose name, Marsa Baqqush, has since come to denote the larger bay of which the Zygris anchorage is a part. Multiple ancient wrecks have been identified within and just outside this anchorage, and an extensive system of interconnected rock-cut cisterns, elliptically mentioned in the Stadiasmus, has been identified ashore."
2270,716657,Zygris (Zawiet el-Chammas?),"The BAtlDir entries for the two ""Zygris"" labels on Map 73 have exchanged gridsquare numbers. The ""Zygris"" labeled in 72 D2 with a solid ""station"" (diamond) symbol corresponds to the directory entry given the grid square E2 and noting ""Zawiet el-Chammas?"" as the Modern Name / Location. An ancient town of the Zygritai on the ancient Libyan coast. Ptolemy provides the name and coordinates; BAtlDir tentatively associates it with modern Zawiet el-Chammas in Egypt's Matruh governorate."
2271,716658,Zygritai,"The New Pauly reference has been removed from this place resource. It cites Desanges and RE, but renders itself incorrect and confusing in both English and German by inverting the descriptive directional relationships with other peoples and communities. The Zygritai were a Libyan people mentioned by Ptolemy and thought to have been localized in the vicinity of the ancient town of Zygris, for which Ptolemy separately provides coordinates. Desanges (whence RE) puts them in the vicinity of modern Sidi Barrani in Egypt's Matruh governorate. BAtlas writes the label just east of Sidi Barrani, between it and Marsa Matruh."
2272,727073,Anthylla,Anthylla was an ancient settlement located on the Canobic branch of the Nile.
2273,727076,Archandropolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell Louquin? An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 74 C2 Archandropolis"
2274,727078,Athribis (Lower Egypt),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell Atrib An ancient city of Lower Egypt located at present-day Tell Atrib to the northeast of Benha on the hill of Kom Sidi Yusuf.
2275,727093,Boutikos potamos,"The Barrington Atlas directory postfixed the string ""fl."" (abbreviation for ""flumen"") to the label ""Boutikos"". This is normal practice for a natural river in BAtlas, but the map symbology clearly indicates that the feature is a canal (a classification confirmed by Trismegistos). The Greek ""potamos"" is part of the attested name as found in the papyri, and this may be the origin for the BAtlas directory's otherwise unaccountable use of the label (which has been removed here in all but the BAtlas citation itself). An ancient canal of the Egyptian delta, with its western end on the Canopic branch of the Nile at Hierakonpolis and its eastern end at Pelusium (modern Tell el-Farama), deviating from an east-west line between the two at Bouto (modern Kom el-Farain)."
2276,727111,Diospolis Kato,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell el-Balamun An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 74 F2 Diospolis Kato"
2277,727120,Herakleion,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Abukir An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 74 C2 Herakleion"
2278,727122,Hermopolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Baklia An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 74 E3 Hermopolis"
2279,727123,Hermopolis Mikra,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Damanhur Hermopolis Mikra was an ancient city of Egypt that served as he capital of Lower Egypt's 7th Nome (A-ment).
2280,727152,Tell el-Heir,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell el-Heir? An ancient site in the Sinai that was the location of a series of successive fortresses from the Persian through the Roman periods. It may be identifiable with the Greek name Magdolos and/or the Hebrew name Migdol. There is also some speculation that much earlier textual evidence for a fortification of Ramses III should be tied to this site as well.
2281,727156,Mendes,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 74 F3 Mendes"
2282,727157,Mendesion Stoma,The third eastern-most ancient mouth of the Nile River.
2283,727172,Nilus (river),"The Nile is a major river of northeastern Africa and is generally acknowledged as the longest river in the world, measuring some 6,650 km (4,130 miles) from source to mouth."
2284,727193,Perseos Skope,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bolbitinos Stoma An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 74 C2 Perseos Skope"
2285,727194,Phakoussa,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Fakus The modern city of Faqous in the southeastern portion of the Nile delta is thought to have been built atop an ancient city known to the Greeks as Phakoussa. Strabo identifies it as the terminus of an ancient canal connecting to the Red Sea.
2286,727203,Pikrai Limnai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Great Bitter Lakes The ""Bitter Lakes"" are hypersaline lacustrine bodies in the Nile River valley of Egypt. Prior to the construction of the Suez Canal, these lakes constituted a dry salt valley or basin. They are referred to by ancient Greek and Latin authors as well as in the so-called ""Pyramid Texts""."
2287,727205,Plinthine,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kom el-Nagous? An ancient settlement at modern Kom el-Nagous in Egypt. Archaeological evidence shows habitation on the site stretches back to at least the Ramesside period. A large Ptolemaic temple seems to have been dismantled and built over during the Roman period.
2288,727217,Sais,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sa el-Hagar Sais was an ancient Egyptian town located in the Western Nile Delta on the Canopic branch of the Nile River. It served as the provincial capital of Sap-Meh, the fifth nome of Lower Egypt, and became a capital during the Twenty-fourth Dynasty (ca. 732–720 BC) as well as during the Late Period. Herodotus claimed that Osiris was buried at Sais. Other Greek authors, including Plato, maintained a connection between Sais and the goddess Athena."
2289,727230,Sethroites Nomos,The Sethroite nome was the 14th nome of Lower Egypt.
2290,727232,Sketis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Wadi Natroun The modern Wadi el Natrun: a valley in northern Egypt that developed as an important locus of Coptic monastic establishments, beginning in late antiquity and continuing to the present day."
2291,727236,Tanis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: San el-Hagar An important settlement in the eastern Nile delta (modern San el-Hagar), Tanis boasted a massive temple precinct and was the burial site for Egyptian kings of the 21st and 22nd dynasties."
2292,727239,Tanitikon/Saitikon Stoma,The second eastern-most ancient mouth of the Nile river.
2293,727249,Thmouis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell Timai el-Amdid An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 74 F3 Thmouis"
2294,727250,Thonis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Abukir The Egyptian site of Thonis, sometimes referred to as Thonis-Heracleion, was located near the Canopic Mouth of the Nile, approximately 32 km northeast of Alexandria. Its ruins are now submerged."
2295,727252,Troia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tura An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 74 E4 Troia"
2296,727256,Xois,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Sakha Ancient Xois was located on an island that was formed by the Sebennytic and Phatnitic branches of the Nile. It became the capital of its own nome, the Xoite nome."
2297,728577,Nekou Diorux,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Wadi Tumilat. The compilers of the Barrington Atlas have not provided much clarity on their choice of toponymy concerning this place and its close, cognate relations. Wadi Tumilat is to be taken as a salt basin and a former distributary of the Nile River. Nekou Diorux can be understood to be the Pharaonic through early Roman canal that is subsequently superseded by the Ptolemaios/Traianos Potamos, in turn superseded by the Arabic canal known as In terms of the ancient literary sources, Redmount 1995 provided an invaluable summary of the textual claims. The Trismegistos data set has conflated Nekou Diorux and Ptolemaios/Traianos Potamos as a single place. Nekou Diorux, an ancient canal in the Wadi Tumilat of Egypt that connected the Pelusiac branch of the Nile River with the Red Sea. Ancient authors, including Aristotle, Strabo, and Pliny the Elder, describe a person-made canal in the Nile River valley that has come to be known colloquially as the ""Canal of the Pharaohs"". According to Pliny, this project began under the 12th Dynasty pharaoh Senusret III (1878–1839 BC). Work on the canal continued under Darius I of Persia, and then yet again under the Ptolemies. Darius I famously erected a series of inscribed monuments marking and commemorating the canal. Pliny continues to state that following this last phase, the canal extended as far as Fontes Amari and was ""100 feet wide, 30 deep, and 37 miles long""."
