2305,736920,Herakleopolis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ihnasya el-Medina Herakleopolis (also called Herakleopolis Magna) was the capital of the Twentieth nome of Egypt.
2306,736932,Karanis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kom Awshim Karanis was a settlement (κώμη) located on the northern edge of the Fayum, now Kom Ausīm. Ptolemy II Philadelphus established Karanias in order to settle Greek mercenaries among indigenous Egyptians. This foundation, and others, also aimed to exploit the agricultural fertility of the Fayum depression."
2307,736936,Kerkeosiris,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: el-Gharaq An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 75 D2 Kerkeosiris"
2308,736948,Krokodilopolis/Ptolemais Euergetis,"Its local name was Shedet [Cohen]. An ancient city in the Heptanomis and the capital of the Arsinoites nome. The ancient Egyptian name of the site was Shedyet, and in Pharaonic times it was a major cult center for the god Sobek."
2309,736952,Labyrinthos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Hawwara Labyrinthos is a name applied by Greek authors, notably Herodotus and Strabo, to describe a monumental funerary temple at Hawara."
2310,736954,Libykon Oros,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Libyan desert Mountains that border the Nile river valley to the west.
2311,736963,Memphis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mit Rahina A city and capital of Ancient Egypt that remained an important center for most of Egyptian history.
2312,736967,Moeris (lake),The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Birket Qarun Moeris Lacus (Lake Moeris) was an ancient lake in the northwestern part of the Fayuum Oasis in Egypt; its surviving modern remnant is Birket Qarun.
