2330,759654,Tanis/Tenis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between Lykopolis and Hermopolis Magna An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 77 unlocated Tanis/Tenis"
2331,766366,Ghuzza,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Ghuzza, fort with settlement and mine A multi-period complex in Egypt's eastern desert comprising a fort, settlement(s?), and production areas for gold-washing of quartz collected from the wadi alluvium. Most visible structures date to the Ptolemaic and Roman periods, but there is evidence for earlier activity during the New Kingdom period."
2332,776172,Esotero (Oasis),The modern Dakhla (Dakhleh) Oasis in Egypt's western desert.
2333,776196,Manawir el-Biyut,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Manawir el-Biyut Remains of a ruined village with mud brick structures, qanat, and an associated necropolis of Roman date all located 3-5 km west of ancient Dush (Egypt)."
2334,778439,Pmoun Paleou,"An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 79 unlocated Pmoun Paleou"
2335,785957,Abaton,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Bigah Is. An ancient site designated as the burial place of Osiris-Serapis.
2336,785968,Akathartos Kolpos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bai Umm el-Ketef Akathartos Kolpos, an embayment of the Red Sea just north of the Tropic of Cancer."
2337,785974,Apollonopolis Magna,"The city of Apollonopolis Magna was located on the west bank of the Nile, south of Thebes. As the seat of Horus, Greeks regarded it as a site connected with Apollo. It served as the capital of the second nome of Upper Egypt."
2338,786020,Eileithyiaspolis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: el-Kab Eileithyiaspolis is a city of Upper Egypt on the eastern bank of the Nile river. The site was occupied from the Neolithic period onwards. Its ancient name was most likely Nekheb.
2339,786036,Hermonthis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Armant Modern Armant, an important town of the Egyptian Middle Kingdom that was enlarged during the Eighteenth Dynasty and continued to thrive through the Ptolemaic, Roman, and Coptic periods."
2340,786037,Nekhen/Hierakonpolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kom el-Ahmar The ritual and political capital of Upper Egypt at the end of the Predynastic period and the Early Dynastic Period, with occupation beginning as early as Naqada I. The site was an important ritual center for the worship of Horus."
2341,786059,Latopolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Esna/Isna An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 80 B3 Latopolis"
2342,786069,Myos Hormos?,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Quseir el-Qadim Myos Hormos was a port on the Red Sea built by the Ptolemies in the third century BC.
2343,786079,Omboi,"The names ""Ambo"" and ""Ombi"" are also presented in the following, though a primary-source reference has not yet been found for either name: Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography , ed. William Smith, 1854, sub voce The Ptolemaic temple to Sobek and Horus the Elder at Kom Ombo."
2344,786123,Syene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: settlement and quarry cluster at Aswan The ancient city of Swenett in Egypt, called Syene by the Greeks."
2345,786127,Tentyra,"The Egyptian name Iunet has been attested in the following source, though a primary-source reference for the name has not yet been found: Geissen, Angelo and Manfred Weber, ""Untersuchungen zu den ägyptischen Nomenprägungen II: 1.-7. Oberägyptischer Gau"", Zeitschrift für Papyrologie und Epigraphik , Bd. 147, 2004: pp. 275 The Egyptian name Tantere is commonly used and referred to in secondary-source texts, but a primary-source reference has not been found for either. The name Tentyra can be considered an alternate spelling of Tantura, the primary name used in Greek sources. The Ptolemaic temple to Hathor at Dendera."
2346,786132,Touphion,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tod Touphion is located on the east bank of the Nile River about 20 km south of Thebes. The ruins of a multi-period temple initially built by Sesostris I were found there.
2347,795790,Blemmyes,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: EGY/SUD The Blemmyes were a nomadic people of Lower Nubia. Strabo places them on the right bank of the Nile River.
2348,795808,Forum Cambusis?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Abu Simbel An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 81 B3 Forum Cambusis?"
