2477,874294,Bingöl Dağları (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Bingöl Dağ The modern Bingöl Dağları is an extinct shield volcano located to the northeast of modern Bingöl in Turkey, lying between the modern settlements of Karlıova, Tekman, Hınıs, and Varto. The mountain was known in Roman times by the name ""Abos""."
2478,874310,Akilisene (region),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: districts of Kemah and Eriza in upper R. Euphrates valley, and of Ovacık to S Kemah An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 89 A1 Akilisene"
2479,874341,Arbela/Arba-ilu,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Erbil IRQ An ancient Mesopotamian city located between the two Zab rivers (modern Erbel/Erbil in Iraq).
2480,874354,Arsamosata,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Haraba A city in Armenia, probably founded by Arsames in the early second century BCE, perhaps at the site of modern Haraba (now under the Keban Dam Lake)"
2481,874361,Artaxata,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: peninsula in R. Aras, near Aralik ARM An ancient city located on and around the hills known today as Khor Virap. This location is near the Armenian village of Lusarat, which is about 8 km south of modern Artashat."
2482,874362,Edremit,"One of the cuneiform inscriptions is on a monument known as ""Hazine Piri Kapısı"". Edremit is a modern settlement and associated administrative region in the Van Province in the southeast of Turkey. In the vicinity, several inscriptions on rock and stone of the Urartian king Minua (9th / 8th century BCE) have been found which report the construction of the 'Canal of Minua'. In Greek and Roman times, Ptolemy gives the name ""Artemita"" for a settlement that modern scholars believe was located at Edremit."
2483,874444,Dara/Anastasioupolis,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Oğuz, formerly Dara Dara/Anastasioupolis was a fortified Roman city of northern Mesopotamia located on the border with the Sassanid Empire."
2484,874593,Matiane/*Kapauta Limne,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: L. Urmia IRN A salt lake located in what is now Iran, Lake Urmia was, at its greatest extent, the sixth-largest saltwater lake on Earth. It once covered an area of approximately 5,200 square kilometers (2,000 sq. miles) and measured 140 km (87 mi) by 55 km (34 mi). The lake continues to shrink in area due to persistent drought."
2485,874597,Media Atropatene,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: between L. Urmia and R. Aras An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 89 H2 Media Atropatene"
2486,874609,Mosul/‘Mespila’?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Mosul, IRQ An ancient city located across the Tigris River from the site of Nineveh. Modern Mosul in Iraq."
2487,874621,Nineveh/Ninos,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kuyunjik IRQ According to the Old Testament book of Jonah, Nineveh was home to 120,000 people and took three days to cross. Islamic Tradition has it that the mound of Nebi Yunus (""Prophet Jonah""), the spot where an Assyrian armory was built, contains the grave of the prophet Jonah, who is believed to have died in Nineveh; it is the site of an important mosque. This important Mesopotamian city flanks the eastern edge of the Tigris flood plain, opposite modern Mosul, of which it is now a suburb. From the third millennium B.C. onwards, Nineveh was the most important religious center of the goddess Ištar in the area that would become the Assyrian heartland. Starting in the Middle Assyrian period, the city came under the authority of the kings of Assyria, who often sponsored large-scale building activities there. However, it was not until 704 B.C. that Nineveh became the administrative capital of Assyria when the Neo-Assyrian king Sennacherib moved the royal family and court there and transformed the city into a thriving imperial metropolis. Nineveh remained Assyria’s capital until 612 B.C., when it was captured and destroyed by a Babylonian-Median coalition led by Nabopolassar and Cyaxares. The visible remains of the (7th-century) Assyrian city include the citadel mound Kuyunjik, the smaller mound of Nebi Yunus, and the twelve-kilometer-long city wall."
2488,874622,Niphates (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Taurus massif, S Lake Van A mountain range in Armenia, belonging to the eastern Taurus massif."
2489,874623,Nisibis/Antiochia,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Nusaybin SYR/TKY Nisibis/Antiochia was an ancient city of Mesopotamia that began as part of an Aramaean kingdom that the Assyrian king Adad-Nirari II captured in 896 BC. Following its capture by Alexander the Great, the city became Antiochia Mygdonia under the Seleucids."
2490,874662,Resaina/Theodosiopolis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell Fakhariya near Ras al-Ain SYR. In AD 243 Gordian III and Timesitheus prevailed in a battle against King Shapur I and an army of Sassanid Persians. A Roman garrison town located near the Chaboras that was later renamed Theodosiopolis.
2491,874681,Sardeoua/‘Sardebar’,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Kale-i Zerzevan Now known as Zerzevan Kalesi, this Roman-era fortified settlement is located in Turkey's Diyarbakir province."
2492,874772,Thospitis Limne,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: L. Van Lake Van, the largest lake in Asia Minor, is a saline soda lake."
2493,874774,Tigranocerta/Cholimma/Chlomaron,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: medieval city site of Arzan A city in Armenia, founded by Tigranes II as capital of his kingdom in the first half of the 1st century BC."
2494,876596,Bimisdeon,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Resaina SYR/TKY One of several forts in the vicinity of Theodosiopolis whose construction Procopius attributes to the emperor Justinian. Its precise location has not been determined.
2495,876665,Nikatorion (mountain),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Arbela An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 89 unlocated Nikatorion M."
2496,876693,Sadrakai,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: S Arbela An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 89 unlocated Sadrakai"
2497,884130,Caspium/Hyrcanium Mare,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Caspian Sea. The Caspian Sea is a remnant of a larger, inland sea and is now the world's largest enclosed body of water. It has been classified as both a lake and a sea."
2498,893928,Aborras/Chaboras (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Habur SYR/TKY An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 91 B2 Aborras/Chaboras fl."
2499,893935,Adiabene,Adiabene was an ancient kingdom in Assyria situated between the Upper Zab (Lycus) and the Lower Zab (Caprus) rivers. Its capital was at Arbela.
2500,893951,Babylon,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell Amran Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage site on 5 July 2019. The ancient city of Babylon began as an Akkadian city-state founded by Amorites in 1894 B.C. The city was also the seat of the Neo-Babylonian Empire from 608 to 539 B.C.
2501,893976,Ctesiphon,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: al-Ma'aridh An imperial capital of the Arsacid and Sasanian empires, this large ancient city originated as early as the third century BCE and declined in the eighth century CE. It was located on the left bank of the Tigris River 35 km south of what would later become modern Baghdad in Iraq. It was part of a complex network of settlements in the area, among which Seleucia (modern Vēh Ardašīr) also featured prominently. It was attacked, and sometimes occupied, by Roman forces multiple times."
2502,893990,D(o)ura/Europos,The Ancient World Image Bank (a publication of NYU's Institute for the Study of the Ancient World ) provides an open-access collection of images from Doura . The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: near Salihiya SYR An ancient settlement on the Euphrates River in modern Syria.
2503,894020,Karastel/Artemita?,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Karastel A city in Sittakene and/or Babylonia, located on the Silla River."
2504,894056,Opis,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell Mujeili' Adams 1965 no. 590 notes a significant site with surface material dating from Cassite to Neo-Babylonian. The sequence of Early Dynastic to Old Babylonian is also attested. Opis was an ancient city of Babylonia located on the Tigris River close to the site of modern Baghdad.
2505,894084,Seleucia/Sliq Kharawta,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Tell Omar Seleucia/Sliq Kharawta was a city founded on the Tigris River by Seleukos I Nikator.
2506,894178,Veh Ardashir/Coche/Mahoza/‘Seleucia’,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Abu Khshaim A significant Hellenistic city that served as a capital of both the Seleucid and Parthian empires. The city was founded by Seleucus Nicator.
2507,896945,Wall of Media,"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Teichos Medias, at Habl as-Sahr The Wall of Media, also known as Mēdías teîchos or the Wall of Semiramis, at Habl as-Sahr is mentioned by Xenophon in the Anabasis as a defensive ditch at the Euphrates built on the orders of Artaxerxes in the fifth century BC."
2508,897713,Apphadana,"An ancient island mentioned by Ptolemy. BAtlDir chose not to place it on a map. EncIran considers the possibility that the name may be related to ""Abadan"", a name for an island and settlement at the head of the Persian Gulf that first appears in literature in the 9th century CE."
2509,897717,Arderikka,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: in N Babylonia A now unlocated site in Assyria located on the Euphrates River upstream from Babylon.
2510,897733,Basileios (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: river An ancient place, cited: BAtlas 91 unlocated Basileios fl."
2511,897772,Gyndes (river),"The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Diyala? The Gyndes was a river of Assyria and Susiana that is discussed by Herodotus. Its precise location can not be determined with certainty, but some scholars have identified it with the modern Diyala river in Iraq."
2512,897786,Korsote,The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: on Maskas fl. Xenophon describes Korsote as a large city located in the desert to the south of the confluence of the Chaboras (Habur) and the Euphrates rivers. It is now unlocated.
